"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"scope\":\"Declared project registry/navigation relationship; not an identified causal effect.\",\"subject_id\":\"design:synth\"}],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"One treated unit, many untreated donors. Build a weighted combination of donors that tracks the treated unit before the intervention, and use it afterwards.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}" .
<urn:capy:design%3Aundecided> a capy:design ; skos:prefLabel "undecided" ; capy:record "{\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"disposition\":\"unresolved\",\"gaps\":[\"No complete catalogue record exists in this snapshot.\"],\"id\":\"design:undecided\",\"kind\":\"design\",\"raw_id\":\"undecided\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Registry marker or referenced item without a maintained catalogue description.\",\"title\":\"undecided\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aadoption> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Adoption pattern" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:adoption\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.adoption\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"adoption\",\"in_more_depth\":\"This is the picture that decides which difference-in-differences estimator you should be using. A single block of lit cells starting at one date is a 2x2. A staircase is staggered adoption, and two-way fixed effects is then averaging comparisons that include already-treated units used as controls. It also shows what you have to compare against: a row of never-treated units at the bottom, or nothing, in which case the comparison group has to be units not yet treated.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A grid of units by period, lit where treatment is on, sorted by when each unit adopted.\",\"plain_language\":\"A grid of units by period, lit where treatment is on, sorted by when each unit adopted.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.chaisemartin\",\"id\":\"did.chaisemartin\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"An estimator built for panels where treatment switches on and off, which the imputation and cohort estimators assume never happens.\",\"title\":\"de Chaisemartin & D'Haultfoeuille\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.dr_did\",\"id\":\"did.dr_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One before-and-after with covariates, using an outcome model and a comparison-group model together so either one can carry it.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust DiD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.event_study\",\"id\":\"did.event_study\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Plot the effect period by period around adoption, so you can see whether the gap opened when the policy arrived or long before it.\",\"title\":\"Event study (leads and lags)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.gardner_2s\",\"id\":\"did.gardner_2s\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Strip out unit and period effects using untreated cells only, then regress what is left on the policy.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage DiD (Gardner / Wooldridge-Mundlak)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.goodman_bacon\",\"id\":\"did.goodman_bacon\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Open up a two-way fixed effects estimate and show every two-group comparison inside it, with the weight each one carries -- including the ones that use already-treated units as controls.\",\"title\":\"Goodman-Bacon decomposition\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.matched\",\"id\":\"did.matched\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Match treated to comparison units on pre-period characteristics first, then difference.\",\"title\":\"Matched difference-in-differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.sun_abraham\",\"id\":\"did.sun_abraham\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Give every cohort its own event-study path, then average the paths by cohort size instead of letting the regression choose the weights.\",\"title\":\"Sun & Abraham event study\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.triple_diff\",\"id\":\"did.triple_diff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the treated-versus-comparison difference for the group the policy could reach with the same difference for a group inside the same units that it could not.\",\"title\":\"Triple differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twfe\",\"id\":\"did.twfe\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One regression with unit and period fixed effects. Familiar, fast, and not an average treatment effect when units adopt at different dates.\",\"title\":\"Two-way fixed effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twoway_2x2\",\"id\":\"did.twoway_2x2\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Two groups, two periods: compare how much the treated group changed with how much the comparison group changed over the same window.\",\"title\":\"Difference-in-differences (2x2)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twfe\",\"id\":\"did.twfe\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One regression with unit and period fixed effects. Familiar, fast, and not an average treatment effect when units adopt at different dates.\",\"title\":\"Two-way fixed effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Adoption pattern\",\"worry_when\":\"No never-treated units; cohorts containing a single unit; treatment that switches back off, which the staggered estimators assume does not happen.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:adoption\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"adoption\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A grid of units by period, lit where treatment is on, sorted by when each unit adopted.\",\"title\":\"Adoption pattern\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aaft_fit> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "AFT model fit and common time scale" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:aft_fit\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.survival\",\"id\":\"survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Follow people from a common time zero; some leave follow-up before the event is observed.\",\"title\":\"Time until an event\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.aft_fit\",\"gaps\":[\"no hand-written explanation yet, so the app generates one from the record\"],\"id\":\"aft_fit\",\"in_more_depth\":\"No check can prove an assumption true. The strongest honest thing any of them can do is fail to contradict one, so read this next to the claim it speaks to rather than as a verdict. This one is not tied to a single assumption: it describes the data or the fit rather than testing a claim about them.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A check Causal Capybara runs for you and draws on the results screen. It appears when the design is \\\"Time until an event\\\". The card it appears on carries a plain summary of what it found in your data, and a line saying what would worry me about it.\",\"plain_language\":\"A check Causal Capybara runs for you and draws on the results screen. It appears when the design is \\\"Time until an event\\\". The card it appears on carries a plain summary of what it found in your data, and a line saying what would worry me about it.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.aft\",\"id\":\"survival.aft\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a right-censored log-normal/Weibull AFT model; standardize survival/RMST over baseline covariates under explicit causal assumptions, or report a conditional time factor.\",\"title\":\"Censored AFT regression and causal standardization\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.survival\",\"id\":\"survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Follow people from a common time zero; some leave follow-up before the event is observed.\",\"title\":\"Time until an event\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Time until an event\"],\"title\":\"AFT model fit and common time scale\",\"worry_when\":\"The card itself says what would worry me here, because that depends on the numbers this particular run produced. A longer written explanation of this check has not been added to the library yet.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[\"no hand-written explanation yet, so the app generates one from the record\"],\"id\":\"diagnostic:aft_fit\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"aft_fit\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A check Causal Capybara runs for you and draws on the results screen. It appears when the design is \\\"Time until an event\\\". The card it appears on carries a plain summary of what it found in your data, and a line saying what would worry me about it.\",\"title\":\"AFT model fit and common time scale\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aaft_support> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Conditional treatment and censoring support" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:aft_support\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.survival\",\"id\":\"survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Follow people from a common time zero; some leave follow-up before the event is observed.\",\"title\":\"Time until an event\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.aft_support\",\"gaps\":[\"no hand-written explanation yet, so the app generates one from the record\"],\"id\":\"aft_support\",\"in_more_depth\":\"No check can prove an assumption true. The strongest honest thing any of them can do is fail to contradict one, so read this next to the claim it speaks to rather than as a verdict. This one is not tied to a single assumption: it describes the data or the fit rather than testing a claim about them.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A check Causal Capybara runs for you and draws on the results screen. It appears when the design is \\\"Time until an event\\\". The card it appears on carries a plain summary of what it found in your data, and a line saying what would worry me about it.\",\"plain_language\":\"A check Causal Capybara runs for you and draws on the results screen. It appears when the design is \\\"Time until an event\\\". The card it appears on carries a plain summary of what it found in your data, and a line saying what would worry me about it.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.aft\",\"id\":\"survival.aft\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a right-censored log-normal/Weibull AFT model; standardize survival/RMST over baseline covariates under explicit causal assumptions, or report a conditional time factor.\",\"title\":\"Censored AFT regression and causal standardization\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.survival\",\"id\":\"survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Follow people from a common time zero; some leave follow-up before the event is observed.\",\"title\":\"Time until an event\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Time until an event\"],\"title\":\"Conditional treatment and censoring support\",\"worry_when\":\"The card itself says what would worry me here, because that depends on the numbers this particular run produced. A longer written explanation of this check has not been added to the library yet.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[\"no hand-written explanation yet, so the app generates one from the record\"],\"id\":\"diagnostic:aft_support\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"aft_support\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A check Causal Capybara runs for you and draws on the results screen. It appears when the design is \\\"Time until an event\\\". The card it appears on carries a plain summary of what it found in your data, and a line saying what would worry me about it.\",\"title\":\"Conditional treatment and censoring support\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aaugmentation_gap> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Augmentation gap" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:augmentation_gap\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.augmentation_gap\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"augmentation_gap\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Doubly robust methods combine a model of the outcome with a model of the treatment, and are consistent if either one is right. The correction term is the part that makes that guarantee work. Its size measures how much the two models disagree about the same data. A small gap means both models tell the same story and the guarantee is not being exercised. A large one means they do not, and the estimate is relying on the promise that at least one of them is right -- a promise that cannot be checked.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The distance between the plain model-based estimate and the version with a correction term added.\",\"plain_language\":\"The distance between the plain model-based estimate and the version with a correction term added.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Regress backwards from the final outcome, then correct with the treatment probabilities: right if either set of models is right.\",\"title\":\"Sequential doubly robust (ICE g-formula with augmentation)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.aipw\",\"id\":\"obs.aipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use a treatment model and an outcome model together; it is right if either one is.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust (AIPW)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Regress backwards from the final outcome, then correct with the treatment probabilities: right if either set of models is right.\",\"title\":\"Sequential doubly robust (ICE g-formula with augmentation)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.tmle_targeting\",\"id\":\"tmle_targeting\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Targeting step\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.convex_hull\",\"explains\":\"The treated unit's characteristics can be written as a weighted average of the donors', with weights that do not go negative.\",\"id\":\"convex_hull\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor convex hull\",\"worry_when\":\"The treated unit is at an extreme of the donor distribution on the predictors, or the pre-period gap has a persistent level offset.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.convex_hull\",\"explains\":\"The treated unit's characteristics can be written as a weighted average of the donors', with weights that do not go negative.\",\"id\":\"convex_hull\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor convex hull\",\"worry_when\":\"The treated unit is at an extreme of the donor distribution on the predictors, or the pre-period gap has a persistent level offset.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\"],\"title\":\"Augmentation gap\",\"worry_when\":\"A large gap: the outcome models and the treatment models disagree about the same data, and being right if either is right is doing real work.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:augmentation_gap\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"augmentation_gap\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The distance between the plain model-based estimate and the version with a correction term added.\",\"title\":\"Augmentation gap\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Abacon_decomposition> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Goodman-Bacon decomposition" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:bacon_decomposition\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.bacon_decomposition\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"bacon_decomposition\",\"in_more_depth\":\"When units adopt a policy at different dates, a two-way fixed-effects regression is not one comparison; it is an average of many two-group, two-period comparisons. Some of them are the ones you would want: a group that adopts against a group that never does. Others compare a late adopter against an early adopter who is already treated -- using treated units as the control group. Those latter comparisons subtract the early group's ongoing effect, so if effects grow over time they can push the average in the wrong direction, and the weight attached to them can even be negative. This decomposition shows how much of your number comes from each kind, which is the evidence for whether a modern estimator is needed.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Every simple before-and-after comparison hidden inside a single fixed-effects coefficient, and how much weight each one carries.\",\"plain_language\":\"Every simple before-and-after comparison hidden inside a single fixed-effects coefficient, and how much weight each one carries.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.goodman_bacon\",\"id\":\"did.goodman_bacon\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Open up a two-way fixed effects estimate and show every two-group comparison inside it, with the weight each one carries -- including the ones that use already-treated units as controls.\",\"title\":\"Goodman-Bacon decomposition\"}],\"references\":[\"Goodman-Bacon (2021), Difference-in-differences with variation in treatment timing\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.twfe\",\"id\":\"concept.twfe\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A regression that gives every unit its own baseline level and every period its own shift, so only change within a unit over time is compared. It is the workhorse behind difference-in-differences, and it misbehaves when units adopt at different dates.\",\"title\":\"Two-way fixed effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.staggered_adoption\",\"id\":\"concept.staggered_adoption\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Units switch the policy on at different dates rather than all at once. It is the ordinary case in policy data, and it breaks the simplest before-and-after arithmetic.\",\"title\":\"Staggered adoption\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.adoption\",\"id\":\"adoption\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Adoption pattern\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Goodman-Bacon decomposition\",\"worry_when\":\"A large share of the estimate coming from comparisons that use already-treated units as controls, especially when their average differs from the clean comparisons.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:bacon_decomposition\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"bacon_decomposition\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Every simple before-and-after comparison hidden inside a single fixed-effects coefficient, and how much weight each one carries.\",\"title\":\"Goodman-Bacon decomposition\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Abandwidth_sensitivity> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Bandwidth sensitivity path" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Every discontinuity estimate is a choice about how far from the threshold to look. Narrow windows use only the most comparable units and are noisy; wide ones are precise and rely on the fitted curve to describe a longer stretch of the relationship. Automatic selectors trade the two off, and reasonable selectors disagree with each other. A finding that persists across a range of windows is much stronger than one that appears at the selected width and disappears either side. This path is how you tell which you have.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The estimate recomputed using progressively wider and narrower windows around the cutoff.\",\"plain_language\":\"The estimate recomputed using progressively wider and narrower windows around the cutoff.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.covariate_balance\",\"id\":\"rd.covariate_balance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same estimator on variables the programme cannot have changed; they should not step at the threshold.\",\"title\":\"Do the covariates jump at the cutoff too?\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.density_test\",\"id\":\"rd.density_test\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare how many units sit just below the threshold with how many sit just above.\",\"title\":\"Did people sort across the cutoff?\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.fuzzy\",\"id\":\"rd.fuzzy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Divide the jump in the outcome by the jump in take-up: the effect for the people the cutoff actually moved.\",\"title\":\"Fuzzy RD (crossing the cutoff only nudged people in)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.kink\",\"id\":\"rd.kink\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Where a formula changes slope rather than switching on, measure the change in slope of the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Regression kink (the rule bends, it does not switch)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_linear\",\"id\":\"rd.local_linear\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a line either side of the threshold and measure the step between them.\",\"title\":\"Sharp RD at the cutoff\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.multi_cutoff\",\"id\":\"rd.multi_cutoff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Several thresholds, or a boundary on a map, pooled into one design.\",\"title\":\"Multi-cutoff and geographic RD\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rd_bandwidth\",\"id\":\"diagnostic.rd_bandwidth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The estimate plotted against the bandwidth, so the choice is visible rather than buried in an option.\",\"title\":\"Bandwidth sensitivity path\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_linear\",\"id\":\"rd.local_linear\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a line either side of the threshold and measure the step between them.\",\"title\":\"Sharp RD at the cutoff\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},\"tags\":[\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"],\"title\":\"Bandwidth sensitivity path\",\"worry_when\":\"An estimate that changes sign or size sharply between neighbouring windows; a result that exists only at the widest windows, where the local approximation is weakest.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The estimate recomputed using progressively wider and narrower windows around the cutoff.\",\"title\":\"Bandwidth sensitivity path\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Abaseline_balance> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Baseline balance" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:baseline_balance\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.baseline_balance\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"baseline_balance\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Randomisation makes the arms comparable in expectation, not in every sample. The balance table is a check on the mechanics: that the randomiser worked, that nobody was reassigned, that the data was not merged wrongly. It is not a test of whether randomisation \\\"worked\\\" in a deeper sense. With many covariates some will differ by chance, and adjusting for whichever ones came out significant is a forking path, not a repair. Pre-specified covariate adjustment for precision is a different and better idea.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"In a randomised design, a check that the arms look alike on characteristics measured before assignment.\",\"plain_language\":\"In a randomised design, a check that the arms look alike on characteristics measured before assignment.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cace\",\"id\":\"rct.cace\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Not everyone offered a programme takes it. Report what the offer did, and separately what taking it up did for the people it moved.\",\"title\":\"ITT and CACE (noncompliance)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cluster\",\"id\":\"rct.cluster\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When whole schools, clinics or villages were assigned, the number of clusters is the sample size that matters.\",\"title\":\"Cluster-randomised trial\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.diff_means\",\"id\":\"rct.diff_means\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the two arms as they were randomised, and let covariates sharpen the comparison without letting them change the question.\",\"title\":\"Difference in means\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.randomization_inference\",\"id\":\"rct.randomization_inference\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-run the randomisation thousands of times with the outcomes held fixed, and see how unusual what actually happened really is.\",\"title\":\"Randomisation (Fisher) inference\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.stratified\",\"id\":\"rct.stratified\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate inside the blocks the randomisation was run in, then average them the way your estimand says to.\",\"title\":\"Blocked or stratified experiment\"}],\"references\":[\"Senn (1994)\",\"Altman (1985), Comparability of randomised groups\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.randomisation\",\"explains\":\"Assignment really was by chance, and nothing after assignment broke the comparison -- no differential dropout, no crossover you have not modelled.\",\"id\":\"randomisation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Randomisation held\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropout differs across arms; take-up is far from assignment; the randomiser was a person with discretion.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.standardised_difference\",\"id\":\"concept.standardised_difference\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The gap between groups on a covariate, divided by a pooled standard deviation, so covariates on different scales can be compared on one axis.\",\"title\":\"Standardised mean difference\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.randomisation\",\"explains\":\"Assignment really was by chance, and nothing after assignment broke the comparison -- no differential dropout, no crossover you have not modelled.\",\"id\":\"randomisation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Randomisation held\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropout differs across arms; take-up is far from assignment; the randomiser was a person with discretion.\"},\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"],\"title\":\"Baseline balance\",\"worry_when\":\"A large imbalance on a strong predictor of the outcome; imbalance that correlates with the order of enrolment; differential dropout after assignment.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:baseline_balance\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"baseline_balance\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"In a randomised design, a check that the arms look alike on characteristics measured before assignment.\",\"title\":\"Baseline balance\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Abias_correction> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Bias correction and robust interval" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:bias_correction\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.bias_correction\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"bias_correction\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The window width that gives the most accurate estimate is deliberately a little too wide -- it accepts some bias in exchange for a lot less noise. That trade means the leftover bias is about the same size as the standard error, so an interval computed as though there were none is too narrow and sits slightly off-centre. The remedy is to estimate the bias with a more flexible fit, subtract it, and widen the interval to account for having estimated it. The corrected interval is wider on purpose. It is the honest one at a window chosen for accuracy.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Fitting a curve over a window leaves a predictable error at the edge. This shows how much correcting for it moves the estimate and widens the interval.\",\"plain_language\":\"Fitting a curve over a window leaves a predictable error at the edge. This shows how much correcting for it moves the estimate and widens the interval.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.fuzzy\",\"id\":\"rd.fuzzy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Divide the jump in the outcome by the jump in take-up: the effect for the people the cutoff actually moved.\",\"title\":\"Fuzzy RD (crossing the cutoff only nudged people in)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.kink\",\"id\":\"rd.kink\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Where a formula changes slope rather than switching on, measure the change in slope of the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Regression kink (the rule bends, it does not switch)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_linear\",\"id\":\"rd.local_linear\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a line either side of the threshold and measure the step between them.\",\"title\":\"Sharp RD at the cutoff\"}],\"references\":[\"Calonico, Cattaneo & Titiunik (2014), Robust nonparametric confidence intervals\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.bias_correction\",\"id\":\"method.rd.bias_correction\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Why the wider interval is the one to quote, and why the corrected point estimate is not always the one shown beside it.\",\"title\":\"Bias correction and the robust interval\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"id\":\"bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Bandwidth sensitivity path\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},\"tags\":[\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"],\"title\":\"Bias correction and robust interval\",\"worry_when\":\"A correction larger than one standard error, which means the fitted curve is doing real work inside the window and the answer depends on the shape you assumed.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:bias_correction\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"bias_correction\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Fitting a curve over a window leaves a predictable error at the edge. This shows how much correcting for it moves the estimate and widens the interval.\",\"title\":\"Bias correction and robust interval\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Ablock_structure> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Block structure" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:block_structure\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.block_structure\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"block_structure\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A blocked trial is many small trials stacked together, and the analysis has to respect that: comparisons are made inside blocks and then averaged. This table is how you check the stacking is sound. Blocks that hold only treated units, or only untreated ones, contain no comparison and are dropped, and the table says how many were lost that way. Block sizes also decide how the interval is computed. With two units in a block there is no spread to measure inside it, so the calculation falls back on pooling across blocks, which assumes the blocks behave alike. That is a real assumption and it is worth knowing when you are leaning on it.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"When randomisation was done separately inside strata -- clinics, schools, waves -- this shows how big each block is and what share of it was treated.\",\"plain_language\":\"When randomisation was done separately inside strata -- clinics, schools, waves -- this shows how big each block is and what share of it was treated.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.stratified\",\"id\":\"rct.stratified\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate inside the blocks the randomisation was run in, then average them the way your estimand says to.\",\"title\":\"Blocked or stratified experiment\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.block_weighting\",\"id\":\"block_weighting\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Block weights\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.randomisation\",\"explains\":\"Assignment really was by chance, and nothing after assignment broke the comparison -- no differential dropout, no crossover you have not modelled.\",\"id\":\"randomisation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Randomisation held\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropout differs across arms; take-up is far from assignment; the randomiser was a person with discretion.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.baseline_balance\",\"id\":\"baseline_balance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Baseline balance\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.randomisation\",\"explains\":\"Assignment really was by chance, and nothing after assignment broke the comparison -- no differential dropout, no crossover you have not modelled.\",\"id\":\"randomisation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Randomisation held\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropout differs across arms; take-up is far from assignment; the randomiser was a person with discretion.\"},\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"],\"title\":\"Block structure\",\"worry_when\":\"Most blocks hold a single treated and a single control unit, or a handful of large blocks carry the whole estimate; the interval then rests on the pooled fallback rather than on anything measured inside the blocks.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:block_structure\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"block_structure\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"When randomisation was done separately inside strata -- clinics, schools, waves -- this shows how big each block is and what share of it was treated.\",\"title\":\"Block structure\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Ablock_weighting> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Block weights" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:block_weighting\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.block_weighting\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"block_weighting\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Averaging the block-level effects with each block weighted by how many units it holds gives the average effect across everyone in the study. Putting an indicator variable in for each block instead gives a precision-weighted average: it leans on the blocks where the treated share is nearest a half, because those blocks carry the most information. The two agree only when the treated share is the same in every block. Neither is wrong. But if you asked for the average effect on this population, the size-weighted number is the one that answers it, and the regression coefficient is quietly answering a different question under the same name.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Two ways of combining blocked results give two different numbers, and the difference is a difference in the question, not in the arithmetic.\",\"plain_language\":\"Two ways of combining blocked results give two different numbers, and the difference is a difference in the question, not in the arithmetic.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.stratified\",\"id\":\"rct.stratified\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate inside the blocks the randomisation was run in, then average them the way your estimand says to.\",\"title\":\"Blocked or stratified experiment\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.block_structure\",\"id\":\"block_structure\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Block structure\"},{\"acronym\":\"ATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ate\",\"id\":\"ATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"If everyone got the programme, how would the average outcome change?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.estimand_first\",\"id\":\"concept.estimand_first\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Decide what number you want before deciding how to compute it. The estimator does not define the question; the question restricts the estimators.\",\"title\":\"Estimand first, estimator second\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.randomisation\",\"explains\":\"Assignment really was by chance, and nothing after assignment broke the comparison -- no differential dropout, no crossover you have not modelled.\",\"id\":\"randomisation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Randomisation held\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropout differs across arms; take-up is far from assignment; the randomiser was a person with discretion.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.randomisation\",\"explains\":\"Assignment really was by chance, and nothing after assignment broke the comparison -- no differential dropout, no crossover you have not modelled.\",\"id\":\"randomisation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Randomisation held\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropout differs across arms; take-up is far from assignment; the randomiser was a person with discretion.\"},\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"],\"title\":\"Block weights\",\"worry_when\":\"The two numbers disagree while the treated share moves across blocks; the choice of estimator is then a choice of estimand, not a choice of standard error.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:block_weighting\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"block_weighting\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Two ways of combining blocked results give two different numbers, and the difference is a difference in the question, not in the arithmetic.\",\"title\":\"Block weights\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Abootstrap_spread> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Bootstrap spread" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:bootstrap_spread\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.bootstrap_spread\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"bootstrap_spread\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Resampling the data with replacement and repeating the entire fit -- models and all -- gives a picture of how much the answer would move if you had drawn a different sample from the same population. It is the fallback when no formula for the standard error is trustworthy, which is often the case once weights have been estimated rather than known. Two things are worth reading here rather than just the interval. First, how many resamples failed: a fit that breaks on some draws is telling you the model is fragile. Second, the shape: two humps mean the models are not fitting the same way on every resample, and an average of two behaviours is not a summary of either.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The whole estimate recomputed on many resamples of the data, and whether those resamples behaved themselves.\",\"plain_language\":\"The whole estimate recomputed on many resamples of the data, and whether those resamples behaved themselves.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.gformula\",\"id\":\"long.gformula\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model each period in turn, then simulate the world where everyone followed the strategy you name.\",\"title\":\"Parametric g-formula\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.seed_stability\",\"id\":\"seed_stability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Stability across seeds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.fold_stability\",\"id\":\"fold_stability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Stability across folds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.subset_stability\",\"id\":\"subset_stability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Stability on random subsets\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\"],\"title\":\"Bootstrap spread\",\"worry_when\":\"Resamples that fail to produce an estimate; a distribution with two humps; a bootstrap spread far above what the formula-based standard error claimed.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:bootstrap_spread\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"bootstrap_spread\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The whole estimate recomputed on many resamples of the data, and whether those resamples behaved themselves.\",\"title\":\"Bootstrap spread\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acate_calibration> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "CATE calibration" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:cate_calibration\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cate_calibration\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"cate_calibration\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A model will always produce varying predictions, including from pure noise. Calibration asks whether the ranking means anything: regress the realised effect on the predicted one, and see whether the slope is near one. A slope near zero says the heterogeneity is a model artefact. This is the check that separates \\\"we found who benefits\\\" from \\\"we fitted a forest\\\".\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Sort units by predicted effect, then check whether the groups you predicted would benefit most actually did.\",\"plain_language\":\"Sort units by predicted effect, then check whether the groups you predicted would benefit most actually did.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_plr\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_plr\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Predict the outcome and the treatment from the confounders, then regress what is left of one on what is left of the other.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (partially linear)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.policy_tree\",\"id\":\"ml.policy_tree\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn a short, readable rule for who to treat, then value that rule on data it never saw.\",\"title\":\"Policy tree (who should be treated)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\"}],\"references\":[\"Chernozhukov et al. (2018), Generic machine learning inference\"],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"CATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cate\",\"id\":\"CATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"How does the effect vary from person to person, or place to place?\",\"title\":\"Conditional average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rate\",\"id\":\"rate\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Rank-weighted average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.honest_splitting\",\"id\":\"concept.honest_splitting\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Use one half of the data to decide where the subgroups are and the other half to estimate their effects.\",\"title\":\"Honest splitting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"CATE calibration\",\"worry_when\":\"A calibration slope far from one, or a confidence interval on it that comfortably includes zero.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:cate_calibration\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"cate_calibration\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Sort units by predicted effect, then check whether the groups you predicted would benefit most actually did.\",\"title\":\"CATE calibration\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acate_distribution> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "CATE distribution" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:cate_distribution\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cate_distribution\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"cate_distribution\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A model that predicts one effect per person will always produce a spread, because estimation error alone produces one. So this picture is a description of the predictions, not evidence that the underlying effects differ. Read it together with the calibration check: if the ranking cannot be shown to mean anything, the width here is mostly noise. A spread of essentially zero is a finding rather than a failure. It says the learner looked for differences between people and did not find any that the data supports, which is a perfectly good answer to the question.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The spread of the per-person effect estimates: how different the model thinks people are.\",\"plain_language\":\"The spread of the per-person effect estimates: how different the model thinks people are.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_plr\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_plr\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Predict the outcome and the treatment from the confounders, then regress what is left of one on what is left of the other.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (partially linear)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.policy_tree\",\"id\":\"ml.policy_tree\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn a short, readable rule for who to treat, then value that rule on data it never saw.\",\"title\":\"Policy tree (who should be treated)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"CATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cate\",\"id\":\"CATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"How does the effect vary from person to person, or place to place?\",\"title\":\"Conditional average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cate_calibration\",\"id\":\"cate_calibration\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"CATE calibration\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rate\",\"id\":\"rate\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Rank-weighted average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.multiplicity\",\"id\":\"concept.multiplicity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Look at enough subgroups and one will look impressive. Pre-specify them, or report that you did not.\",\"title\":\"Subgroups and multiplicity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"CATE distribution\",\"worry_when\":\"A wide spread while the calibration check says the ranking carries no information; you are then looking at estimation error, not heterogeneity.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:cate_distribution\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"cate_distribution\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The spread of the per-person effect estimates: how different the model thinks people are.\",\"title\":\"CATE distribution\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acde_path> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Controlled direct effect across mediator levels" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:cde_path\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cde_path\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"cde_path\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A controlled direct effect asks what the treatment would do if the mediator were fixed, by intervention, at a particular level for everybody. That is a well-defined and checkable question -- unlike the natural decomposition, it does not require imagining a world nobody could live in -- but the answer depends on which level you fix. When the path across levels is flat, quoting one number is harmless. When it slopes, the path is the answer, and the level chosen must be one that a policy could actually impose.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The direct effect computed with the mediator held at each of several values. There is one number per value, not one number.\",\"plain_language\":\"The direct effect computed with the mediator held at each of several values. There is one number per value, not one number.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.controlled_direct\",\"id\":\"med.controlled_direct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"What the treatment would still do if the mediator were fixed at one value for everybody.\",\"title\":\"Controlled direct effect\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.controlled_direct\",\"id\":\"med.controlled_direct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"What the treatment would still do if the mediator were fixed at one value for everybody.\",\"title\":\"Controlled direct effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.treatment_mediator_interaction\",\"id\":\"treatment_mediator_interaction\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Treatment-mediator interaction\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.cross_world\",\"id\":\"concept.cross_world\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A condition relating outcomes under treatment and mediator values that no single person could ever experience together. Not testable, even in principle.\",\"title\":\"Cross-world assumptions\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.sequential_ignorability\",\"explains\":\"No unmeasured confounding of the treatment-outcome relationship, and none of the mediator-outcome relationship either, even after conditioning on treatment.\",\"id\":\"sequential_ignorability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Sequential ignorability\",\"worry_when\":\"There is a variable affected by treatment that also confounds the mediator-outcome relationship. Under that structure the natural effects are not identified at all.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.sequential_ignorability\",\"explains\":\"No unmeasured confounding of the treatment-outcome relationship, and none of the mediator-outcome relationship either, even after conditioning on treatment.\",\"id\":\"sequential_ignorability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Sequential ignorability\",\"worry_when\":\"There is a variable affected by treatment that also confounds the mediator-outcome relationship. Under that structure the natural effects are not identified at all.\"},\"tags\":[\"How it worked (pathways)\"],\"title\":\"Controlled direct effect across mediator levels\",\"worry_when\":\"A steep path reported as \\\"the direct effect\\\"; the honest answer is then the path itself.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:cde_path\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"cde_path\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The direct effect computed with the mediator held at each of several values. There is one number per value, not one number.\",\"title\":\"Controlled direct effect across mediator levels\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acell_means> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Cell means" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:cell_means\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cell_means\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"cell_means\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A two-group, two-period difference-in-differences is arithmetic on four numbers: the change in the treated group minus the change in the comparison group. Seeing the four makes the estimate legible, and it makes obvious things that a coefficient hides, such as a comparison group that moved a long way on its own. When a third dimension is involved -- a group inside the treated units that the policy could not have affected -- the same table shows the extra subtraction, and whether the thing being subtracted really looks like a common shock.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The four averages -- treated and comparison, before and after -- that the headline difference is built from.\",\"plain_language\":\"The four averages -- treated and comparison, before and after -- that the headline difference is built from.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.triple_diff\",\"id\":\"did.triple_diff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the treated-versus-comparison difference for the group the policy could reach with the same difference for a group inside the same units that it could not.\",\"title\":\"Triple differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twoway_2x2\",\"id\":\"did.twoway_2x2\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Two groups, two periods: compare how much the treated group changed with how much the comparison group changed over the same window.\",\"title\":\"Difference-in-differences (2x2)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.raw_means\",\"id\":\"raw_means\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Raw means by cohort and period\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twoway_2x2\",\"id\":\"did.twoway_2x2\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Two groups, two periods: compare how much the treated group changed with how much the comparison group changed over the same window.\",\"title\":\"Difference-in-differences (2x2)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Cell means\",\"worry_when\":\"One cell holding very few observations; a comparison group whose change is large in its own right, so the estimate is a difference between two moving things.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:cell_means\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"cell_means\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The four averages -- treated and comparison, before and after -- that the headline difference is built from.\",\"title\":\"Cell means\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acem_strata> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Coarsened strata and common support" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:cem_strata\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cem_strata\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"cem_strata\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Coarsened exact matching guarantees balance by refusing to compare across cells. The price is pruning: with several covariates, most cells contain only one arm, and the treated units in those cells leave the analysis. That pruning is information. It tells you exactly where the data cannot answer the question, which a smooth propensity model hides.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many of the coarsened cells contain both treated and untreated units, and how many treated units had no comparable cell at all.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many of the coarsened cells contain both treated and untreated units, and how many treated units had no comparable cell at all.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.cem\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.cem\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Bin the covariates, then compare only inside bins that contain both arms.\",\"title\":\"Coarsened exact matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.optimal\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.optimal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Choose the whole set of matches at once to minimise total distance, rather than greedily one treated unit at a time.\",\"title\":\"Optimal and full matching\"}],\"references\":[\"Iacus, King & Porro (2012)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.cem\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.cem\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Bin the covariates, then compare only inside bins that contain both arms.\",\"title\":\"Coarsened exact matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.consort\",\"id\":\"concept.consort\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A step-by-step record of how the imported rows became the analysis sample, with a reason for every row that left.\",\"title\":\"The sample flow\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Coarsened strata and common support\",\"worry_when\":\"A large share of treated units pruned; the estimand quietly becoming the effect for an unusual subset.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:cem_strata\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"cem_strata\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many of the coarsened cells contain both treated and untreated units, and how many treated units had no comparable cell at all.\",\"title\":\"Coarsened strata and common support\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acensoring_weights> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Censoring weights" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:censoring_weights\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.censoring_weights\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"censoring_weights\",\"in_more_depth\":\"People drop out, and if they drop out for reasons connected to their treatment or their outcome, the ones who remain are a selected group. The standard repair is to model who stays and then let each person who stayed stand in for the similar people who left. That works only if staying is predictable from what was measured. It also has the same failure mode as any weighting: when few people like someone remained, that person's weight becomes large and the estimate leans on them. The range of weights reported here is how you see it happening.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How people who left the study before the end are accounted for.\",\"plain_language\":\"How people who left the study before the end are accounted for.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle_survival\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle_survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Time-to-event outcomes with time-varying treatment and censoring, targeted at each time.\",\"title\":\"Longitudinal TMLE for survival outcomes\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.msm_iptw\",\"id\":\"long.msm_iptw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight each person-period so the treated and untreated histories look alike, then fit one simple model to the reweighted data.\",\"title\":\"Marginal structural model (time-varying weights)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.selection_bias\",\"id\":\"concept.selection_bias\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Who ends up in your analysis sample can create an association all by itself, quite apart from confounding.\",\"title\":\"Selection bias\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.differential_attrition\",\"id\":\"differential_attrition\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Differential attrition\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.weights_by_period\",\"id\":\"weights_by_period\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Weights by period\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_time_varying_confounding\",\"explains\":\"At every period, the things that drive the treatment decision and also affect the outcome are measured -- and measured before that decision.\",\"id\":\"no_time_varying_confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No unmeasured time-varying confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Clinical measurements taken after the dose was changed; employment status recorded at the end of a period used to explain treatment at its start.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_time_varying_confounding\",\"explains\":\"At every period, the things that drive the treatment decision and also affect the outcome are measured -- and measured before that decision.\",\"id\":\"no_time_varying_confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No unmeasured time-varying confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Clinical measurements taken after the dose was changed; employment status recorded at the end of a period used to explain treatment at its start.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\"],\"title\":\"Censoring weights\",\"worry_when\":\"Large censoring weights, meaning a few people who stayed are standing in for many who left; or dropout that is clearly related to treatment.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:censoring_weights\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"censoring_weights\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How people who left the study before the end are accounted for.\",\"title\":\"Censoring weights\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acluster_structure> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Cluster structure" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:cluster_structure\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cluster_structure\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"cluster_structure\",\"in_more_depth\":\"When whole schools, villages or clinics were assigned together, the schools are the units that were randomised, not the pupils. The correction for that is a cluster-robust standard error, and its reliability grows with the number of clusters, not the number of rows: fifty thousand pupils in twelve schools carry twelve schools' worth of information about the policy. The table therefore reports the count, the spread of sizes and the share held by the largest cluster, because one or two dominant clusters behave like an even smaller study than the count suggests.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many groups treatment was assigned to, how big they are, and how much of the sample the largest one holds.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many groups treatment was assigned to, how big they are, and how much of the sample the largest one holds.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.interference.exposure\",\"id\":\"interference.exposure\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When one unit's treatment affects another's outcome, estimate the direct and spillover effects separately instead of pretending neither exists.\",\"title\":\"Interference and spillovers\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cluster\",\"id\":\"rct.cluster\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When whole schools, clinics or villages were assigned, the number of clusters is the sample size that matters.\",\"title\":\"Cluster-randomised trial\"}],\"references\":[\"Cameron & Miller (2015), A practitioner's guide to cluster-robust inference\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.clustering\",\"id\":\"concept.clustering\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"When treatment was assigned to whole groups -- states, schools, clinics -- the number of independent observations is closer to the number of groups than the number of rows.\",\"title\":\"Clustering\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.cluster_robust_se\",\"id\":\"concept.cluster_robust_se\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A way of computing uncertainty that treats each group -- a school, a village, a clinic -- as one group's worth of independent evidence, rather than counting every row inside it separately.\",\"title\":\"Cluster-robust standard errors\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.icc_design_effect\",\"id\":\"icc_design_effect\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Intra-cluster correlation and design effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"],\"title\":\"Cluster structure\",\"worry_when\":\"Fewer than about forty clusters, or one or two clusters holding much of the sample. The interval is then too narrow and the p-value too small.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:cluster_structure\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"cluster_structure\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many groups treatment was assigned to, how big they are, and how much of the sample the largest one holds.\",\"title\":\"Cluster structure\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acohort_att> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Cohort effects" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:cohort_att\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cohort_att\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"cohort_att\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Units that adopted in 2015 and units that adopted in 2019 may have had very different experiences: different circumstances, different versions of the policy, different lengths of exposure. Estimating each cohort separately shows that variation instead of averaging it away, and the overall figure is then an explicit average of these with weights you can see. Cohorts built from very few units produce noisy estimates, so read the spread together with the intervals rather than as a set of firm findings.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"One effect per group of units that adopted at the same date, instead of one number for all of them.\",\"plain_language\":\"One effect per group of units that adopted at the same date, instead of one number for all of them.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.dr_did\",\"id\":\"did.dr_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One before-and-after with covariates, using an outcome model and a comparison-group model together so either one can carry it.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust DiD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.gardner_2s\",\"id\":\"did.gardner_2s\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Strip out unit and period effects using untreated cells only, then regress what is left on the policy.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage DiD (Gardner / Wooldridge-Mundlak)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.sun_abraham\",\"id\":\"did.sun_abraham\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Give every cohort its own event-study path, then average the paths by cohort size instead of letting the regression choose the weights.\",\"title\":\"Sun & Abraham event study\"}],\"references\":[\"Callaway & Sant'Anna (2021)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"ATT(g,t)\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cohort_att\",\"id\":\"cohort_ATT\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For each group that adopted it, what did their own rollout do?\",\"title\":\"Group-time average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.group_time_att\",\"id\":\"group_time_att\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.staggered_adoption\",\"id\":\"concept.staggered_adoption\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Units switch the policy on at different dates rather than all at once. It is the ordinary case in policy data, and it breaks the simplest before-and-after arithmetic.\",\"title\":\"Staggered adoption\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Cohort effects\",\"worry_when\":\"Cohorts that disagree in sign or size, which means a single average hides which rollout actually worked; or a cohort resting on one or two units.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:cohort_att\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"cohort_att\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"One effect per group of units that adopted at the same date, instead of one number for all of them.\",\"title\":\"Cohort effects\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acohort_interactions> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Cohort-by-period interactions" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:cohort_interactions\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cohort_interactions\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"cohort_interactions\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Interacting the cohort with time since adoption avoids the trap of forcing one path on every group. It also produces a great many coefficients, and the ones estimated from a single small cohort will swing around and drag the weighted average with them. Reading the grid is worthwhile before reading the average: it shows whether the pattern is a genuine common shape or one cohort's story presented as everybody's.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The full set of coefficients, one for each cohort at each distance from its own adoption date, before they are averaged.\",\"plain_language\":\"The full set of coefficients, one for each cohort at each distance from its own adoption date, before they are averaged.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.sun_abraham\",\"id\":\"did.sun_abraham\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Give every cohort its own event-study path, then average the paths by cohort size instead of letting the regression choose the weights.\",\"title\":\"Sun & Abraham event study\"}],\"references\":[\"Sun & Abraham (2021)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.group_time_att\",\"id\":\"group_time_att\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.event_study\",\"id\":\"event_study\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Event study\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.staggered_adoption\",\"id\":\"concept.staggered_adoption\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Units switch the policy on at different dates rather than all at once. It is the ordinary case in policy data, and it breaks the simplest before-and-after arithmetic.\",\"title\":\"Staggered adoption\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Cohort-by-period interactions\",\"worry_when\":\"A cell resting on one cohort with a handful of units; its coefficient will swing and pull the weighted average with it.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:cohort_interactions\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"cohort_interactions\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The full set of coefficients, one for each cohort at each distance from its own adoption date, before they are averaged.\",\"title\":\"Cohort-by-period interactions\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acomparison_group> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Comparison group used" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:comparison_group\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.comparison_group\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"comparison_group\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A staggered design can draw its comparison from units that never adopt, or from units that have not adopted yet, or from both. Never-treated units are the cleanest comparison and are sometimes unusual for the very reason they never adopted. Not-yet-treated units are more similar but stop being available as the panel fills up with adopters. This row says which pool was used and how thin it is. A handful of never-treated units, or cohorts of one, can do most of the work while the confidence interval gives no hint of how much rests on them.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Which units are standing in for what would have happened without the policy, and how many of them there are.\",\"plain_language\":\"Which units are standing in for what would have happened without the policy, and how many of them there are.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.dr_did\",\"id\":\"did.dr_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One before-and-after with covariates, using an outcome model and a comparison-group model together so either one can carry it.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust DiD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.gardner_2s\",\"id\":\"did.gardner_2s\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Strip out unit and period effects using untreated cells only, then regress what is left on the policy.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage DiD (Gardner / Wooldridge-Mundlak)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.sun_abraham\",\"id\":\"did.sun_abraham\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Give every cohort its own event-study path, then average the paths by cohort size instead of letting the regression choose the weights.\",\"title\":\"Sun & Abraham event study\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.not_yet_treated\",\"id\":\"concept.not_yet_treated\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Units that do adopt the policy, but later. While they are still waiting they can serve as the comparison group for units that adopted earlier.\",\"title\":\"Not-yet-treated comparisons\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.adoption\",\"id\":\"adoption\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Adoption pattern\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Comparison group used\",\"worry_when\":\"A very small comparison pool, or one built entirely from units that are unlike the adopters in obvious ways.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:comparison_group\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"comparison_group\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Which units are standing in for what would have happened without the policy, and how many of them there are.\",\"title\":\"Comparison group used\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acompliance> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Compliance with assignment" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:compliance\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.compliance\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"compliance\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Offering a programme is not the same as delivering it. Some people offered it decline; some people not offered it find it anyway. The gap in take-up between the arms is what the analysis divides by when it converts the effect of the offer into the effect of the treatment, so a small gap means dividing by a small number, and the interval widens fast. That same gap defines who the number describes. Provided the offer never pushed anybody away from treatment, the people whose take-up the offer actually changed are the compliers, and the estimate is their effect -- not the effect for the people who would have taken it up regardless, nor for those who never would, nor for the population as a whole.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many people in each arm actually took the treatment, and what that implies about who the estimate is about.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many people in each arm actually took the treatment, and what that implies about who the estimate is about.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cace\",\"id\":\"rct.cace\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Not everyone offered a programme takes it. Report what the offer did, and separately what taking it up did for the people it moved.\",\"title\":\"ITT and CACE (noncompliance)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"CACE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cace\",\"id\":\"CACE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For the people who comply with what they were offered, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Complier average causal effect\"},{\"acronym\":\"ITT\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.itt\",\"id\":\"ITT\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For everyone who was offered it, what did the offer do?\",\"title\":\"Intention to treat\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.monotonicity\",\"explains\":\"The instrument pushes everyone the same way, or not at all. Nobody takes the treatment only because they were discouraged from it.\",\"id\":\"monotonicity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Monotonicity / no defiers\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is an incentive that could plausibly backfire for some people, or it is a judge or examiner whose leniency ordering differs across case types.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.compliers\",\"id\":\"compliers\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Complier share\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.monotonicity\",\"explains\":\"The instrument pushes everyone the same way, or not at all. Nobody takes the treatment only because they were discouraged from it.\",\"id\":\"monotonicity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Monotonicity / no defiers\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is an incentive that could plausibly backfire for some people, or it is a judge or examiner whose leniency ordering differs across case types.\"},\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"],\"title\":\"Compliance with assignment\",\"worry_when\":\"Take-up in the control arm is high, or the take-up gap is small; the group the estimate describes is then a small and unusual slice of the sample.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:compliance\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"compliance\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many people in each arm actually took the treatment, and what that implies about who the estimate is about.\",\"title\":\"Compliance with assignment\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acompliers> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Complier share" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:compliers\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.compliers\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"compliers\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Divide the sample into three kinds. Some would take the treatment whatever happened. Some would never take it. The rest -- the ones the instrument actually moves -- are the only people the estimate describes. Their share can be worked out from the take-up rates in each group, provided the instrument never pushed anybody in the wrong direction. A small share is not a flaw in the arithmetic, but it does limit the claim. An estimate for eight per cent of the sample is an estimate for eight per cent of the sample, and there is no statistical route from there to everybody else.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument only moves some people. The estimate describes those people, and this says roughly who and how many they are.\",\"plain_language\":\"An instrument only moves some people. The estimate describes those people, and this says roughly who and how many they are.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.mte\",\"id\":\"iv.mte\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Trace the effect as a function of how reluctant a unit is to be treated, instead of reporting one local average.\",\"title\":\"Marginal treatment effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.weak_robust\",\"id\":\"iv.weak_robust\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the set of effects the data cannot reject, which stays honest however weak the instrument is.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust confidence set\"}],\"references\":[\"Imbens & Angrist (1994)\",\"Angrist, Imbens & Rubin (1996)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"LATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.late\",\"id\":\"LATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who only got it because of the instrument or the cutoff, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Local average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.monotonicity\",\"explains\":\"The instrument pushes everyone the same way, or not at all. Nobody takes the treatment only because they were discouraged from it.\",\"id\":\"monotonicity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Monotonicity / no defiers\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is an incentive that could plausibly backfire for some people, or it is a judge or examiner whose leniency ordering differs across case types.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.compliance\",\"id\":\"compliance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Compliance with assignment\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.first_stage\",\"id\":\"first_stage\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"First stage\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.monotonicity\",\"explains\":\"The instrument pushes everyone the same way, or not at all. Nobody takes the treatment only because they were discouraged from it.\",\"id\":\"monotonicity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Monotonicity / no defiers\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is an incentive that could plausibly backfire for some people, or it is a judge or examiner whose leniency ordering differs across case types.\"},\"tags\":[\"Something else pushed people in\"],\"title\":\"Complier share\",\"worry_when\":\"A small share of movers, which makes the estimate about a sliver of the sample; or a negative share, which means the encouragement pushed some people the other way and the required assumption is in trouble.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:compliers\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"compliers\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"An instrument only moves some people. The estimate describes those people, and this says roughly who and how many they are.\",\"title\":\"Complier share\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aconstant_effect> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Constant-effect assumption" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:constant_effect\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.constant_effect\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"constant_effect\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The partially linear model writes the outcome as one effect multiplied by the treatment, plus a flexible function of the confounders. The flexibility is all on the confounding side; the effect itself is a single number by construction. That restriction makes the estimator efficient and simple. When effects really do differ between people, the number it returns is a weighted average that leans towards the units whose treatment was hardest to predict. That is not the average treatment effect, though it is often close. If the heterogeneity checks find real differences, use the estimator that targets the average directly instead.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Some estimators assume the treatment does the same thing to everybody. This says what that assumption buys and what it costs.\",\"plain_language\":\"Some estimators assume the treatment does the same thing to everybody. This says what that assumption buys and what it costs.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_plr\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_plr\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Predict the outcome and the treatment from the confounders, then regress what is left of one on what is left of the other.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (partially linear)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_plr\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_plr\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Predict the outcome and the treatment from the confounders, then regress what is left of one on what is left of the other.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (partially linear)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"acronym\":\"ATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ate\",\"id\":\"ATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"If everyone got the programme, how would the average outcome change?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cate_calibration\",\"id\":\"cate_calibration\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"CATE calibration\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Constant-effect assumption\",\"worry_when\":\"The calibration diagnostic finds real heterogeneity, in which case this restriction is doing work that the data does not support.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:constant_effect\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"constant_effect\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Some estimators assume the treatment does the same thing to everybody. This says what that assumption buys and what it costs.\",\"title\":\"Constant-effect assumption\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Acovariate_balance> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Covariate balance" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:covariate_balance\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.covariate_balance\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"covariate_balance\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Age, sex, anything recorded before the threshold was applied: none of these can be affected by crossing the line, so any apparent jump in them is a jump the design has manufactured. It usually means something else changes at the same threshold -- another programme, an administrative boundary, a different data source either side. This is one of the few checks that can catch the continuity assumption failing. A clean result does not establish it: the variables you did not measure could still jump.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same estimate on variables the programme cannot have changed. They should show no jump.\",\"plain_language\":\"Run the same estimate on variables the programme cannot have changed. They should show no jump.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.covariate_balance\",\"id\":\"rd.covariate_balance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same estimator on variables the programme cannot have changed; they should not step at the threshold.\",\"title\":\"Do the covariates jump at the cutoff too?\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.fuzzy\",\"id\":\"rd.fuzzy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Divide the jump in the outcome by the jump in take-up: the effect for the people the cutoff actually moved.\",\"title\":\"Fuzzy RD (crossing the cutoff only nudged people in)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.kink\",\"id\":\"rd.kink\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Where a formula changes slope rather than switching on, measure the change in slope of the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Regression kink (the rule bends, it does not switch)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_linear\",\"id\":\"rd.local_linear\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a line either side of the threshold and measure the step between them.\",\"title\":\"Sharp RD at the cutoff\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_randomization\",\"id\":\"rd.local_randomization\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat a narrow window around the cutoff as a small randomised experiment rather than fitting a curve through it.\",\"title\":\"Local randomisation RD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.multi_cutoff\",\"id\":\"rd.multi_cutoff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Several thresholds, or a boundary on a map, pooled into one design.\",\"title\":\"Multi-cutoff and geographic RD\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rd.covariate_balance\",\"id\":\"diagnostic.rd.covariate_balance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Each pre-existing characteristic's jump at the threshold, expressed on a common scale so variables in different units can be compared.\",\"title\":\"Covariate jumps at the cutoff, drawn\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.standardised_difference\",\"id\":\"concept.standardised_difference\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The gap between groups on a covariate, divided by a pooled standard deviation, so covariates on different scales can be compared on one axis.\",\"title\":\"Standardised mean difference\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"],\"title\":\"Covariate balance\",\"worry_when\":\"A variable the programme cannot have changed jumping at the cutoff; or no covariates supplied at all, in which case nothing has been checked.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:covariate_balance\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"covariate_balance\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Run the same estimate on variables the programme cannot have changed. They should show no jump.\",\"title\":\"Covariate balance\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Adifferential_attrition> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Differential attrition" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:differential_attrition\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.differential_attrition\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"differential_attrition\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Randomisation makes the arms comparable at the moment of assignment. It says nothing about who is still in the file at the end. If the people offered the programme are more likely to answer the follow-up survey -- or less likely -- then the sample being compared has been selected by something that happened after assignment, and the protection randomisation bought you is gone. Adjusting for baseline characteristics does not repair this, because the selection happened downstream of the treatment rather than before it. What helps is bounding the answer under best and worst cases for the missing rows, and reporting the loss plainly.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Whether the treated arm and the control arm lost rows at the same rate on the way from assignment to analysis.\",\"plain_language\":\"Whether the treated arm and the control arm lost rows at the same rate on the way from assignment to analysis.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cace\",\"id\":\"rct.cace\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Not everyone offered a programme takes it. Report what the offer did, and separately what taking it up did for the people it moved.\",\"title\":\"ITT and CACE (noncompliance)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cluster\",\"id\":\"rct.cluster\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When whole schools, clinics or villages were assigned, the number of clusters is the sample size that matters.\",\"title\":\"Cluster-randomised trial\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.diff_means\",\"id\":\"rct.diff_means\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the two arms as they were randomised, and let covariates sharpen the comparison without letting them change the question.\",\"title\":\"Difference in means\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.randomization_inference\",\"id\":\"rct.randomization_inference\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-run the randomisation thousands of times with the outcomes held fixed, and see how unusual what actually happened really is.\",\"title\":\"Randomisation (Fisher) inference\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.stratified\",\"id\":\"rct.stratified\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate inside the blocks the randomisation was run in, then average them the way your estimand says to.\",\"title\":\"Blocked or stratified experiment\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.selection_bias\",\"id\":\"concept.selection_bias\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Who ends up in your analysis sample can create an association all by itself, quite apart from confounding.\",\"title\":\"Selection bias\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.consort\",\"id\":\"concept.consort\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A step-by-step record of how the imported rows became the analysis sample, with a reason for every row that left.\",\"title\":\"The sample flow\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.complete_case\",\"id\":\"concept.complete_case\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Dropping every row that is missing any value the analysis needs and computing on what is left. Most software does this silently.\",\"title\":\"Complete-case analysis\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.randomisation\",\"explains\":\"Assignment really was by chance, and nothing after assignment broke the comparison -- no differential dropout, no crossover you have not modelled.\",\"id\":\"randomisation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Randomisation held\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropout differs across arms; take-up is far from assignment; the randomiser was a person with discretion.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.randomisation\",\"explains\":\"Assignment really was by chance, and nothing after assignment broke the comparison -- no differential dropout, no crossover you have not modelled.\",\"id\":\"randomisation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Randomisation held\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropout differs across arms; take-up is far from assignment; the randomiser was a person with discretion.\"},\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"],\"title\":\"Differential attrition\",\"worry_when\":\"The two arms lose rows at visibly different rates, or the reasons for leaving differ by arm.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:differential_attrition\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"differential_attrition\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Whether the treated arm and the control arm lost rows at the same rate on the way from assignment to analysis.\",\"title\":\"Differential attrition\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aebal_convergence> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Moment balance" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:ebal_convergence\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.ebal_convergence\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"ebal_convergence\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Entropy balancing does not fit a model of treatment and hope for balance; it solves directly for weights that make named summaries of the covariates -- means, and optionally spreads -- match between the groups exactly. When a solution exists, the remaining gaps are zero by construction and this table is a receipt. When no solution exists, the gaps are not zero, and that is informative: it means the treated group's profile is outside anything a reweighting of the available comparison units can reproduce. That is a data problem, not a solver problem, and no amount of tuning fixes it.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Whether the weighting actually achieved the balance it was asked for, and how far short it fell.\",\"plain_language\":\"Whether the weighting actually achieved the balance it was asked for, and how far short it fell.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.entropy\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.entropy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Find the weights closest to equal that make the covariate means match exactly.\",\"title\":\"Entropy balancing\"}],\"references\":[\"Hainmueller (2012), Entropy balancing\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.love\",\"id\":\"love\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Covariate balance (Love plot)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.weights\",\"id\":\"diagnostic.weights\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A histogram of the weight each row was given. A long tail means a few rows are carrying the answer.\",\"title\":\"The weights themselves\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Moment balance\",\"worry_when\":\"Non-zero gaps after the solve, which say the treated profile is outside what reweighting the comparison group can reach.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:ebal_convergence\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"ebal_convergence\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Whether the weighting actually achieved the balance it was asked for, and how far short it fell.\",\"title\":\"Moment balance\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aendogeneity> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Endogeneity test" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:endogeneity\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.endogeneity\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"endogeneity\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The two numbers are not competing estimates of the same thing. The plain regression describes an association across everybody; the instrumented figure is a causal effect for the people the instrument moves. They can differ because of confounding, because those people are unusual, or both. A formal test of whether the plain regression would have been enough exists and is reported, but it is only meaningful if the instrument is valid and strong. When the instrument is weak, a large gap between the two numbers is exactly what weak-instrument bias produces, so the gap is not evidence of confounding.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The plain regression estimate next to the instrumented one, and what their difference does and does not tell you.\",\"plain_language\":\"The plain regression estimate next to the instrumented one, and what their difference does and does not tell you.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.weak_robust\",\"id\":\"iv.weak_robust\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the set of effects the data cannot reject, which stays honest however weak the instrument is.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust confidence set\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.first_stage\",\"id\":\"first_stage\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"First stage\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exclusion\",\"explains\":\"The instrument affects the outcome only through the treatment. On the board, that is the arrow you did not draw.\",\"id\":\"exclusion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exclusion restriction\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is a policy that changed several things at once; it is correlated with geography, time or institutions that have their own effects; it was chosen because it predicted the outcome.\"},{\"acronym\":\"LATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.late\",\"id\":\"LATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who only got it because of the instrument or the cutoff, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Local average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Something else pushed people in\"],\"title\":\"Endogeneity test\",\"worry_when\":\"An instrumented estimate several times the size of the plain one alongside a weak first stage; weak instruments, a small violation of the exclusion restriction, and a genuinely different group of movers all look like that.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:endogeneity\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"endogeneity\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The plain regression estimate next to the instrumented one, and what their difference does and does not tell you.\",\"title\":\"Endogeneity test\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aess> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Effective sample size" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:ess\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.ess\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"ess\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Weighting concentrates the analysis on the units that resemble the other arm. Kish's effective sample size turns that concentration into a count you can compare with the nominal one. The weight histogram is the same information as a picture: a long right tail means a handful of rows are carrying the estimate, and their idiosyncrasies now show up in your confidence interval as precision you do not have.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many observations the weighted comparison is really worth, and how much of the total weight the single largest unit carries.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many observations the weighted comparison is really worth, and how much of the total weight the single largest unit carries.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.aipw\",\"id\":\"obs.aipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use a treatment model and an outcome model together; it is right if either one is.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust (AIPW)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.cem\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.cem\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Bin the covariates, then compare only inside bins that contain both arms.\",\"title\":\"Coarsened exact matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.genetic\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.genetic\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Search over distance-metric weights until balance is as good as it can get, instead of trusting one metric.\",\"title\":\"Genetic matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.nn\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.nn\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Pair each treated unit with the most similar untreated unit, then compare within pairs.\",\"title\":\"Nearest-neighbour matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.optimal\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.optimal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Choose the whole set of matches at once to minimise total distance, rather than greedily one treated unit at a time.\",\"title\":\"Optimal and full matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.cbps\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.cbps\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the treatment model so that it balances covariates, rather than fitting it to predict treatment and hoping balance follows.\",\"title\":\"Covariate balancing propensity score\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.entropy\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.entropy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Find the weights closest to equal that make the covariate means match exactly.\",\"title\":\"Entropy balancing\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.ipw\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.ipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight the sample so the treated and untreated groups look alike on the measured confounders.\",\"title\":\"Propensity weighting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.twang\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.twang\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow a boosted model for the propensity score, stopping at the iteration that balances covariates best.\",\"title\":\"Boosted propensity scores (twang)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.effective_sample_size\",\"id\":\"concept.effective_sample_size\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"How many observations your weighted comparison is really worth. Almost always smaller than the number of rows, sometimes dramatically.\",\"title\":\"Effective sample size\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.trim_curve\",\"id\":\"probe.trim_curve\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-estimate across a grid of propensity-score trimming thresholds instead of picking one and hoping.\",\"title\":\"Trimming curve\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Effective sample size\",\"worry_when\":\"An effective sample below about 40% of the nominal one; a single weight above about a fifth of the total; a histogram with a visible tail of extreme weights.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:ess\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"ess\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many observations the weighted comparison is really worth, and how much of the total weight the single largest unit carries.\",\"title\":\"Effective sample size\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aevalue> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "E-value" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:evalue\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.evalue\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"evalue\",\"in_more_depth\":\"An E-value of 2.5 means a hidden variable would have to be associated with both the treatment and the outcome by a risk ratio of at least 2.5, above and beyond the covariates already in the model, before the finding could be attributed to it entirely. The same calculation applied to the end of the confidence interval says what it would take to move the result to the edge of statistical significance rather than to nothing. It is a convenient summary and it is not a test. It says nothing about whether such a variable exists, and it assumes a single confounder acting simply. Its value comes from the comparison with the associations your measured covariates actually show.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The smallest strength of association -- with the treatment and with the outcome -- that an unmeasured variable would need to explain away the result.\",\"plain_language\":\"The smallest strength of association -- with the treatment and with the outcome -- that an unmeasured variable would need to explain away the result.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.evalue\",\"id\":\"probe.evalue\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"The smallest confounder-treatment and confounder-outcome association that could explain the result away.\",\"title\":\"E-value\"}],\"references\":[\"VanderWeele & Ding (2017), Sensitivity analysis: introducing the E-value\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.evalue_scale\",\"id\":\"evalue_scale\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"E-value scale\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.robustness_value\",\"id\":\"robustness_value\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Robustness value\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Any design\"],\"title\":\"E-value\",\"worry_when\":\"An E-value near 1, meaning a very ordinary unmeasured variable would be enough; or an E-value smaller than the associations your measured covariates display.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:evalue\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"evalue\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The smallest strength of association -- with the treatment and with the outcome -- that an unmeasured variable would need to explain away the result.\",\"title\":\"E-value\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aevalue_scale> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "E-value scale" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:evalue_scale\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.evalue_scale\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"evalue_scale\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A difference in means, an odds ratio, a hazard ratio: each needs a different bridge to the risk-ratio scale the E-value formula expects, and each bridge carries its own conditions. Converting an odds ratio assumes the outcome is rare. Converting a standardised mean difference assumes the outcome is roughly bell-shaped. Where those conditions fail, the E-value inherits the failure. This row records which conversion was used, so that a reader can judge it rather than take the resulting number at face value.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The E-value is defined for ratios of risks, so anything measured on another scale has to be converted first -- and the conversion is an approximation.\",\"plain_language\":\"The E-value is defined for ratios of risks, so anything measured on another scale has to be converted first -- and the conversion is an approximation.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.evalue\",\"id\":\"probe.evalue\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"The smallest confounder-treatment and confounder-outcome association that could explain the result away.\",\"title\":\"E-value\"}],\"references\":[\"VanderWeele & Ding (2017)\",\"Ding & VanderWeele (2016)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.evalue\",\"id\":\"evalue\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"E-value\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Any design\"],\"title\":\"E-value scale\",\"worry_when\":\"The conversion's own conditions fail -- a common outcome treated as rare, or a mean difference on an outcome that is far from bell-shaped.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:evalue_scale\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"evalue_scale\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The E-value is defined for ratios of risks, so anything measured on another scale has to be converted first -- and the conversion is an approximation.\",\"title\":\"E-value scale\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aevent_study> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Event study" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:event_study\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.event_study\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"event_study\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The post-period coefficients trace out how the effect develops -- immediate, growing, fading. The pre-period coefficients are the closest thing to evidence about parallel trends: if the groups were already diverging, the design is in trouble. Read the pre-period as a picture rather than as a test. A wide, flat pre-period with large standard errors is not evidence of parallel trends; it is evidence you cannot tell.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"One coefficient per period relative to treatment, with the period before treatment set to zero.\",\"plain_language\":\"One coefficient per period relative to treatment, with the period before treatment set to zero.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.chaisemartin\",\"id\":\"did.chaisemartin\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"An estimator built for panels where treatment switches on and off, which the imputation and cohort estimators assume never happens.\",\"title\":\"de Chaisemartin & D'Haultfoeuille\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.dr_did\",\"id\":\"did.dr_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One before-and-after with covariates, using an outcome model and a comparison-group model together so either one can carry it.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust DiD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.event_study\",\"id\":\"did.event_study\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Plot the effect period by period around adoption, so you can see whether the gap opened when the policy arrived or long before it.\",\"title\":\"Event study (leads and lags)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.gardner_2s\",\"id\":\"did.gardner_2s\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Strip out unit and period effects using untreated cells only, then regress what is left on the policy.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage DiD (Gardner / Wooldridge-Mundlak)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.goodman_bacon\",\"id\":\"did.goodman_bacon\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Open up a two-way fixed effects estimate and show every two-group comparison inside it, with the weight each one carries -- including the ones that use already-treated units as controls.\",\"title\":\"Goodman-Bacon decomposition\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.matched\",\"id\":\"did.matched\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Match treated to comparison units on pre-period characteristics first, then difference.\",\"title\":\"Matched difference-in-differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.sun_abraham\",\"id\":\"did.sun_abraham\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Give every cohort its own event-study path, then average the paths by cohort size instead of letting the regression choose the weights.\",\"title\":\"Sun & Abraham event study\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.synthdid_r\",\"id\":\"did.synthdid_r\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight both units and periods, then take a difference-in-differences -- halfway between synthetic control and DiD.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences (synthdid)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.triple_diff\",\"id\":\"did.triple_diff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the treated-versus-comparison difference for the group the policy could reach with the same difference for a group inside the same units that it could not.\",\"title\":\"Triple differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twfe\",\"id\":\"did.twfe\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One regression with unit and period fixed effects. Familiar, fast, and not an average treatment effect when units adopt at different dates.\",\"title\":\"Two-way fixed effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twoway_2x2\",\"id\":\"did.twoway_2x2\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Two groups, two periods: compare how much the treated group changed with how much the comparison group changed over the same window.\",\"title\":\"Difference-in-differences (2x2)\"}],\"references\":[\"Sun & Abraham (2021)\",\"Borusyak, Jaravel & Spiess (2024)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.pre_trends\",\"id\":\"pre_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Pre-trend test\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_anticipation\",\"explains\":\"Units did not change behaviour before treatment arrived because they knew it was coming.\",\"id\":\"no_anticipation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No anticipation\",\"worry_when\":\"There was a public announcement well before the policy started, or the pre-period coefficients move in the period immediately before adoption.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"id\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"What survives when parallel trends is allowed to fail by a stated amount.\",\"title\":\"Honest DiD (Rambachan-Roth)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Event study\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period coefficients drifting away from zero, especially starting at the announcement date; effects that appear entirely in the first period and vanish; wildly different dynamics across cohorts.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:event_study\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"event_study\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"One coefficient per period relative to treatment, with the period before treatment set to zero.\",\"title\":\"Event study\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aextreme_confounder> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Confounder strength needed to explain the estimate" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:extreme_confounder\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.extreme_confounder\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"extreme_confounder\",\"in_more_depth\":\"This is the worst case for one half of the problem and therefore a useful bound. By granting the hidden variable complete command of the treatment assignment, the question reduces to a single quantity: how strongly it would have to relate to the outcome to move the estimate to zero. If that remaining requirement is large, the finding survives even the most adverse assumption about selection into treatment. If it is small, then even a mild relationship with the outcome would suffice, and the estimate should be described as fragile.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Suppose a hidden variable explained everything left unexplained about who was treated. How much of the outcome would it still need to explain?\",\"plain_language\":\"Suppose a hidden variable explained everything left unexplained about who was treated. How much of the outcome would it still need to explain?\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"id\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"How strong would an unmeasured confounder have to be -- compared with the covariates you already have -- to explain the estimate away?\",\"title\":\"Cinelli-Hazlett sensitivity (partial R2)\"}],\"references\":[\"Cinelli & Hazlett (2020), Making sense of sensitivity\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.robustness_value\",\"id\":\"robustness_value\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Robustness value\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.unobserved_confounder\",\"id\":\"unobserved_confounder\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Simulated unobserved confounder\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Any design\"],\"title\":\"Confounder strength needed to explain the estimate\",\"worry_when\":\"The share required is small enough that an ordinary unmeasured variable could reach it.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:extreme_confounder\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"extreme_confounder\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Suppose a hidden variable explained everything left unexplained about who was treated. How much of the outcome would it still need to explain?\",\"title\":\"Confounder strength needed to explain the estimate\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Afirst_stage> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "First stage" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:first_stage\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.first_stage\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"first_stage\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A significant first stage is not the same as a strong one. What matters for the reliability of the second stage is the effective F statistic, which accounts for heteroskedasticity and clustering; the naive F can be comfortably large while the effective F is not. Below roughly ten, two-stage least squares is biased towards ordinary least squares and its confidence interval undercovers badly. The honest report there is a weak-instrument-robust confidence set.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How much the instrument actually moves the treatment, shown as a picture and as an F statistic.\",\"plain_language\":\"How much the instrument actually moves the treatment, shown as a picture and as an F statistic.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.mte\",\"id\":\"iv.mte\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Trace the effect as a function of how reluctant a unit is to be treated, instead of reporting one local average.\",\"title\":\"Marginal treatment effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.shift_share\",\"id\":\"iv.shift_share\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Combine national shocks with local exposure shares to build an instrument for local changes.\",\"title\":\"Shift-share (Bartik) instruments\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.weak_robust\",\"id\":\"iv.weak_robust\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the set of effects the data cannot reject, which stays honest however weak the instrument is.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust confidence set\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.fuzzy\",\"id\":\"rd.fuzzy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Divide the jump in the outcome by the jump in take-up: the effect for the people the cutoff actually moved.\",\"title\":\"Fuzzy RD (crossing the cutoff only nudged people in)\"}],\"references\":[\"Olea & Pflueger (2013)\",\"Andrews, Stock & Sun (2019)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.relevance\",\"explains\":\"The instrument actually moves treatment, and moves it enough that the resulting estimate is not mostly noise.\",\"id\":\"relevance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Instrument relevance\",\"worry_when\":\"An effective F below about 10; a first-stage coefficient that is substantively tiny even if statistically significant; many instruments.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.weak_iv_set\",\"id\":\"weak_iv_set\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust set\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.weak_robust\",\"id\":\"iv.weak_robust\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the set of effects the data cannot reject, which stays honest however weak the instrument is.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust confidence set\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.relevance\",\"explains\":\"The instrument actually moves treatment, and moves it enough that the resulting estimate is not mostly noise.\",\"id\":\"relevance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Instrument relevance\",\"worry_when\":\"An effective F below about 10; a first-stage coefficient that is substantively tiny even if statistically significant; many instruments.\"},\"tags\":[\"Something else pushed people in\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"],\"title\":\"First stage\",\"worry_when\":\"An effective F below about ten; a first-stage effect that is statistically significant but substantively negligible; many instruments each doing very little.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:first_stage\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"first_stage\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How much the instrument actually moves the treatment, shown as a picture and as an F statistic.\",\"title\":\"First stage\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Afold_stability> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Stability across folds" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:fold_stability\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.fold_stability\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"fold_stability\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A spread far larger than the standard error means the estimate is being driven by which rows happened to fall in which fold, and the reported precision is fiction. Re-running with a different seed and comparing is the cheapest follow-up.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The estimate computed within each cross-fitting fold. They should scatter around the overall estimate by roughly its standard error.\",\"plain_language\":\"The estimate computed within each cross-fitting fold. They should scatter around the overall estimate by roughly its standard error.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_plr\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_plr\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Predict the outcome and the treatment from the confounders, then regress what is left of one on what is left of the other.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (partially linear)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.policy_tree\",\"id\":\"ml.policy_tree\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn a short, readable rule for who to treat, then value that rule on data it never saw.\",\"title\":\"Policy tree (who should be treated)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.aipw\",\"id\":\"obs.aipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use a treatment model and an outcome model together; it is right if either one is.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust (AIPW)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.superlearner\",\"id\":\"obs.superlearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Combine several candidate learners by cross-validated weights instead of betting on one.\",\"title\":\"Super Learner nuisance models\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.cross_fitting\",\"id\":\"concept.cross_fitting\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the nuisance models on one part of the data and apply them to another, so a flexible learner cannot fit its own noise into the effect estimate.\",\"title\":\"Cross-fitting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.nuisance_rmse\",\"id\":\"nuisance_rmse\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Nuisance models by fold\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Stability across folds\",\"worry_when\":\"Fold estimates spanning several standard errors, or changing sign.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:fold_stability\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"fold_stability\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The estimate computed within each cross-fitting fold. They should scatter around the overall estimate by roughly its standard error.\",\"title\":\"Stability across folds\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aforest_honesty> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Honest splitting scheme" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:forest_honesty\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.forest_honesty\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"forest_honesty\",\"in_more_depth\":\"If the same rows both choose a split and estimate the effect on either side of it, the effect will look large -- the split was chosen because it looked large. Honest forests avoid that by dividing each tree's sample in two: one half draws the boundaries, the other half fills in the numbers, and no unit contributes to both jobs in the same tree. Each unit's own predicted effect then comes only from trees that never saw it. The cost is sample size, and this panel reports what that cost was: leaves too sparse to estimate, and units with few trees to average over, both push the per-person predictions back towards the overall average.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Confirmation that each tree used one set of rows to decide where the subgroups are and a different set to estimate the effect inside them.\",\"plain_language\":\"Confirmation that each tree used one set of rows to decide where the subgroups are and a different set to estimate the effect inside them.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"}],\"references\":[\"Wager & Athey (2018), Estimation and inference of heterogeneous treatment effects\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.honest_splitting\",\"id\":\"concept.honest_splitting\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Use one half of the data to decide where the subgroups are and the other half to estimate their effects.\",\"title\":\"Honest splitting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cate_calibration\",\"id\":\"cate_calibration\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"CATE calibration\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Honest splitting scheme\",\"worry_when\":\"A large share of leaves falling back to the overall estimate, or units with very few trees that never saw them; the per-person effects are then mostly the average wearing a disguise.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:forest_honesty\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"forest_honesty\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Confirmation that each tree used one set of rows to decide where the subgroups are and a different set to estimate the effect inside them.\",\"title\":\"Honest splitting scheme\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Agroup_time_att> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Group-time effects" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:group_time_att\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.group_time_att\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"group_time_att\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Rather than fitting one coefficient to everything, these estimators compute a separate comparison for each cohort and each period, always against units that are not treated in either of the two periods being compared. Every headline number -- overall, by cohort, by time since adoption -- is then an average of these blocks with stated weights. The advantage is that no already-treated unit is ever used as a control, so the negative-weight problem cannot arise. The cost is that some blocks rest on very little data, and the table lets you see which.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"One effect for each adoption cohort in each period, which is the building block every modern staggered estimator aggregates.\",\"plain_language\":\"One effect for each adoption cohort in each period, which is the building block every modern staggered estimator aggregates.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.dr_did\",\"id\":\"did.dr_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One before-and-after with covariates, using an outcome model and a comparison-group model together so either one can carry it.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust DiD\"}],\"references\":[\"Callaway & Sant'Anna (2021)\",\"Sun & Abraham (2021)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cohort_att\",\"id\":\"cohort_att\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Cohort effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.not_yet_treated\",\"id\":\"concept.not_yet_treated\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Units that do adopt the policy, but later. While they are still waiting they can serve as the comparison group for units that adopted earlier.\",\"title\":\"Not-yet-treated comparisons\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Group-time effects\",\"worry_when\":\"A block resting on one or two treated units, or on a very small comparison group; the average inherits that fragility without showing it.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:group_time_att\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"group_time_att\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"One effect for each adoption cohort in each period, which is the building block every modern staggered estimator aggregates.\",\"title\":\"Group-time effects\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Ahonest_did> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Honest DiD breakdown value" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:honest_did\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.honest_did\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"honest_did\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A pre-trend test that fails to reject is weak evidence, because such tests have little power exactly when a violation would matter. The alternative is to allow the trends to differ by some amount after treatment, widen the interval accordingly, and report the largest violation the conclusion can absorb. That number is called a breakdown value. Expressed in relative terms, it says how big a post-treatment violation would have to be compared with the wobbles already visible before treatment. It is a much more useful summary than a yes-or-no test, and it is still an assumption -- about the class of violations considered, not about their absence.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Rather than testing whether trends were parallel, this asks how far from parallel they could have been before your conclusion changes.\",\"plain_language\":\"Rather than testing whether trends were parallel, this asks how far from parallel they could have been before your conclusion changes.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"id\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"What survives when parallel trends is allowed to fail by a stated amount.\",\"title\":\"Honest DiD (Rambachan-Roth)\"}],\"references\":[\"Rambachan & Roth (2023)\",\"Roth (2022), Pretest with caution\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"id\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"What survives when parallel trends is allowed to fail by a stated amount.\",\"title\":\"Honest DiD (Rambachan-Roth)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.pre_trend_size\",\"id\":\"pre_trend_size\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Size of the pre-trend\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.pre_trends\",\"id\":\"pre_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Pre-trend test\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Honest DiD breakdown value\",\"worry_when\":\"A breakdown value below one, which means the conclusion needs the post-treatment violation to be smaller than the ones you can already see in the pre-period.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:honest_did\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"honest_did\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Rather than testing whether trends were parallel, this asks how far from parallel they could have been before your conclusion changes.\",\"title\":\"Honest DiD breakdown value\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aicc_design_effect> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Intra-cluster correlation and design effect" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:icc_design_effect\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.icc_design_effect\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"icc_design_effect\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Two pupils in one school resemble each other more than two pupils picked at random from the country, and the intracluster correlation puts a number between zero and one on that resemblance. Combine it with the cluster sizes and you get the design effect: the factor by which the sample behaves smaller than it looks. A design effect of four means a thousand rows carry about two hundred and fifty observations' worth of information. This is not a fault to be repaired. It is the price of assigning treatment to groups, and it should have been in the sample-size calculation before the study ran.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How alike people are inside the same cluster, and how many independent observations your rows are really worth once that similarity is counted.\",\"plain_language\":\"How alike people are inside the same cluster, and how many independent observations your rows are really worth once that similarity is counted.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cluster\",\"id\":\"rct.cluster\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When whole schools, clinics or villages were assigned, the number of clusters is the sample size that matters.\",\"title\":\"Cluster-randomised trial\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.clustering\",\"id\":\"concept.clustering\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"When treatment was assigned to whole groups -- states, schools, clinics -- the number of independent observations is closer to the number of groups than the number of rows.\",\"title\":\"Clustering\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cluster_structure\",\"id\":\"cluster_structure\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Cluster structure\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.effective_sample_size\",\"id\":\"concept.effective_sample_size\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"How many observations your weighted comparison is really worth. Almost always smaller than the number of rows, sometimes dramatically.\",\"title\":\"Effective sample size\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"],\"title\":\"Intra-cluster correlation and design effect\",\"worry_when\":\"A design effect well above two, or an effective sample close to the number of clusters; in the latter case analyse the cluster averages and say so.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:icc_design_effect\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"icc_design_effect\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How alike people are inside the same cluster, and how many independent observations your rows are really worth once that similarity is counted.\",\"title\":\"Intra-cluster correlation and design effect\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aimputation_fit> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Untreated-cell model fit" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:imputation_fit\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.imputation_fit\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"imputation_fit\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Imputation estimators work by fitting a model of what units do when untreated -- unit differences plus period differences -- on the untreated observations only, then predicting what each treated unit would have done and subtracting. The effect is the average of those differences. That makes the fit of the comparison model the load-bearing element. If the untreated cells are not well described by unit and period effects alone, the predicted counterfactual carries error that lands entirely in the estimate.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How well the model of untreated behaviour describes the periods where nobody was treated, since everything else is built on it.\",\"plain_language\":\"How well the model of untreated behaviour describes the periods where nobody was treated, since everything else is built on it.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.gardner_2s\",\"id\":\"did.gardner_2s\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Strip out unit and period effects using untreated cells only, then regress what is left on the policy.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage DiD (Gardner / Wooldridge-Mundlak)\"}],\"references\":[\"Borusyak, Jaravel & Spiess (2024), Revisiting event-study designs\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.raw_means\",\"id\":\"raw_means\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Raw means by cohort and period\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Untreated-cell model fit\",\"worry_when\":\"A residual spread that is large next to the effect you are estimating; the imputed counterfactual then carries most of the uncertainty.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:imputation_fit\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"imputation_fit\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How well the model of untreated behaviour describes the periods where nobody was treated, since everything else is built on it.\",\"title\":\"Untreated-cell model fit\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Ainfluence_spread> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Influence-function spread" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:influence_spread\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.influence_spread\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"influence_spread\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Most modern estimators can be written as an average of one number per unit, and the standard error is the spread of those numbers divided by the sample size. That makes the contributions readable: you can see whether the answer is an average over many people or a handful of unusual rows with everybody else along for the ride. A single dominant contribution is an extreme weight wearing different clothes, and it has the same consequence -- the confidence interval assumes an averaging that did not really happen.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Each unit's contribution to the estimate, and how much of the total the largest single contribution is.\",\"plain_language\":\"Each unit's contribution to the estimate, and how much of the total the largest single contribution is.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Regress backwards from the final outcome, then correct with the treatment probabilities: right if either set of models is right.\",\"title\":\"Sequential doubly robust (ICE g-formula with augmentation)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.weights\",\"id\":\"diagnostic.weights\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A histogram of the weight each row was given. A long tail means a few rows are carrying the answer.\",\"title\":\"The weights themselves\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.ess\",\"id\":\"ess\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Effective sample size\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.effective_sample_size\",\"id\":\"concept.effective_sample_size\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"How many observations your weighted comparison is really worth. Almost always smaller than the number of rows, sometimes dramatically.\",\"title\":\"Effective sample size\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\"],\"title\":\"Influence-function spread\",\"worry_when\":\"One unit carrying a large share of the total contribution; removing it would visibly move the estimate.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:influence_spread\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"influence_spread\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Each unit's contribution to the estimate, and how much of the total the largest single contribution is.\",\"title\":\"Influence-function spread\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aits_autocorrelation> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Residual autocorrelation" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:its_autocorrelation\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_autocorrelation\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"its_autocorrelation\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Monthly and quarterly series are almost always autocorrelated: a busy month is followed by a busy month. Ignoring it does not bias the estimate much but it shrinks the standard error, sometimes by a factor of two or three, which turns noise into a finding. The autocorrelation function of the residuals and a Ljung-Box test are the standard checks. When they fire, use a HAC standard error or model the error process explicitly, and say which in the report.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Whether the residuals of the segmented regression are still correlated over time. If they are, ordinary standard errors are far too small.\",\"plain_language\":\"Whether the residuals of the segmented regression are still correlated over time. If they are, ordinary standard errors are far too small.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.arima\",\"id\":\"its.arima\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the memory in the series itself, then ask how big a step, pulse or ramp the data need at the intervention date.\",\"title\":\"ARIMA with an intervention\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.causal_impact\",\"id\":\"its.causal_impact\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the counterfactual series with a structural time-series model and control series, and report a posterior rather than an interval.\",\"title\":\"Bayesian structural time series (CausalImpact)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.controlled\",\"id\":\"its.controlled\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The same interruption, measured against a series the intervention did not touch, so a shock that hit everybody is not booked as your effect.\",\"title\":\"Controlled ITS\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.segmented\",\"id\":\"its.segmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the trend before the interruption, project it forward, and measure the jump and the change of direction after it.\",\"title\":\"Segmented regression\"}],\"references\":[\"Bernal, Cummins & Gasparrini (2017)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.segmented\",\"id\":\"its.segmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the trend before the interruption, project it forward, and measure the jump and the change of direction after it.\",\"title\":\"Segmented regression\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},\"tags\":[\"A shock in a single series\"],\"title\":\"Residual autocorrelation\",\"worry_when\":\"Large residual autocorrelation at lag one, or at the seasonal lag, that survives the fitted model.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:its_autocorrelation\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"its_autocorrelation\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Whether the residuals of the segmented regression are still correlated over time. If they are, ordinary standard errors are far too small.\",\"title\":\"Residual autocorrelation\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aits_control_shock> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Control series at the interruption" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:its_control_shock\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_control_shock\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"its_control_shock\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The weakness of a single interrupted series is that anything else happening at the same time is indistinguishable from the policy. A control series -- a neighbouring region, an unaffected condition, an untargeted product -- turns that from an assumption into something partly checkable. If the control jumps at the same date in the same direction, the change was not the policy. If the two series were already drifting apart beforehand, the control was not a good stand-in to begin with, and the comparison inherits the drift.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A second series that the policy did not touch, to see whether the change happened everywhere.\",\"plain_language\":\"A second series that the policy did not touch, to see whether the change happened everywhere.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.controlled\",\"id\":\"its.controlled\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The same interruption, measured against a series the intervention did not touch, so a shock that hit everybody is not booked as your effect.\",\"title\":\"Controlled ITS\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.controlled\",\"id\":\"its.controlled\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The same interruption, measured against a series the intervention did not touch, so a shock that hit everybody is not booked as your effect.\",\"title\":\"Controlled ITS\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_cointerventions\",\"explains\":\"Nothing else changed at the same moment that also moves the outcome.\",\"id\":\"no_cointerventions\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No co-interventions at the interruption\",\"worry_when\":\"A busy policy period; a national event at the same date; a change in how the outcome was measured or coded at the same date.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_series\",\"id\":\"its_series\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"The series and the interruption\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_cointerventions\",\"explains\":\"Nothing else changed at the same moment that also moves the outcome.\",\"id\":\"no_cointerventions\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No co-interventions at the interruption\",\"worry_when\":\"A busy policy period; a national event at the same date; a change in how the outcome was measured or coded at the same date.\"},\"tags\":[\"A shock in a single series\"],\"title\":\"Control series at the interruption\",\"worry_when\":\"The control jumping at the interruption in the same direction as the treated series, or the two series diverging before the interruption.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:its_control_shock\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"its_control_shock\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A second series that the policy did not touch, to see whether the change happened everywhere.\",\"title\":\"Control series at the interruption\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aits_counterfactual> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Counterfactual projection" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:its_counterfactual\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_counterfactual\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"its_counterfactual\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The effect is the distance between what happened and this projected line, so the projection deserves as much scrutiny as the estimate. Reading it is mostly a matter of plausibility: does the line stay inside the range the series has ever occupied, and does the fitted pattern describe the pre-period well enough to be worth extending? The band widens the further out it goes, and it prices in only the uncertainty in the fitted line. It does not price in the risk that the shape itself is wrong, which is usually the larger risk.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The line the series would have followed if the earlier pattern had simply continued, with a band around it.\",\"plain_language\":\"The line the series would have followed if the earlier pattern had simply continued, with a band around it.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.arima\",\"id\":\"its.arima\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the memory in the series itself, then ask how big a step, pulse or ramp the data need at the intervention date.\",\"title\":\"ARIMA with an intervention\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.causal_impact\",\"id\":\"its.causal_impact\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the counterfactual series with a structural time-series model and control series, and report a posterior rather than an interval.\",\"title\":\"Bayesian structural time series (CausalImpact)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.controlled\",\"id\":\"its.controlled\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The same interruption, measured against a series the intervention did not touch, so a shock that hit everybody is not booked as your effect.\",\"title\":\"Controlled ITS\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.segmented\",\"id\":\"its.segmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the trend before the interruption, project it forward, and measure the jump and the change of direction after it.\",\"title\":\"Segmented regression\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_pre_period\",\"id\":\"its_pre_period\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Pre-period length\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_cointerventions\",\"explains\":\"Nothing else changed at the same moment that also moves the outcome.\",\"id\":\"no_cointerventions\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No co-interventions at the interruption\",\"worry_when\":\"A busy policy period; a national event at the same date; a change in how the outcome was measured or coded at the same date.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},\"tags\":[\"A shock in a single series\"],\"title\":\"Counterfactual projection\",\"worry_when\":\"A projection that leaves the range the series has ever occupied; a band so wide that any conclusion fits inside it; a pre-period fit that already misses systematically.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:its_counterfactual\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"its_counterfactual\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The line the series would have followed if the earlier pattern had simply continued, with a band around it.\",\"title\":\"Counterfactual projection\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aits_model_choice> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Model choice" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:its_model_choice\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_model_choice\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"its_model_choice\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Time-series models differ in how they treat the series' memory -- whether today's value depends on yesterday's, on the surprise in yesterday's, or on the trend needing removal before either. Automatic selection picks the model that scores best on a fit criterion, and often several candidates score almost the same. When they do, the choice is close to arbitrary and it can matter for the estimate. The honest report says which was picked, what the alternatives were, and whether the conclusion holds under them too.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Which time-series model was fitted, what it was chosen against, and how close the runners-up were.\",\"plain_language\":\"Which time-series model was fitted, what it was chosen against, and how close the runners-up were.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.arima\",\"id\":\"its.arima\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the memory in the series itself, then ask how big a step, pulse or ramp the data need at the intervention date.\",\"title\":\"ARIMA with an intervention\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.arima\",\"id\":\"its.arima\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the memory in the series itself, then ask how big a step, pulse or ramp the data need at the intervention date.\",\"title\":\"ARIMA with an intervention\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_autocorrelation\",\"id\":\"its_autocorrelation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Residual autocorrelation\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},\"tags\":[\"A shock in a single series\"],\"title\":\"Model choice\",\"worry_when\":\"Two very different models scoring almost the same; a chosen model that still leaves correlation in the residuals; differencing forced rather than tested.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:its_model_choice\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"its_model_choice\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Which time-series model was fitted, what it was chosen against, and how close the runners-up were.\",\"title\":\"Model choice\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aits_pre_period> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Pre-period length" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:its_pre_period\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_pre_period\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"its_pre_period\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The counterfactual in this design is the continuation of the earlier trend, so the earlier trend has to be estimable. With six monthly points, one unusual month tilts the whole projected line, and the projection is what the effect is measured against. There is no fixed minimum, but eight to twelve points is a common floor and more is better -- particularly when the series has seasonality, which needs whole cycles rather than points to identify.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many periods there are before the change, and whether they are enough to establish what was going to happen anyway.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many periods there are before the change, and whether they are enough to establish what was going to happen anyway.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.arima\",\"id\":\"its.arima\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the memory in the series itself, then ask how big a step, pulse or ramp the data need at the intervention date.\",\"title\":\"ARIMA with an intervention\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.controlled\",\"id\":\"its.controlled\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The same interruption, measured against a series the intervention did not touch, so a shock that hit everybody is not booked as your effect.\",\"title\":\"Controlled ITS\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.segmented\",\"id\":\"its.segmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the trend before the interruption, project it forward, and measure the jump and the change of direction after it.\",\"title\":\"Segmented regression\"}],\"references\":[\"Bernal, Cummins & Gasparrini (2017), Interrupted time series regression\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_series\",\"id\":\"its_series\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"The series and the interruption\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_counterfactual\",\"id\":\"its_counterfactual\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Counterfactual projection\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},\"tags\":[\"A shock in a single series\"],\"title\":\"Pre-period length\",\"worry_when\":\"Fewer than about eight to twelve pre-period points; or a pre-period so short that a single unusual value tilts the projected line.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:its_pre_period\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"its_pre_period\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many periods there are before the change, and whether they are enough to establish what was going to happen anyway.\",\"title\":\"Pre-period length\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aits_seasonality> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Seasonality" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:its_seasonality\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_seasonality\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"its_seasonality\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Admissions rise in winter, sales rise in December, and a policy introduced in January will look effective if the season is not accounted for. The check looks for a cycle left over in what the model could not explain, and it matters most when the interruption falls near a seasonal peak or trough. Over-fitting seasonality has its own cost: terms for twelve months in a short series consume the degrees of freedom the effect needs. The aim is terms the data can actually support.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Whether a repeating annual or weekly cycle remains in the series after the model has done its work.\",\"plain_language\":\"Whether a repeating annual or weekly cycle remains in the series after the model has done its work.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.arima\",\"id\":\"its.arima\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the memory in the series itself, then ask how big a step, pulse or ramp the data need at the intervention date.\",\"title\":\"ARIMA with an intervention\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.causal_impact\",\"id\":\"its.causal_impact\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the counterfactual series with a structural time-series model and control series, and report a posterior rather than an interval.\",\"title\":\"Bayesian structural time series (CausalImpact)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.controlled\",\"id\":\"its.controlled\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The same interruption, measured against a series the intervention did not touch, so a shock that hit everybody is not booked as your effect.\",\"title\":\"Controlled ITS\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.segmented\",\"id\":\"its.segmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the trend before the interruption, project it forward, and measure the jump and the change of direction after it.\",\"title\":\"Segmented regression\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_autocorrelation\",\"id\":\"its_autocorrelation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Residual autocorrelation\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.segmented\",\"id\":\"its.segmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the trend before the interruption, project it forward, and measure the jump and the change of direction after it.\",\"title\":\"Segmented regression\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},\"tags\":[\"A shock in a single series\"],\"title\":\"Seasonality\",\"worry_when\":\"A cycle surviving in the residuals, particularly one whose peak sits near the interruption; or seasonal terms fitted that the data cannot support.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:its_seasonality\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"its_seasonality\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Whether a repeating annual or weekly cycle remains in the series after the model has done its work.\",\"title\":\"Seasonality\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aits_series> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "The series and the interruption" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:its_series\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_series\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"its_series\",\"in_more_depth\":\"An interrupted time series has one group and no comparison, so the entire argument is that the series was going one way and then changed. Whether that is visible here decides how much the modelling can be trusted; a model can always fit a step, and this is where you find out whether there is one to fit. Three things are worth checking. That the visible change sits at the marked date rather than before or after it. That the series had not already turned. And that the step is larger than the ordinary period-to-period movement of the series.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The outcome plotted over time with a line at the date of the change. Read this before anything else.\",\"plain_language\":\"The outcome plotted over time with a line at the date of the change. Read this before anything else.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.arima\",\"id\":\"its.arima\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the memory in the series itself, then ask how big a step, pulse or ramp the data need at the intervention date.\",\"title\":\"ARIMA with an intervention\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.causal_impact\",\"id\":\"its.causal_impact\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the counterfactual series with a structural time-series model and control series, and report a posterior rather than an interval.\",\"title\":\"Bayesian structural time series (CausalImpact)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.controlled\",\"id\":\"its.controlled\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The same interruption, measured against a series the intervention did not touch, so a shock that hit everybody is not booked as your effect.\",\"title\":\"Controlled ITS\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.segmented\",\"id\":\"its.segmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the trend before the interruption, project it forward, and measure the jump and the change of direction after it.\",\"title\":\"Segmented regression\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.its_pre_period\",\"id\":\"its_pre_period\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Pre-period length\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.segmented\",\"id\":\"its.segmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the trend before the interruption, project it forward, and measure the jump and the change of direction after it.\",\"title\":\"Segmented regression\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},\"tags\":[\"A shock in a single series\"],\"title\":\"The series and the interruption\",\"worry_when\":\"The visible jump is at a different date from the marker; the series already turned before the interruption; the level shift is the size of the ordinary month-to-month wobble.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:its_series\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"its_series\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The outcome plotted over time with a line at the date of the change. Read this before anything else.\",\"title\":\"The series and the interruption\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aleave_one_out> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Leave one unit out" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:leave_one_out\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.leave_one_out\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"leave_one_out\",\"in_more_depth\":\"An estimate that survives dropping any single unit is about a population. An estimate that collapses when one row leaves is about that row. This check does not need a model of anything; it just re-runs the analysis with each unit held out and plots where the answers land. It matters most when the number of units doing the work is small: a synthetic control built from a handful of donors, a panel with few clusters, or a matched analysis where one comparison unit was reused many times.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Drop one unit -- or one cluster, or one donor -- at a time, refit, and see how far the estimate travels.\",\"plain_language\":\"Drop one unit -- or one cluster, or one donor -- at a time, refit, and see how far the estimate travels.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.leave_one_out\",\"id\":\"probe.leave_one_out\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Drop one cluster, cohort or donor at a time and see whether any single one is carrying the result.\",\"title\":\"Leave one out\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_loo\",\"id\":\"sc_loo\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Leave one donor out\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.influence_spread\",\"id\":\"influence_spread\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Influence-function spread\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.subset_stability\",\"id\":\"subset_stability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Stability on random subsets\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"A shock in a single series\",\"Something else pushed people in\",\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Leave one unit out\",\"worry_when\":\"One omission that changes the answer materially. The estimate is then about that unit, not about the population it claims to describe.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:leave_one_out\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"leave_one_out\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Drop one unit -- or one cluster, or one donor -- at a time, refit, and see how far the estimate travels.\",\"title\":\"Leave one unit out\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Alevel_jump_at_kink> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Level jump at a kink" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:level_jump_at_kink\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.level_jump_at_kink\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"level_jump_at_kink\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Where a benefit formula changes its rate at a threshold, the design reads the change in slope as the effect. That reading requires the level itself to be continuous: if crossing the threshold also delivers a lump sum, or switches on some other entitlement, then a step and a bend are both present and attributing everything to the bend is wrong. The test fits a step in the same window. Finding one does not invalidate the data; it means the threshold does two things, and both should be estimated.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A kink design assumes the rule changes the slope and not the level. This fits a step at the same threshold to check.\",\"plain_language\":\"A kink design assumes the rule changes the slope and not the level. This fits a step at the same threshold to check.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.kink\",\"id\":\"rd.kink\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Where a formula changes slope rather than switching on, measure the change in slope of the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Regression kink (the rule bends, it does not switch)\"}],\"references\":[\"Card, Lee, Pei & Weber (2015), Inference on causal effects in a generalized regression kink design\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rd.level_jump\",\"id\":\"diagnostic.rd.level_jump\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The estimated step in the level at the threshold, with its interval, beside the bend the design is about.\",\"title\":\"The step at the kink, drawn\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.kink\",\"id\":\"rd.kink\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Where a formula changes slope rather than switching on, measure the change in slope of the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Regression kink (the rule bends, it does not switch)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rd_plot\",\"id\":\"rd_plot\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Binned scatter at the cutoff\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},\"tags\":[\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"],\"title\":\"Level jump at a kink\",\"worry_when\":\"A clear step at the threshold. The rule then changes the level as well as the slope, and the slope change alone is not the whole story.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:level_jump_at_kink\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"level_jump_at_kink\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A kink design assumes the rule changes the slope and not the level. This fits a step at the same threshold to check.\",\"title\":\"Level jump at a kink\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Alinear_form> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Linearity of the outcome model" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:linear_form\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.linear_form\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"linear_form\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A marginal structural model reports the parameter of a model you chose -- commonly, that each additional period of treatment adds the same amount. If that is right, the model is a compact and efficient summary. If it is wrong, the number is the parameter of a wrong model rather than the contrast you wanted, and it will not agree with methods that make no such assumption. Refitting with a separate term for each treatment level is the direct check. When the two disagree, the linear form is carrying the estimate rather than describing the data.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Whether the answer is being read off the data or produced by the shape the model was told to assume.\",\"plain_language\":\"Whether the answer is being read off the data or produced by the shape the model was told to assume.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"id\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"How strong would an unmeasured confounder have to be -- compared with the covariates you already have -- to explain the estimate away?\",\"title\":\"Cinelli-Hazlett sensitivity (partial R2)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.msm_iptw\",\"id\":\"long.msm_iptw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight each person-period so the treated and untreated histories look alike, then fit one simple model to the reweighted data.\",\"title\":\"Marginal structural model (time-varying weights)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.gformula\",\"id\":\"long.gformula\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model each period in turn, then simulate the world where everyone followed the strategy you name.\",\"title\":\"Parametric g-formula\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Any design\"],\"title\":\"Linearity of the outcome model\",\"worry_when\":\"A gap between the model-based number and the version that assumes no shape; or a suspicion that several confounders act together in ways a simple model cannot express.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:linear_form\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"linear_form\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Whether the answer is being read off the data or produced by the shape the model was told to assume.\",\"title\":\"Linearity of the outcome model\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Alink_heterogeneity> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Conditional treatment-by-modifier interaction" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:link_heterogeneity\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.link_heterogeneity\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"link_heterogeneity\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The link scale is the conditional mean for OLS, log odds for binary logistic models, log mean or rate for Poisson models, and category log odds versus the reference for multinomial models. Equality on that conditional scale need not imply equality of marginal risk, mean or survival contrasts. A coefficient test depends on the specified functional form and supports no claim that intervening on the modifier causes the treatment effect to change.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Test all fitted treatment-by-modifier coefficients jointly while retaining their main effects.\",\"plain_language\":\"Test all fitted treatment-by-modifier coefficients jointly while retaining their main effects.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.binary\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.binary\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Logistic interactions plus separate standardized risk-difference, risk-ratio or marginal odds-ratio heterogeneity tests.\",\"title\":\"Binary outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.categorical\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.categorical\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Multinomial treatment interactions and a joint test of category-probability contrasts; preserves categories without assigning numeric scores.\",\"title\":\"Nominal and ordinal outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.continuous\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.continuous\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"OLS treatment-by-baseline-modifier interactions with HC3 or cluster sandwich inference, direct mean-difference tests and Holm pairwise comparisons.\",\"title\":\"Continuous outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.count\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.count\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Poisson conditional-mean interactions with robust sandwich inference; compare standardized count or fixed-exposure rate contrasts.\",\"title\":\"Count and rate outcome heterogeneity\"}],\"references\":[\"Knol and VanderWeele (2012), Recommendations for presenting analyses of effect modification and interaction\",\"statsmodels joint Wald test documentation\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.outcome_heterogeneity\",\"id\":\"outcome_heterogeneity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Direct heterogeneity test on the selected outcome scale\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.heterogeneity_scale\",\"id\":\"concept.heterogeneity_scale\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Absolute and relative treatment contrasts can vary differently across the same baseline groups.\",\"title\":\"Heterogeneity depends on the effect scale\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Conditional treatment-by-modifier interaction\",\"worry_when\":null},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:link_heterogeneity\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"link_heterogeneity\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Test all fitted treatment-by-modifier coefficients jointly while retaining their main effects.\",\"title\":\"Conditional treatment-by-modifier interaction\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Alove> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Covariate balance (Love plot)" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:love\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.love\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"love\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Named after Thomas Love. Each covariate contributes one point before adjustment and one after, so you can see at a glance whether matching or weighting achieved what it promised. The dashed line at 0.1 is a convention. What matters is whether a residual imbalance sits on a variable that strongly predicts the outcome; a large remaining difference on an irrelevant covariate is harmless, and a small one on a powerful predictor is not. Variance ratios are reported alongside: two groups can have the same mean and very different spread, and matching on means alone will not show it.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Absolute standardised differences for every covariate, before and after adjustment, on one axis.\",\"plain_language\":\"Absolute standardised differences for every covariate, before and after adjustment, on one axis.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.matched\",\"id\":\"did.matched\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Match treated to comparison units on pre-period characteristics first, then difference.\",\"title\":\"Matched difference-in-differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.aipw\",\"id\":\"obs.aipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use a treatment model and an outcome model together; it is right if either one is.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust (AIPW)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.cem\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.cem\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Bin the covariates, then compare only inside bins that contain both arms.\",\"title\":\"Coarsened exact matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.genetic\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.genetic\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Search over distance-metric weights until balance is as good as it can get, instead of trusting one metric.\",\"title\":\"Genetic matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.nn\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.nn\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Pair each treated unit with the most similar untreated unit, then compare within pairs.\",\"title\":\"Nearest-neighbour matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.optimal\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.optimal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Choose the whole set of matches at once to minimise total distance, rather than greedily one treated unit at a time.\",\"title\":\"Optimal and full matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.outcome_regression\",\"id\":\"obs.outcome_regression\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the outcome, then ask what would have happened if everyone had been treated, and if nobody had.\",\"title\":\"Outcome regression (g-computation)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.cbps\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.cbps\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the treatment model so that it balances covariates, rather than fitting it to predict treatment and hoping balance follows.\",\"title\":\"Covariate balancing propensity score\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.entropy\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.entropy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Find the weights closest to equal that make the covariate means match exactly.\",\"title\":\"Entropy balancing\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.ipw\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.ipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight the sample so the treated and untreated groups look alike on the measured confounders.\",\"title\":\"Propensity weighting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.twang\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.twang\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow a boosted model for the propensity score, stopping at the iteration that balances covariates best.\",\"title\":\"Boosted propensity scores (twang)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.transport.generalize\",\"id\":\"transport.generalize\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Take an effect estimated in one population and ask what it would be in another with a different mix of characteristics.\",\"title\":\"Transportability and generalisability\"}],\"references\":[\"Austin (2009), Balance diagnostics for comparing the distribution of baseline covariates\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.standardised_difference\",\"id\":\"concept.standardised_difference\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The gap between groups on a covariate, divided by a pooled standard deviation, so covariates on different scales can be compared on one axis.\",\"title\":\"Standardised mean difference\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.ess\",\"id\":\"ess\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Effective sample size\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Covariate balance (Love plot)\",\"worry_when\":\"Any covariate still far from zero after adjustment; a variance ratio far from one; balance achieved only by discarding much of the sample.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:love\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"love\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Absolute standardised differences for every covariate, before and after adjustment, on one axis.\",\"title\":\"Covariate balance (Love plot)\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Amanipulation> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Density at the cutoff" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:manipulation\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.manipulation\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"manipulation\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The design works because units just either side of the threshold are otherwise alike -- an accident of a decimal place decided who got the programme. That stops being true if people could see the threshold and move themselves across it, because the ones who managed it are precisely those who wanted the treatment most or had the resources to arrange it. The check is a comparison of how densely packed the units are on each side. A smooth transition does not prove nobody manipulated anything; a visible cliff is strong evidence that somebody did.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Count how many units sit just below the threshold and just above it. A cliff between the two means somebody was steering.\",\"plain_language\":\"Count how many units sit just below the threshold and just above it. A cliff between the two means somebody was steering.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.density_test\",\"id\":\"rd.density_test\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare how many units sit just below the threshold with how many sit just above.\",\"title\":\"Did people sort across the cutoff?\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.fuzzy\",\"id\":\"rd.fuzzy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Divide the jump in the outcome by the jump in take-up: the effect for the people the cutoff actually moved.\",\"title\":\"Fuzzy RD (crossing the cutoff only nudged people in)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.kink\",\"id\":\"rd.kink\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Where a formula changes slope rather than switching on, measure the change in slope of the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Regression kink (the rule bends, it does not switch)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_linear\",\"id\":\"rd.local_linear\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a line either side of the threshold and measure the step between them.\",\"title\":\"Sharp RD at the cutoff\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_randomization\",\"id\":\"rd.local_randomization\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat a narrow window around the cutoff as a small randomised experiment rather than fitting a curve through it.\",\"title\":\"Local randomisation RD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.multi_cutoff\",\"id\":\"rd.multi_cutoff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Several thresholds, or a boundary on a map, pooled into one design.\",\"title\":\"Multi-cutoff and geographic RD\"}],\"references\":[\"McCrary (2008)\",\"Cattaneo, Jansson & Ma (2020), Simple local polynomial density estimators\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_manipulation\",\"explains\":\"People could not precisely control which side of the cutoff they landed on.\",\"id\":\"no_manipulation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No manipulation of the running variable\",\"worry_when\":\"A visible spike or gap in the density at the cutoff; covariates that jump; a cutoff that people knew about and had time to respond to.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rd_density\",\"id\":\"diagnostic.rd_density\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The number of units just either side of the threshold. A cliff means somebody was sorting.\",\"title\":\"Density at the cutoff\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.density_test\",\"id\":\"rd.density_test\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare how many units sit just below the threshold with how many sit just above.\",\"title\":\"Did people sort across the cutoff?\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_manipulation\",\"explains\":\"People could not precisely control which side of the cutoff they landed on.\",\"id\":\"no_manipulation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No manipulation of the running variable\",\"worry_when\":\"A visible spike or gap in the density at the cutoff; covariates that jump; a cutoff that people knew about and had time to respond to.\"},\"tags\":[\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"],\"title\":\"Density at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Many more units just on the winning side of the cutoff than just below it; scores that pile up on round numbers; a threshold that was public and easy to game.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:manipulation\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"manipulation\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Count how many units sit just below the threshold and just above it. A cliff between the two means somebody was steering.\",\"title\":\"Density at the cutoff\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Amediation_decomposition> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Total = direct + indirect" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:mediation_decomposition\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.mediation_decomposition\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"mediation_decomposition\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The split is arithmetic: the two parts add to the total by construction, so their adding up is not evidence that either is right. Both pieces rest on the assumption that nothing unmeasured confounds the mediator and the outcome, which is usually harder to defend than the assumption about the treatment itself -- randomising a programme does not randomise how much of it people take up. The proportion mediated is the most-quoted and least-reliable summary here. It is a ratio of two estimates, it is unstable when the total effect is small, and it is not a share of anything at all when the two parts have opposite signs.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The total effect split into the part that runs through the mediator and the part that does not.\",\"plain_language\":\"The total effect split into the part that runs through the mediator and the part that does not.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.controlled_direct\",\"id\":\"med.controlled_direct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"What the treatment would still do if the mediator were fixed at one value for everybody.\",\"title\":\"Controlled direct effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.interventional\",\"id\":\"med.interventional\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Shift the mediator to the distribution it would have in the population, rather than to each person's own unobservable counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Interventional direct and indirect effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.natural_effects\",\"id\":\"med.natural_effects\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Split the total effect into the part that travelled through the mediator and the part that did not.\",\"title\":\"Natural direct and indirect effects\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.sequential_ignorability\",\"explains\":\"No unmeasured confounding of the treatment-outcome relationship, and none of the mediator-outcome relationship either, even after conditioning on treatment.\",\"id\":\"sequential_ignorability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Sequential ignorability\",\"worry_when\":\"There is a variable affected by treatment that also confounds the mediator-outcome relationship. Under that structure the natural effects are not identified at all.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.mediator_exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Whatever determines how much of the mediator a person gets is unrelated to their outcome, once you condition on the treatment and the covariates you measured.\",\"id\":\"mediator_exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No unmeasured confounding of the mediator and the outcome\",\"worry_when\":\"The mediator is a behaviour or a choice; there is a variable affected by treatment that also confounds the mediator and the outcome, in which case the natural pathway effects are not identified at all.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.cross_world\",\"id\":\"concept.cross_world\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A condition relating outcomes under treatment and mediator values that no single person could ever experience together. Not testable, even in principle.\",\"title\":\"Cross-world assumptions\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.natural_effects\",\"id\":\"med.natural_effects\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Split the total effect into the part that travelled through the mediator and the part that did not.\",\"title\":\"Natural direct and indirect effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.sequential_ignorability\",\"explains\":\"No unmeasured confounding of the treatment-outcome relationship, and none of the mediator-outcome relationship either, even after conditioning on treatment.\",\"id\":\"sequential_ignorability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Sequential ignorability\",\"worry_when\":\"There is a variable affected by treatment that also confounds the mediator-outcome relationship. Under that structure the natural effects are not identified at all.\"},\"tags\":[\"How it worked (pathways)\"],\"title\":\"Total = direct + indirect\",\"worry_when\":\"A proportion mediated quoted on its own; a total effect close to zero; two components with opposite signs.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:mediation_decomposition\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"mediation_decomposition\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The total effect split into the part that runs through the mediator and the part that does not.\",\"title\":\"Total = direct + indirect\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Ametalearner_plug_in> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Plug-in bias of the meta-learner" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:metalearner_plug_in\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.metalearner_plug_in\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"metalearner_plug_in\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Flexible learners are deliberately cautious: they pull predictions towards the middle to avoid fitting noise. That shrinkage is what makes them good at ranking people, and it is exactly what makes their average a poor estimate of the average effect -- the bias does not cancel out when you average, and no standard error is available for it. So the two numbers do different jobs. Use the per-person predictions to decide who to prioritise; use the headline, which is built to have a valid interval, to say how large the effect is.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Averaging a machine-learning model's per-person predictions gives a different number from the headline estimate, and the headline is the one with an honest interval.\",\"plain_language\":\"Averaging a machine-learning model's per-person predictions gives a different number from the headline estimate, and the headline is the one with an honest interval.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"CATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cate\",\"id\":\"CATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"How does the effect vary from person to person, or place to place?\",\"title\":\"Conditional average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cate_distribution\",\"id\":\"cate_distribution\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"CATE distribution\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Plug-in bias of the meta-learner\",\"worry_when\":\"The two numbers are far apart, which measures how much the learner shrank its predictions; quoting the plug-in average as a magnitude would then be wrong.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:metalearner_plug_in\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"metalearner_plug_in\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Averaging a machine-learning model's per-person predictions gives a different number from the headline estimate, and the headline is the one with an honest interval.\",\"title\":\"Plug-in bias of the meta-learner\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Amodel_reliance> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "How much the model is doing" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:model_reliance\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.model_reliance\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"model_reliance\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Matching and weighting fail loudly when there is nobody to compare with: units get dropped, or weights explode. A regression just predicts. The number it returns for a treated unit with no comparable control is the model's opinion, and nothing in the coefficient table marks it as such. That is why this product pairs outcome regression with the overlap plot rather than presenting it alone.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A reminder that outcome regression extrapolates silently wherever the groups do not overlap.\",\"plain_language\":\"A reminder that outcome regression extrapolates silently wherever the groups do not overlap.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.outcome_regression\",\"id\":\"obs.outcome_regression\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the outcome, then ask what would have happened if everyone had been treated, and if nobody had.\",\"title\":\"Outcome regression (g-computation)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.outcome_regression\",\"id\":\"obs.outcome_regression\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the outcome, then ask what would have happened if everyone had been treated, and if nobody had.\",\"title\":\"Outcome regression (g-computation)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.overlap\",\"id\":\"overlap\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Overlap between the arms\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"How much the model is doing\",\"worry_when\":null},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:model_reliance\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"model_reliance\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A reminder that outcome regression extrapolates silently wherever the groups do not overlap.\",\"title\":\"How much the model is doing\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Amultiple_mediators> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Multiple mediators" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:multiple_mediators\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.multiple_mediators\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"multiple_mediators\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Shifting each mediator on its own and then shifting them together gives three numbers, and the single-mediator effects will generally not sum to the joint one. The difference is what the mediators do jointly -- through interacting with each other, or because one causes another -- and it cannot be assigned to either of them. Pathway effects therefore partition the total only when the mediators neither interact nor cause one another, which is a strong condition and rarely stated when such tables are published.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"With more than one mediator, the separate pathway effects do not add up to the total, and the shortfall is meaningful.\",\"plain_language\":\"With more than one mediator, the separate pathway effects do not add up to the total, and the shortfall is meaningful.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.interventional\",\"id\":\"med.interventional\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Shift the mediator to the distribution it would have in the population, rather than to each person's own unobservable counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Interventional direct and indirect effects\"}],\"references\":[\"VanderWeele & Vansteelandt (2014), Mediation analysis with multiple mediators\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.sequential_ignorability\",\"explains\":\"No unmeasured confounding of the treatment-outcome relationship, and none of the mediator-outcome relationship either, even after conditioning on treatment.\",\"id\":\"sequential_ignorability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Sequential ignorability\",\"worry_when\":\"There is a variable affected by treatment that also confounds the mediator-outcome relationship. Under that structure the natural effects are not identified at all.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.mediation_decomposition\",\"id\":\"mediation_decomposition\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Total = direct + indirect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.interventional\",\"id\":\"med.interventional\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Shift the mediator to the distribution it would have in the population, rather than to each person's own unobservable counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Interventional direct and indirect effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.sequential_ignorability\",\"explains\":\"No unmeasured confounding of the treatment-outcome relationship, and none of the mediator-outcome relationship either, even after conditioning on treatment.\",\"id\":\"sequential_ignorability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Sequential ignorability\",\"worry_when\":\"There is a variable affected by treatment that also confounds the mediator-outcome relationship. Under that structure the natural effects are not identified at all.\"},\"tags\":[\"How it worked (pathways)\"],\"title\":\"Multiple mediators\",\"worry_when\":\"Path-specific effects presented as if they partitioned the total; a large difference between the sum of the separate effects and the joint one.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:multiple_mediators\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"multiple_mediators\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"With more than one mediator, the separate pathway effects do not add up to the total, and the shortfall is meaningful.\",\"title\":\"Multiple mediators\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3An_matched> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Match quality" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:n_matched\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.n_matched\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"n_matched\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Matching without replacement forces bad pairs when the comparison pool is thin; matching with replacement fixes that and concentrates the estimate on a few units, which the effective sample size then reveals. Treated units that found no partner within the caliper leave the analysis, and that changes the estimand: it is now the effect for the treated units that could be matched.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many treated units found a partner, how many comparison units were used, and how often the same one was reused.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many treated units found a partner, how many comparison units were used, and how often the same one was reused.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.matched\",\"id\":\"did.matched\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Match treated to comparison units on pre-period characteristics first, then difference.\",\"title\":\"Matched difference-in-differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.genetic\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.genetic\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Search over distance-metric weights until balance is as good as it can get, instead of trusting one metric.\",\"title\":\"Genetic matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.nn\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.nn\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Pair each treated unit with the most similar untreated unit, then compare within pairs.\",\"title\":\"Nearest-neighbour matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.optimal\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.optimal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Choose the whole set of matches at once to minimise total distance, rather than greedily one treated unit at a time.\",\"title\":\"Optimal and full matching\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.nn\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.nn\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Pair each treated unit with the most similar untreated unit, then compare within pairs.\",\"title\":\"Nearest-neighbour matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.consort\",\"id\":\"concept.consort\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A step-by-step record of how the imported rows became the analysis sample, with a reason for every row that left.\",\"title\":\"The sample flow\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.love\",\"id\":\"love\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Covariate balance (Love plot)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Match quality\",\"worry_when\":\"Many unmatched treated units; one comparison unit reused a large number of times; matches that are close on the score but far apart on the covariates.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:n_matched\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"n_matched\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many treated units found a partner, how many comparison units were used, and how often the same one was reused.\",\"title\":\"Match quality\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Anatural_course> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Natural-course calibration" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:natural_course\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.natural_course\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"natural_course\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Simulation-based methods answer questions by generating fictional worlds from fitted models -- everybody treated, nobody treated, treated under some rule. The one world that can be checked is the one that already happened: let the simulation draw treatment from the fitted model instead of setting it by hand, and the result should look like the observed data. This is the only check on the models these methods depend on. Passing it does not make the intervened worlds correct, since those extrapolate beyond what was observed, but failing it means the machinery is misdescribing even the world it was fitted to.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Simulate the world as it actually was and check that the simulation reproduces the data it was built from.\",\"plain_language\":\"Simulate the world as it actually was and check that the simulation reproduces the data it was built from.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.gformula\",\"id\":\"long.gformula\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model each period in turn, then simulate the world where everyone followed the strategy you name.\",\"title\":\"Parametric g-formula\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.lmtp\",\"id\":\"long.lmtp\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate the effect of shifting a continuous or multi-valued treatment, rather than of setting everyone to one value.\",\"title\":\"Longitudinal modified treatment policies (lmtp)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle_survival\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle_survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Time-to-event outcomes with time-varying treatment and censoring, targeted at each time.\",\"title\":\"Longitudinal TMLE for survival outcomes\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.gformula\",\"id\":\"long.gformula\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model each period in turn, then simulate the world where everyone followed the strategy you name.\",\"title\":\"Parametric g-formula\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.strategy_paths\",\"id\":\"strategy_paths\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Treatment strategies compared\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_time_varying_confounding\",\"explains\":\"At every period, the things that drive the treatment decision and also affect the outcome are measured -- and measured before that decision.\",\"id\":\"no_time_varying_confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No unmeasured time-varying confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Clinical measurements taken after the dose was changed; employment status recorded at the end of a period used to explain treatment at its start.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_time_varying_confounding\",\"explains\":\"At every period, the things that drive the treatment decision and also affect the outcome are measured -- and measured before that decision.\",\"id\":\"no_time_varying_confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No unmeasured time-varying confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Clinical measurements taken after the dose was changed; employment status recorded at the end of a period used to explain treatment at its start.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\"],\"title\":\"Natural-course calibration\",\"worry_when\":\"A simulated natural course that does not resemble the data it came from. Nothing else checks these models.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:natural_course\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"natural_course\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Simulate the world as it actually was and check that the simulation reproduces the data it was built from.\",\"title\":\"Natural-course calibration\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Anegative_control> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Negative control" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:negative_control\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.negative_control\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"negative_control\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Choose something that shares the same confounding as your real outcome but that the treatment could not touch -- an event before treatment began, or an unrelated condition. If people who took the treatment also do better on that, the difference cannot be the treatment, so it is whatever else separates the groups. This is more useful than any balance table, because it puts a number on the confounding rather than asserting its absence. A clean negative control is reassuring but not decisive: it rules out one route to a false positive, not all of them.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"An outcome the treatment cannot possibly have affected. Any effect found there is a measurement of your own bias.\",\"plain_language\":\"An outcome the treatment cannot possibly have affected. Any effect found there is a measurement of your own bias.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.negative_control\",\"id\":\"probe.negative_control\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate something that shares the confounding but not the causal path. A non-zero answer is bias you can see.\",\"title\":\"Negative control\"}],\"references\":[\"Lipsitch, Tchetgen Tchetgen & Cohen (2010), Negative controls\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.negative_control\",\"id\":\"concept.negative_control\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A variable that shares your suspected confounding but cannot be affected by the treatment. An effect there is a measurement of your bias.\",\"title\":\"Negative controls\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.negative_control\",\"id\":\"probe.negative_control\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate something that shares the confounding but not the causal path. A non-zero answer is bias you can see.\",\"title\":\"Negative control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"A shock in a single series\",\"Something else pushed people in\",\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Negative control\",\"worry_when\":\"A negative control that is not null. The bias it reveals is not confined to the control -- it is a property of the comparison you are making.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:negative_control\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"negative_control\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"An outcome the treatment cannot possibly have affected. Any effect found there is a measurement of your own bias.\",\"title\":\"Negative control\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Anuisance_rmse> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Nuisance models by fold" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:nuisance_rmse\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.nuisance_rmse\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"nuisance_rmse\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Double machine learning is only as good as its cross-fitting. These numbers tell you whether the nuisance models are learning anything, and whether they are stable: an out-of-fold error that swings wildly means the answer depends on which rows landed where. They are not model-selection criteria. A better-predicting nuisance model does not automatically give a better causal estimate; orthogonality is what protects the estimate, and these diagnostics tell you whether the machinery behind it is behaving.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How well the treatment and outcome models predicted on data they did not see, fold by fold.\",\"plain_language\":\"How well the treatment and outcome models predicted on data they did not see, fold by fold.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_plr\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_plr\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Predict the outcome and the treatment from the confounders, then regress what is left of one on what is left of the other.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (partially linear)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.policy_tree\",\"id\":\"ml.policy_tree\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn a short, readable rule for who to treat, then value that rule on data it never saw.\",\"title\":\"Policy tree (who should be treated)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.aipw\",\"id\":\"obs.aipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use a treatment model and an outcome model together; it is right if either one is.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust (AIPW)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.superlearner\",\"id\":\"obs.superlearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Combine several candidate learners by cross-validated weights instead of betting on one.\",\"title\":\"Super Learner nuisance models\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.cross_fitting\",\"id\":\"concept.cross_fitting\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the nuisance models on one part of the data and apply them to another, so a flexible learner cannot fit its own noise into the effect estimate.\",\"title\":\"Cross-fitting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.fold_stability\",\"id\":\"fold_stability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Stability across folds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Nuisance models by fold\",\"worry_when\":\"Error or discrimination that varies a lot across folds; a treatment model that predicts treatment almost perfectly, which is a positivity problem in disguise.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:nuisance_rmse\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"nuisance_rmse\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How well the treatment and outcome models predicted on data they did not see, fold by fold.\",\"title\":\"Nuisance models by fold\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aoster_assumptions> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Oster's assumptions" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:oster_assumptions\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.oster_assumptions\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"oster_assumptions\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The first is proportional selection: the variables you did not measure are related to the treatment in the same proportion as the ones you did. That would be true if your controls were a random draw from all possible confounders, and they almost never are -- researchers measure what they suspect matters. The second is a value for how well a regression including everything would fit the outcome. It is assumed rather than estimated, usually as a multiple of the fit you achieved, and the bound is sensitive to the choice. Both assumptions are reported here so the bound can be read as the conditional statement it is.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The two assumptions behind the delta figure, stated plainly, because the number is meaningless without them.\",\"plain_language\":\"The two assumptions behind the delta figure, stated plainly, because the number is meaningless without them.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.oster\",\"id\":\"probe.oster\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"How important would unobserved variables have to be, relative to the controls you already have, to drive the coefficient to zero?\",\"title\":\"Oster coefficient stability\"}],\"references\":[\"Oster (2019)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.oster_delta\",\"id\":\"oster_delta\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Oster delta\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Any design\"],\"title\":\"Oster's assumptions\",\"worry_when\":\"Controls that were chosen precisely because they were thought to matter, which is exactly when proportional selection is least plausible.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:oster_assumptions\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"oster_assumptions\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The two assumptions behind the delta figure, stated plainly, because the number is meaningless without them.\",\"title\":\"Oster's assumptions\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aoster_delta> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Oster delta" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:oster_delta\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.oster_delta\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"oster_delta\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The idea starts from something you can watch: how far the estimate moves when you add your control variables, and how much better the model fits once they are in. If adding controls barely moves the estimate but greatly improves the fit, the controls were informative and yet unimportant to the answer -- which is mild evidence that further controls would behave the same way. Delta turns that reasoning into a number. Read it with care. The calculation needs an assumption about how well a regression on absolutely everything would fit, and nothing in your data pins that down; the bound moves a long way as you change it.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How much more important the things you did not measure would have to be than the things you did, to explain the result away.\",\"plain_language\":\"How much more important the things you did not measure would have to be than the things you did, to explain the result away.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.oster\",\"id\":\"probe.oster\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"How important would unobserved variables have to be, relative to the controls you already have, to drive the coefficient to zero?\",\"title\":\"Oster coefficient stability\"}],\"references\":[\"Oster (2019), Unobservable selection and coefficient stability\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.oster_assumptions\",\"id\":\"oster_assumptions\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Oster's assumptions\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.robustness_value\",\"id\":\"robustness_value\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Robustness value\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.oster\",\"id\":\"probe.oster\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"How important would unobserved variables have to be, relative to the controls you already have, to drive the coefficient to zero?\",\"title\":\"Oster coefficient stability\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Any design\"],\"title\":\"Oster delta\",\"worry_when\":\"A delta below 1, which means the conclusion needs the unmeasured variables to matter less than the ones you happened to collect; or a bound that swings as the assumed maximum fit is varied.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:oster_delta\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"oster_delta\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How much more important the things you did not measure would have to be than the things you did, to explain the result away.\",\"title\":\"Oster delta\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aoutcome_distribution> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Outcome distribution" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:outcome_distribution\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.outcome_distribution\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"outcome_distribution\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A difference in means is a summary, and summaries hide things. An outcome piled up at zero, a count with a long tail, or a scale where almost everybody gives the same answer will each produce a mean difference, and in each case the mean difference is a poor description of what the treatment did. Look at the shape first. It tells you whether a straight-line model is a reasonable working description, whether the effect might sit at one end of the distribution rather than in the middle, and whether a transformation would change the question you are answering rather than just the units it is answered in.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The shape of the outcome in each arm, before any model is fitted.\",\"plain_language\":\"The shape of the outcome in each arm, before any model is fitted.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.bounds.manski\",\"id\":\"bounds.manski\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the range the effect must lie in given only what the data can support, without assuming away the missing counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Manski bounds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.missing.mice\",\"id\":\"obs.missing.mice\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Impute the missing values several times, run the analysis in each, and combine -- instead of dropping every row with a gap.\",\"title\":\"Multiple imputation compatible with the analysis\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cace\",\"id\":\"rct.cace\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Not everyone offered a programme takes it. Report what the offer did, and separately what taking it up did for the people it moved.\",\"title\":\"ITT and CACE (noncompliance)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cluster\",\"id\":\"rct.cluster\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When whole schools, clinics or villages were assigned, the number of clusters is the sample size that matters.\",\"title\":\"Cluster-randomised trial\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.diff_means\",\"id\":\"rct.diff_means\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the two arms as they were randomised, and let covariates sharpen the comparison without letting them change the question.\",\"title\":\"Difference in means\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.randomization_inference\",\"id\":\"rct.randomization_inference\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-run the randomisation thousands of times with the outcomes held fixed, and see how unusual what actually happened really is.\",\"title\":\"Randomisation (Fisher) inference\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.stratified\",\"id\":\"rct.stratified\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate inside the blocks the randomisation was run in, then average them the way your estimand says to.\",\"title\":\"Blocked or stratified experiment\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},{\"acronym\":\"ATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ate\",\"id\":\"ATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"If everyone got the programme, how would the average outcome change?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.baseline_balance\",\"id\":\"baseline_balance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Baseline balance\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"],\"title\":\"Outcome distribution\",\"worry_when\":\"A heavy tail; a floor or ceiling that a large share of the sample sits on; two arms with the same mean and visibly different spread.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:outcome_distribution\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"outcome_distribution\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The shape of the outcome in each arm, before any model is fitted.\",\"title\":\"Outcome distribution\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aoutcome_heterogeneity> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Direct heterogeneity test on the selected outcome scale" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:outcome_heterogeneity\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.outcome_heterogeneity\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"outcome_heterogeneity\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The Wald statistic compares an independent vector of between-group contrasts to zero. The tested scale can be a mean or risk difference, log ratio, or category-probability contrast. The null concerns that scale and target population. Singular covariance produces no p-value. Pairwise p-values use Holm adjustment over all displayed subgroup-pair/category comparisons in this run; the intervals remain pointwise. Additional outcomes, models, modifiers and horizons lie outside that family and require a separate analysis plan.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Test equality of the specified subgroup treatment contrasts using their full joint covariance.\",\"plain_language\":\"Test equality of the specified subgroup treatment contrasts using their full joint covariance.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.binary\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.binary\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Logistic interactions plus separate standardized risk-difference, risk-ratio or marginal odds-ratio heterogeneity tests.\",\"title\":\"Binary outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.categorical\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.categorical\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Multinomial treatment interactions and a joint test of category-probability contrasts; preserves categories without assigning numeric scores.\",\"title\":\"Nominal and ordinal outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.continuous\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.continuous\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"OLS treatment-by-baseline-modifier interactions with HC3 or cluster sandwich inference, direct mean-difference tests and Holm pairwise comparisons.\",\"title\":\"Continuous outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.count\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.count\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Poisson conditional-mean interactions with robust sandwich inference; compare standardized count or fixed-exposure rate contrasts.\",\"title\":\"Count and rate outcome heterogeneity\"}],\"references\":[\"Altman and Bland (2003), Interaction revisited: the difference between two estimates\",\"statsmodels joint Wald test documentation\",\"statsmodels multiple-testing documentation\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.link_heterogeneity\",\"id\":\"link_heterogeneity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Conditional treatment-by-modifier interaction\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.heterogeneity_prespecification\",\"id\":\"concept.heterogeneity_prespecification\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Fix the modifier, coding, outcome and tested scale before inspecting effects, and report the complete testing family.\",\"title\":\"Baseline hypotheses, support and multiplicity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Direct heterogeneity test on the selected outcome scale\",\"worry_when\":null},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:outcome_heterogeneity\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"outcome_heterogeneity\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Test equality of the specified subgroup treatment contrasts using their full joint covariance.\",\"title\":\"Direct heterogeneity test on the selected outcome scale\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aoverid> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Overidentification test" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:overid\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.overid\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"overid\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Sargan under homoskedasticity, Hansen's J with robust errors. Rejection says at least one instrument is invalid, or that effects are heterogeneous so different instruments identify different local effects. Failing to reject says the instruments agree. If they are all invalid in the same direction, they will agree perfectly, which is why this test is not evidence for the exclusion restriction.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"With more instruments than endogenous regressors, a check that they agree with each other.\",\"plain_language\":\"With more instruments than endogenous regressors, a check that they agree with each other.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.shift_share\",\"id\":\"iv.shift_share\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Combine national shocks with local exposure shares to build an instrument for local changes.\",\"title\":\"Shift-share (Bartik) instruments\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.weak_robust\",\"id\":\"iv.weak_robust\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the set of effects the data cannot reject, which stays honest however weak the instrument is.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust confidence set\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exclusion\",\"explains\":\"The instrument affects the outcome only through the treatment. On the board, that is the arrow you did not draw.\",\"id\":\"exclusion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exclusion restriction\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is a policy that changed several things at once; it is correlated with geography, time or institutions that have their own effects; it was chosen because it predicted the outcome.\"},{\"acronym\":\"LATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.late\",\"id\":\"LATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who only got it because of the instrument or the cutoff, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Local average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exclusion\",\"explains\":\"The instrument affects the outcome only through the treatment. On the board, that is the arrow you did not draw.\",\"id\":\"exclusion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exclusion restriction\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is a policy that changed several things at once; it is correlated with geography, time or institutions that have their own effects; it was chosen because it predicted the outcome.\"},\"tags\":[\"Something else pushed people in\"],\"title\":\"Overidentification test\",\"worry_when\":\"A rejection; or a comfortable pass being described as validating exclusion.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:overid\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"overid\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"With more instruments than endogenous regressors, a check that they agree with each other.\",\"title\":\"Overidentification test\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aoverlap> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Overlap between the arms" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:overlap\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.overlap\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"overlap\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Fit a model for the probability of being treated given the covariates, then plot the fitted probabilities by arm. If treated units sit at values no control unit reaches, the estimate for those units is produced by extrapolating the outcome model rather than by comparing anything. The summary reports the share of units outside the common support and how many effective control observations survive reweighting. Both matter: a picture can look reasonable while the effective sample has collapsed.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The distribution of the estimated probability of treatment, drawn separately for the treated and the untreated. Where they do not overlap, there is no comparison to make.\",\"plain_language\":\"The distribution of the estimated probability of treatment, drawn separately for the treated and the untreated. Where they do not overlap, there is no comparison to make.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.dr_did\",\"id\":\"did.dr_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One before-and-after with covariates, using an outcome model and a comparison-group model together so either one can carry it.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust DiD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.aipw\",\"id\":\"obs.aipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use a treatment model and an outcome model together; it is right if either one is.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust (AIPW)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.genetic\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.genetic\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Search over distance-metric weights until balance is as good as it can get, instead of trusting one metric.\",\"title\":\"Genetic matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.nn\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.nn\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Pair each treated unit with the most similar untreated unit, then compare within pairs.\",\"title\":\"Nearest-neighbour matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.cbps\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.cbps\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the treatment model so that it balances covariates, rather than fitting it to predict treatment and hoping balance follows.\",\"title\":\"Covariate balancing propensity score\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.ipw\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.ipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight the sample so the treated and untreated groups look alike on the measured confounders.\",\"title\":\"Propensity weighting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.twang\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.twang\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow a boosted model for the propensity score, stopping at the iteration that balances covariates best.\",\"title\":\"Boosted propensity scores (twang)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.transport.generalize\",\"id\":\"transport.generalize\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Take an effect estimated in one population and ask what it would be in another with a different mix of characteristics.\",\"title\":\"Transportability and generalisability\"}],\"references\":[\"Rosenbaum & Rubin (1983)\",\"Crump et al. (2009)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.ess\",\"id\":\"ess\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Effective sample size\"},{\"acronym\":\"ATO\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ato\",\"id\":\"ATO\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For the people where treated and untreated genuinely overlap, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the overlap population\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Overlap between the arms\",\"worry_when\":\"A long stretch where only one arm appears; a treatment model that separates the groups almost perfectly; an effective control sample far below the nominal one.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:overlap\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"overlap\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The distribution of the estimated probability of treatment, drawn separately for the treated and the untreated. Where they do not overlap, there is no comparison to make.\",\"title\":\"Overlap between the arms\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Apair_quality> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Matched-pair quality" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:pair_quality\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.pair_quality\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"pair_quality\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Some sensitivity analyses -- the ones that ask how strong hidden bias would have to be -- are defined on matched pairs. When the main analysis matched differently, with replacement or one treated unit to several controls, the probe has to build its own pairs first, and the number it reports then describes those pairs rather than the estimate on the screen. That is not a bug, but it is a caveat worth carrying into the write-up: the sensitivity statement applies to the paired comparison shown here.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How the matched pairs behind a sensitivity analysis were formed, and whether they are the same pairs the headline estimate used.\",\"plain_language\":\"How the matched pairs behind a sensitivity analysis were formed, and whether they are the same pairs the headline estimate used.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.rosenbaum\",\"id\":\"probe.rosenbaum\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"How unequal could the odds of treatment be inside a matched pair before the conclusion stopped being clear?\",\"title\":\"Rosenbaum bounds\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rosenbaum_bounds\",\"id\":\"rosenbaum_bounds\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Rosenbaum bounds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.n_matched\",\"id\":\"n_matched\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Match quality\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.caliper\",\"id\":\"concept.caliper\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The largest difference you are willing to tolerate inside a matched pair. Beyond it the treated unit is left unmatched rather than paired with somebody unlike them.\",\"title\":\"Caliper\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Matched-pair quality\",\"worry_when\":\"The main analysis used a different matching rule from the probe, so the sensitivity number describes pairs the headline never used.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:pair_quality\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"pair_quality\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How the matched pairs behind a sensitivity analysis were formed, and whether they are the same pairs the headline estimate used.\",\"title\":\"Matched-pair quality\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Apanel_balance> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Panel balance" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:panel_balance\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.panel_balance\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"panel_balance\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Fixed effects compare a unit with itself over time, which works when the unit is there throughout. Units that appear or disappear mid-panel change the composition of the average, and if they do so around the time treatment arrives, the change in the average is partly a change in who is being averaged. That is selection, not bookkeeping, and no fixed effect removes it. The honest responses are to restrict to a balanced set of units and see whether the answer moves, or to model the entry and exit explicitly.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Whether the same units are present in every period, and when the ones that come and go do so.\",\"plain_language\":\"Whether the same units are present in every period, and when the ones that come and go do so.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.chaisemartin\",\"id\":\"did.chaisemartin\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"An estimator built for panels where treatment switches on and off, which the imputation and cohort estimators assume never happens.\",\"title\":\"de Chaisemartin & D'Haultfoeuille\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.dr_did\",\"id\":\"did.dr_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One before-and-after with covariates, using an outcome model and a comparison-group model together so either one can carry it.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust DiD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.event_study\",\"id\":\"did.event_study\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Plot the effect period by period around adoption, so you can see whether the gap opened when the policy arrived or long before it.\",\"title\":\"Event study (leads and lags)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.gardner_2s\",\"id\":\"did.gardner_2s\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Strip out unit and period effects using untreated cells only, then regress what is left on the policy.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage DiD (Gardner / Wooldridge-Mundlak)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.goodman_bacon\",\"id\":\"did.goodman_bacon\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Open up a two-way fixed effects estimate and show every two-group comparison inside it, with the weight each one carries -- including the ones that use already-treated units as controls.\",\"title\":\"Goodman-Bacon decomposition\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.sun_abraham\",\"id\":\"did.sun_abraham\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Give every cohort its own event-study path, then average the paths by cohort size instead of letting the regression choose the weights.\",\"title\":\"Sun & Abraham event study\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.triple_diff\",\"id\":\"did.triple_diff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the treated-versus-comparison difference for the group the policy could reach with the same difference for a group inside the same units that it could not.\",\"title\":\"Triple differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twfe\",\"id\":\"did.twfe\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One regression with unit and period fixed effects. Familiar, fast, and not an average treatment effect when units adopt at different dates.\",\"title\":\"Two-way fixed effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twoway_2x2\",\"id\":\"did.twoway_2x2\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Two groups, two periods: compare how much the treated group changed with how much the comparison group changed over the same window.\",\"title\":\"Difference-in-differences (2x2)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.selection_bias\",\"id\":\"concept.selection_bias\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Who ends up in your analysis sample can create an association all by itself, quite apart from confounding.\",\"title\":\"Selection bias\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.consort\",\"id\":\"concept.consort\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A step-by-step record of how the imported rows became the analysis sample, with a reason for every row that left.\",\"title\":\"The sample flow\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.differential_attrition\",\"id\":\"differential_attrition\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Differential attrition\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Panel balance\",\"worry_when\":\"Units entering or leaving the panel around the time they adopt; the composition, rather than the policy, then moves the outcome.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:panel_balance\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"panel_balance\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Whether the same units are present in every period, and when the ones that come and go do so.\",\"title\":\"Panel balance\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Apermutation_distribution> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Randomisation distribution" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:permutation_distribution\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.permutation_distribution\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"permutation_distribution\",\"in_more_depth\":\"This is inference that borrows nothing from the bell curve. If the treatment did nothing at all to anybody, then any relabelling of who was treated was equally likely, and the estimate you got should look like a typical draw from the reshuffled ones. The p-value is the share of reshuffles that came out at least as extreme as the real result. The reshuffling has to imitate the real assignment -- inside blocks, whole clusters at a time -- or it is answering a question about a study that was never run. Note also what is being tested: the sharp null that treatment changed nothing for anyone, which is a stronger statement than the usual null that the average effect is zero.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Reshuffle the treatment labels the way the study actually assigned them, thousands of times, and see how unusual the real result looks.\",\"plain_language\":\"Reshuffle the treatment labels the way the study actually assigned them, thousands of times, and see how unusual the real result looks.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.randomization_inference\",\"id\":\"rct.randomization_inference\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-run the randomisation thousands of times with the outcomes held fixed, and see how unusual what actually happened really is.\",\"title\":\"Randomisation (Fisher) inference\"}],\"references\":[\"Fisher (1935)\",\"Imbens & Rubin (2015), Causal Inference for Statistics, ch. 5\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.randomisation\",\"explains\":\"Assignment really was by chance, and nothing after assignment broke the comparison -- no differential dropout, no crossover you have not modelled.\",\"id\":\"randomisation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Randomisation held\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropout differs across arms; take-up is far from assignment; the randomiser was a person with discretion.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cluster_structure\",\"id\":\"cluster_structure\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Cluster structure\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.block_structure\",\"id\":\"block_structure\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Block structure\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.randomisation\",\"explains\":\"Assignment really was by chance, and nothing after assignment broke the comparison -- no differential dropout, no crossover you have not modelled.\",\"id\":\"randomisation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Randomisation held\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropout differs across arms; take-up is far from assignment; the randomiser was a person with discretion.\"},\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"],\"title\":\"Randomisation distribution\",\"worry_when\":\"The reshuffled distribution is not centred near zero, which means the scheme being simulated is not the scheme that produced the data; or so few distinct assignments exist that no small p-value is attainable at all.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:permutation_distribution\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"permutation_distribution\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Reshuffle the treatment labels the way the study actually assigned them, thousands of times, and see how unusual the real result looks.\",\"title\":\"Randomisation distribution\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aperson_time> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Person-time and switching" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:person_time\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.person_time\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"person_time\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Methods for treatment that changes over time learn from people whose treatment changes. If almost everybody is either always treated or never treated, there is very little switching to learn from, and the machinery built for time-varying treatment has nothing much to work with. The count of distinct histories is the honest measure of that. It is also the reason these analyses need more data than they appear to: the effective sample is the number of people whose history differs, not the number of person-period rows.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many people, over how many periods, following how many distinct treatment histories.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many people, over how many periods, following how many distinct treatment histories.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.gformula\",\"id\":\"long.gformula\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model each period in turn, then simulate the world where everyone followed the strategy you name.\",\"title\":\"Parametric g-formula\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Regress backwards from the final outcome, then correct with the treatment probabilities: right if either set of models is right.\",\"title\":\"Sequential doubly robust (ICE g-formula with augmentation)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.msm_iptw\",\"id\":\"long.msm_iptw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight each person-period so the treated and untreated histories look alike, then fit one simple model to the reweighted data.\",\"title\":\"Marginal structural model (time-varying weights)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.time_varying_confounding\",\"id\":\"concept.time_varying_confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A variable that is both a consequence of past treatment and a cause of future treatment. Ordinary regression cannot handle it; g-methods can.\",\"title\":\"Time-varying confounding\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.weights_by_period\",\"id\":\"weights_by_period\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Weights by period\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_time_varying_confounding\",\"explains\":\"At every period, the things that drive the treatment decision and also affect the outcome are measured -- and measured before that decision.\",\"id\":\"no_time_varying_confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No unmeasured time-varying confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Clinical measurements taken after the dose was changed; employment status recorded at the end of a period used to explain treatment at its start.\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_time_varying_confounding\",\"explains\":\"At every period, the things that drive the treatment decision and also affect the outcome are measured -- and measured before that decision.\",\"id\":\"no_time_varying_confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No unmeasured time-varying confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Clinical measurements taken after the dose was changed; employment status recorded at the end of a period used to explain treatment at its start.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\"],\"title\":\"Person-time and switching\",\"worry_when\":\"Almost everybody following one of two patterns; there is then little for a time-varying method to learn.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:person_time\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"person_time\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many people, over how many periods, following how many distinct treatment histories.\",\"title\":\"Person-time and switching\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aplacebo_outcome> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Placebo outcome" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:placebo_outcome\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.placebo_outcome\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"placebo_outcome\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The logic is the same as a negative control and the emphasis is different: here the point is to test the design as a whole -- the comparison group, the timing, the adjustment -- rather than to measure confounding specifically. A placebo effect of the same size and sign as the headline is a serious problem, because whatever produced it is presumably also present in the real outcome. A null placebo is worth reporting and is weak evidence: it shows the design did not fail in this particular way.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same analysis on an outcome the treatment should not move. Finding an effect means the design manufactures them.\",\"plain_language\":\"Run the same analysis on an outcome the treatment should not move. Finding an effect means the design manufactures them.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"id\":\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same analysis on something the treatment could not have changed. It should find nothing.\",\"title\":\"Placebo outcome\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.placebo\",\"id\":\"concept.placebo\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same analysis somewhere the effect cannot exist. If you find one, the design is producing effects that are not there.\",\"title\":\"Placebo tests\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"id\":\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same analysis on something the treatment could not have changed. It should find nothing.\",\"title\":\"Placebo outcome\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.placebo_treatment\",\"id\":\"placebo_treatment\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Placebo treatment\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"A shock in a single series\",\"Something else pushed people in\",\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Placebo outcome\",\"worry_when\":\"A placebo effect of the same size and sign as the headline. The design cannot tell the two apart.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:placebo_outcome\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"placebo_outcome\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Run the same analysis on an outcome the treatment should not move. Finding an effect means the design manufactures them.\",\"title\":\"Placebo outcome\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aplacebo_reduced_form> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Placebo reduced form" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:placebo_reduced_form\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.placebo_reduced_form\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"placebo_reduced_form\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The design requires the instrument to reach the outcome only through the treatment. That cannot be proved, but it can be caught failing: pick outcomes the treatment could not plausibly affect, and see whether the instrument moves them. If it does, there is a second route out of the instrument, and the exclusion restriction is not holding. Nothing moving is worth reporting and is not proof. It narrows the space of violations rather than closing it.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Check whether the instrument moves outcomes it has no business moving.\",\"plain_language\":\"Check whether the instrument moves outcomes it has no business moving.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.weak_robust\",\"id\":\"iv.weak_robust\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the set of effects the data cannot reject, which stays honest however weak the instrument is.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust confidence set\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exclusion\",\"explains\":\"The instrument affects the outcome only through the treatment. On the board, that is the arrow you did not draw.\",\"id\":\"exclusion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exclusion restriction\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is a policy that changed several things at once; it is correlated with geography, time or institutions that have their own effects; it was chosen because it predicted the outcome.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.placebo\",\"id\":\"concept.placebo\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same analysis somewhere the effect cannot exist. If you find one, the design is producing effects that are not there.\",\"title\":\"Placebo tests\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.reduced_form\",\"id\":\"reduced_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Reduced form\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exclusion\",\"explains\":\"The instrument affects the outcome only through the treatment. On the board, that is the arrow you did not draw.\",\"id\":\"exclusion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exclusion restriction\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is a policy that changed several things at once; it is correlated with geography, time or institutions that have their own effects; it was chosen because it predicted the outcome.\"},\"tags\":[\"Something else pushed people in\"],\"title\":\"Placebo reduced form\",\"worry_when\":\"Any placebo outcome the instrument does move. Exclusion cannot be established, but it can be caught failing.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:placebo_reduced_form\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"placebo_reduced_form\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Check whether the instrument moves outcomes it has no business moving.\",\"title\":\"Placebo reduced form\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aplacebo_treatment> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Placebo treatment" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:placebo_treatment\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.placebo_treatment\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"placebo_treatment\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A fake treatment has no effect by construction, so anything the analysis reports for it is coming from somewhere else: a trend the design does not handle, a comparison group that was never comparable, or a model flexible enough to fit noise. This is one of the few checks that can demonstrate a problem rather than merely fail to find one, which makes a dirty result here far more informative than a clean one.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Pretend the treatment happened to someone else, or at a date when nothing happened, and see whether the method still finds an effect.\",\"plain_language\":\"Pretend the treatment happened to someone else, or at a date when nothing happened, and see whether the method still finds an effect.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.placebo_treatment\",\"id\":\"probe.placebo_treatment\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Move the policy date earlier, reshuffle who was treated, or swap in a variable that cannot cause the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Placebo treatment\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.placebo\",\"id\":\"concept.placebo\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same analysis somewhere the effect cannot exist. If you find one, the design is producing effects that are not there.\",\"title\":\"Placebo tests\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.placebo_treatment\",\"id\":\"probe.placebo_treatment\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Move the policy date earlier, reshuffle who was treated, or swap in a variable that cannot cause the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Placebo treatment\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_placebo_time\",\"id\":\"sc_placebo_time\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Placebo in time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"A shock in a single series\",\"Something else pushed people in\",\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Placebo treatment\",\"worry_when\":\"A fake treatment that reproduces the headline effect. Whatever the design is picking up, it is not the policy.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:placebo_treatment\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"placebo_treatment\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Pretend the treatment happened to someone else, or at a date when nothing happened, and see whether the method still finds an effect.\",\"title\":\"Placebo treatment\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Apolicy_value> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Honest policy value" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:policy_value\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.policy_value\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"policy_value\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A rule learned from data always looks good on that same data. The only honest valuation holds part of the sample back, applies the finished rule to it, and scores what it would have achieved there. That is the number to quote. The comparison that matters is not against treating nobody but against treating everybody, since blanket treatment is usually simpler to run and easier to defend. If the gain over treating everybody has an interval that includes zero, targeting has not been shown to be worth its complexity. Note the interval holds the fitted models fixed, so it is slightly narrower than the truth.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How much better off you would be following the model's recommendation about who to treat, valued on data the model never saw.\",\"plain_language\":\"How much better off you would be following the model's recommendation about who to treat, valued on data the model never saw.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.policy_tree\",\"id\":\"ml.policy_tree\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn a short, readable rule for who to treat, then value that rule on data it never saw.\",\"title\":\"Policy tree (who should be treated)\"}],\"references\":[\"Athey & Wager (2021), Policy learning with observational data\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.policy_tree\",\"id\":\"ml.policy_tree\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn a short, readable rule for who to treat, then value that rule on data it never saw.\",\"title\":\"Policy tree (who should be treated)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rate\",\"id\":\"rate\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Rank-weighted average treatment effect\"},{\"acronym\":\"CATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cate\",\"id\":\"CATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"How does the effect vary from person to person, or place to place?\",\"title\":\"Conditional average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Honest policy value\",\"worry_when\":\"The held-out value falling well below the in-sample one, which means the rule fitted noise; or a gain over treating everybody whose interval includes zero.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:policy_value\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"policy_value\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How much better off you would be following the model's recommendation about who to treat, valued on data the model never saw.\",\"title\":\"Honest policy value\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Apositivity_by_period> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Positivity by period" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:positivity_by_period\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.positivity_by_period\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"positivity_by_period\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Overlap is harder over time than at a single point, because it has to hold at each period and for each history. Once a patient has been on a drug for three periods, almost everybody like them stays on it, and the analysis is then asking what would have happened had they stopped -- a question the data contains no example of. This panel reports the worst period rather than the average, because positivity has to hold everywhere. A single period where treatment is nearly certain contaminates every quantity computed across the whole follow-up.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Whether, at every time point, people of every kind had a real chance of both receiving and not receiving the treatment.\",\"plain_language\":\"Whether, at every time point, people of every kind had a real chance of both receiving and not receiving the treatment.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.g_estimation\",\"id\":\"long.g_estimation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate the effect of a treatment regime by finding the parameter that removes the association between treatment and the blipped-down outcome.\",\"title\":\"G-estimation of structural nested models\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.gformula\",\"id\":\"long.gformula\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model each period in turn, then simulate the world where everyone followed the strategy you name.\",\"title\":\"Parametric g-formula\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.lmtp\",\"id\":\"long.lmtp\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate the effect of shifting a continuous or multi-valued treatment, rather than of setting everyone to one value.\",\"title\":\"Longitudinal modified treatment policies (lmtp)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Regress backwards from the final outcome, then correct with the treatment probabilities: right if either set of models is right.\",\"title\":\"Sequential doubly robust (ICE g-formula with augmentation)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle_survival\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle_survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Time-to-event outcomes with time-varying treatment and censoring, targeted at each time.\",\"title\":\"Longitudinal TMLE for survival outcomes\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.msm_iptw\",\"id\":\"long.msm_iptw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight each person-period so the treated and untreated histories look alike, then fit one simple model to the reweighted data.\",\"title\":\"Marginal structural model (time-varying weights)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.weights_by_period\",\"id\":\"weights_by_period\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Weights by period\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.regime_support\",\"id\":\"regime_support\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Support for the intervention regime\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\"],\"title\":\"Positivity by period\",\"worry_when\":\"A period where nearly everybody is certain to be treated, or certain not to be; the weights and simulated histories there are extrapolation rather than comparison.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:positivity_by_period\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"positivity_by_period\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Whether, at every time point, people of every kind had a real chance of both receiving and not receiving the treatment.\",\"title\":\"Positivity by period\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Apre_trend_size> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Size of the pre-trend" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:pre_trend_size\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.pre_trend_size\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"pre_trend_size\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Instead of testing whether the pre-period is flat, the honest approach asks how far trends could have differed after treatment before the conclusion changes -- and expresses that as a multiple of the differences you can see before treatment. This row reports the yardstick: the largest one-period movement in the pre-treatment coefficients, and how large it could plausibly be given the noise in estimating it. Everything reported in units of \\\"relative magnitudes\\\" is measured against this number, so a misleadingly small yardstick makes the robustness bounds look better than they are.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The size of the movements in the pre-treatment period, which is the yardstick the robustness bounds are measured against.\",\"plain_language\":\"The size of the movements in the pre-treatment period, which is the yardstick the robustness bounds are measured against.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"id\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"What survives when parallel trends is allowed to fail by a stated amount.\",\"title\":\"Honest DiD (Rambachan-Roth)\"}],\"references\":[\"Rambachan & Roth (2023), A more credible approach to parallel trends\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.honest_did\",\"id\":\"honest_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Honest DiD breakdown value\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.pre_trends\",\"id\":\"pre_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Pre-trend test\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Size of the pre-trend\",\"worry_when\":\"A pre-period that is flat only because it is imprecisely estimated; a tiny measured wobble with wide intervals makes the bounds look reassuring when they are not.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:pre_trend_size\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"pre_trend_size\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The size of the movements in the pre-treatment period, which is the yardstick the robustness bounds are measured against.\",\"title\":\"Size of the pre-trend\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Apre_trends> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Pre-trend test" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:pre_trends\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.pre_trends\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"pre_trends\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Failing to reject is not confirmation. Pre-trend tests often have low power precisely in the situations where a violation would matter, and conditioning the analysis on having passed one distorts the estimates that follow. The better question is the one honest DiD asks: given what the pre-period shows, how far could trends have differed before the conclusion stops holding? That produces a breakdown value rather than a verdict.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A joint test that the pre-treatment coefficients are all zero. Useful, and much weaker evidence than it looks.\",\"plain_language\":\"A joint test that the pre-treatment coefficients are all zero. Useful, and much weaker evidence than it looks.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.chaisemartin\",\"id\":\"did.chaisemartin\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"An estimator built for panels where treatment switches on and off, which the imputation and cohort estimators assume never happens.\",\"title\":\"de Chaisemartin & D'Haultfoeuille\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.dr_did\",\"id\":\"did.dr_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One before-and-after with covariates, using an outcome model and a comparison-group model together so either one can carry it.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust DiD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.event_study\",\"id\":\"did.event_study\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Plot the effect period by period around adoption, so you can see whether the gap opened when the policy arrived or long before it.\",\"title\":\"Event study (leads and lags)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.gardner_2s\",\"id\":\"did.gardner_2s\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Strip out unit and period effects using untreated cells only, then regress what is left on the policy.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage DiD (Gardner / Wooldridge-Mundlak)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.goodman_bacon\",\"id\":\"did.goodman_bacon\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Open up a two-way fixed effects estimate and show every two-group comparison inside it, with the weight each one carries -- including the ones that use already-treated units as controls.\",\"title\":\"Goodman-Bacon decomposition\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.matched\",\"id\":\"did.matched\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Match treated to comparison units on pre-period characteristics first, then difference.\",\"title\":\"Matched difference-in-differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.sun_abraham\",\"id\":\"did.sun_abraham\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Give every cohort its own event-study path, then average the paths by cohort size instead of letting the regression choose the weights.\",\"title\":\"Sun & Abraham event study\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.triple_diff\",\"id\":\"did.triple_diff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the treated-versus-comparison difference for the group the policy could reach with the same difference for a group inside the same units that it could not.\",\"title\":\"Triple differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twfe\",\"id\":\"did.twfe\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One regression with unit and period fixed effects. Familiar, fast, and not an average treatment effect when units adopt at different dates.\",\"title\":\"Two-way fixed effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twoway_2x2\",\"id\":\"did.twoway_2x2\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Two groups, two periods: compare how much the treated group changed with how much the comparison group changed over the same window.\",\"title\":\"Difference-in-differences (2x2)\"}],\"references\":[\"Roth (2022), Pretest with caution\",\"Rambachan & Roth (2023)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"id\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"What survives when parallel trends is allowed to fail by a stated amount.\",\"title\":\"Honest DiD (Rambachan-Roth)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Pre-trend test\",\"worry_when\":\"A test that passes only because the pre-period intervals are enormous; a visible trend that the joint test does not reject.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:pre_trends\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"pre_trends\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A joint test that the pre-treatment coefficients are all zero. Useful, and much weaker evidence than it looks.\",\"title\":\"Pre-trend test\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aprobe_runs> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Probe runs" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:probe_runs\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.probe_runs\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"probe_runs\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A probe such as a placebo test or a specification curve works by running the whole analysis again under changed conditions. Some of those runs fail: a model does not converge, a subsample has nobody in one arm, a bandwidth leaves too few observations. Failures are not neutral and they are not noise. The specifications that could not be estimated are often the demanding ones, so a probe that only reports its successes is quietly reporting the easy half. This row exists so the count is visible next to the conclusion drawn from it.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many of the re-runs a probe needed actually produced a number, and why the rest stopped.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many of the re-runs a probe needed actually produced a number, and why the rest stopped.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.add_unobserved_confounder\",\"id\":\"probe.add_unobserved_confounder\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Simulate a common cause of a stated strength and watch the estimate move; read off how strong one would have to be to explain the result away.\",\"title\":\"Unobserved confounder\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.alternate_spec\",\"id\":\"probe.alternate_spec\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the grid of defensible specifications and sort the answers. The curve is the result, not the one you happened to run.\",\"title\":\"Specification curve\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.leave_one_out\",\"id\":\"probe.leave_one_out\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Drop one cluster, cohort or donor at a time and see whether any single one is carrying the result.\",\"title\":\"Leave one out\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.negative_control\",\"id\":\"probe.negative_control\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate something that shares the confounding but not the causal path. A non-zero answer is bias you can see.\",\"title\":\"Negative control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"id\":\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same analysis on something the treatment could not have changed. It should find nothing.\",\"title\":\"Placebo outcome\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.placebo_treatment\",\"id\":\"probe.placebo_treatment\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Move the policy date earlier, reshuffle who was treated, or swap in a variable that cannot cause the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Placebo treatment\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.random_common_cause\",\"id\":\"probe.random_common_cause\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Add a variable that causes nothing to the adjustment set. The estimate should barely move.\",\"title\":\"Random common cause\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.subset\",\"id\":\"probe.subset\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-estimate on random subsets. The answer should move about as much as losing that much data costs, and no more.\",\"title\":\"Subset stability\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.spec_curve\",\"id\":\"spec_curve\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Specification curve\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.provisional\",\"id\":\"concept.provisional\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A result the product does not think you should trust as it stands. It still computed it; it just will not let the flag disappear.\",\"title\":\"Provisional results\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.comparison\",\"id\":\"probe.comparison\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"One row per re-run of the analysis, on the same axis as the estimate they are probing, so you can see the movement rather than read about it.\",\"title\":\"The probe against the headline\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"A shock in a single series\",\"Something else pushed people in\",\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Probe runs\",\"worry_when\":\"A large share of re-runs failing, especially when the failures cluster in one part of the grid.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:probe_runs\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"probe_runs\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many of the re-runs a probe needed actually produced a number, and why the rest stopped.\",\"title\":\"Probe runs\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Apropensity_clipping> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Propensity clipping" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:propensity_clipping\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.propensity_clipping\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"propensity_clipping\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Inverse-probability weighting divides by the estimated chance of getting the treatment you actually got. When that chance is 0.001 the weight is a thousand, and one row swamps everything. The standard defence is clipping: pull extreme probabilities back to a floor and a ceiling before the division. Clipping stabilises the arithmetic and quietly changes the question. The units at the clip are the ones with no counterpart in the other arm, so keeping them at a fictitious probability is a modelling choice, not a measurement. Whichever way you go, the honest report says how many units were clipped and where the boundaries were set.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many units had an estimated chance of treatment so close to zero or one that the analysis had to trim it back before dividing by it.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many units had an estimated chance of treatment so close to zero or one that the analysis had to trim it back before dividing by it.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_plr\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_plr\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Predict the outcome and the treatment from the confounders, then regress what is left of one on what is left of the other.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (partially linear)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.policy_tree\",\"id\":\"ml.policy_tree\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn a short, readable rule for who to treat, then value that rule on data it never saw.\",\"title\":\"Policy tree (who should be treated)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.aipw\",\"id\":\"obs.aipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use a treatment model and an outcome model together; it is right if either one is.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust (AIPW)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.ipw\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.ipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight the sample so the treated and untreated groups look alike on the measured confounders.\",\"title\":\"Propensity weighting\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.overlap\",\"id\":\"overlap\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Overlap between the arms\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.trim_cost\",\"id\":\"trim_cost\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"What trimming costs\"},{\"acronym\":\"ATO\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ato\",\"id\":\"ATO\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For the people where treated and untreated genuinely overlap, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the overlap population\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Propensity clipping\",\"worry_when\":\"More than a few per cent of units sitting at the clip, or one arm reaching probabilities the other never approaches; the estimate is then carried by extrapolation rather than by comparable units.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:propensity_clipping\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"propensity_clipping\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many units had an estimated chance of treatment so close to zero or one that the analysis had to trim it back before dividing by it.\",\"title\":\"Propensity clipping\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aps_calibration> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Treatment-model calibration" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:ps_calibration\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.ps_calibration\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"ps_calibration\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A propensity score is only useful if it is a probability. Sorting units into deciles of the predicted probability and comparing with the realised treatment share is the simplest check that the model is not badly misspecified. A badly calibrated treatment model makes every weight derived from it suspect, and a doubly robust estimator loses one of its two chances.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Within bands of predicted treatment probability, does the share actually treated match the prediction?\",\"plain_language\":\"Within bands of predicted treatment probability, does the share actually treated match the prediction?\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.aipw\",\"id\":\"obs.aipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use a treatment model and an outcome model together; it is right if either one is.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust (AIPW)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.nn\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.nn\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Pair each treated unit with the most similar untreated unit, then compare within pairs.\",\"title\":\"Nearest-neighbour matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.cbps\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.cbps\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the treatment model so that it balances covariates, rather than fitting it to predict treatment and hoping balance follows.\",\"title\":\"Covariate balancing propensity score\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.ipw\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.ipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight the sample so the treated and untreated groups look alike on the measured confounders.\",\"title\":\"Propensity weighting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.twang\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.twang\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow a boosted model for the propensity score, stopping at the iteration that balances covariates best.\",\"title\":\"Boosted propensity scores (twang)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.overlap\",\"id\":\"overlap\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Overlap between the arms\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.aipw\",\"id\":\"obs.aipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use a treatment model and an outcome model together; it is right if either one is.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust (AIPW)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Treatment-model calibration\",\"worry_when\":\"Deciles where the predicted and realised shares are far apart, especially at the extremes where the weights are largest.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:ps_calibration\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"ps_calibration\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Within bands of predicted treatment probability, does the share actually treated match the prediction?\",\"title\":\"Treatment-model calibration\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Arandom_common_cause> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Random common cause" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:random_common_cause\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.random_common_cause\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"random_common_cause\",\"in_more_depth\":\"This is a check on the machinery rather than on the design. A variable generated at random causes nothing and is caused by nothing, so including it should leave the estimate essentially where it was, give or take a little sampling noise. When the estimate does move, the adjustment is unstable -- typically because the model is straining against thin overlap, or has more parameters than the data can support. Passing this check is reassuring about the arithmetic and says nothing whatever about unmeasured confounding.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Add a variable that is pure noise to the adjustment set. A well-behaved estimate should not move.\",\"plain_language\":\"Add a variable that is pure noise to the adjustment set. A well-behaved estimate should not move.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.random_common_cause\",\"id\":\"probe.random_common_cause\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Add a variable that causes nothing to the adjustment set. The estimate should barely move.\",\"title\":\"Random common cause\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.random_common_cause\",\"id\":\"probe.random_common_cause\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Add a variable that causes nothing to the adjustment set. The estimate should barely move.\",\"title\":\"Random common cause\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.subset_stability\",\"id\":\"subset_stability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Stability on random subsets\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.confounding\",\"id\":\"concept.confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A common cause of both the treatment and the outcome makes the two look related even when the treatment does nothing.\",\"title\":\"Confounding\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"A shock in a single series\",\"Something else pushed people in\",\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Random common cause\",\"worry_when\":\"An estimate that shifts when a variable known to cause nothing is added. That is instability in the adjustment, not evidence about the effect.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:random_common_cause\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"random_common_cause\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Add a variable that is pure noise to the adjustment set. A well-behaved estimate should not move.\",\"title\":\"Random common cause\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Arate> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Rank-weighted average treatment effect" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:rate\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rate\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"rate\",\"in_more_depth\":\"It summarises the curve traced out as you treat progressively more of the population in predicted-benefit order. A RATE indistinguishable from zero means the model's ranking is not usable for targeting, whatever its predictions look like. It has a standard error, which matters: comparing two targeting rules by eye is how people convince themselves of heterogeneity that is not there.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A single number for how much better you would do by treating people in the order the model recommends, rather than at random.\",\"plain_language\":\"A single number for how much better you would do by treating people in the order the model recommends, rather than at random.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_plr\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_plr\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Predict the outcome and the treatment from the confounders, then regress what is left of one on what is left of the other.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (partially linear)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.policy_tree\",\"id\":\"ml.policy_tree\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn a short, readable rule for who to treat, then value that rule on data it never saw.\",\"title\":\"Policy tree (who should be treated)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\"}],\"references\":[\"Yadlowsky et al. (2021), Evaluating treatment prioritization rules\"],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"CATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cate\",\"id\":\"CATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"How does the effect vary from person to person, or place to place?\",\"title\":\"Conditional average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cate_calibration\",\"id\":\"cate_calibration\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"CATE calibration\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.policy_tree\",\"id\":\"ml.policy_tree\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn a short, readable rule for who to treat, then value that rule on data it never saw.\",\"title\":\"Policy tree (who should be treated)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Rank-weighted average treatment effect\",\"worry_when\":null},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:rate\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"rate\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A single number for how much better you would do by treating people in the order the model recommends, rather than at random.\",\"title\":\"Rank-weighted average treatment effect\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Araw_means> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Raw means by cohort and period" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:raw_means\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.raw_means\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"raw_means\",\"in_more_depth\":\"This is the picture the whole design argues about. Difference-in-differences claims that, absent the policy, the treated group's line would have continued parallel to the comparison group's. You cannot check that claim after treatment, but you can look at the lines before it, and you can see whether they were the same shape. Everything the estimator does afterwards is a subtraction on these averages. If the lines look wrong here -- crossing, diverging, one of them jumping in a period when nothing happened to it -- no fixed effect further down the pipeline will repair that.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The average outcome for each group in each period, before any model is fitted.\",\"plain_language\":\"The average outcome for each group in each period, before any model is fitted.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.dr_did\",\"id\":\"did.dr_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One before-and-after with covariates, using an outcome model and a comparison-group model together so either one can carry it.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust DiD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.event_study\",\"id\":\"did.event_study\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Plot the effect period by period around adoption, so you can see whether the gap opened when the policy arrived or long before it.\",\"title\":\"Event study (leads and lags)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.gardner_2s\",\"id\":\"did.gardner_2s\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Strip out unit and period effects using untreated cells only, then regress what is left on the policy.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage DiD (Gardner / Wooldridge-Mundlak)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.goodman_bacon\",\"id\":\"did.goodman_bacon\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Open up a two-way fixed effects estimate and show every two-group comparison inside it, with the weight each one carries -- including the ones that use already-treated units as controls.\",\"title\":\"Goodman-Bacon decomposition\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.sun_abraham\",\"id\":\"did.sun_abraham\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Give every cohort its own event-study path, then average the paths by cohort size instead of letting the regression choose the weights.\",\"title\":\"Sun & Abraham event study\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.triple_diff\",\"id\":\"did.triple_diff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the treated-versus-comparison difference for the group the policy could reach with the same difference for a group inside the same units that it could not.\",\"title\":\"Triple differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twfe\",\"id\":\"did.twfe\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One regression with unit and period fixed effects. Familiar, fast, and not an average treatment effect when units adopt at different dates.\",\"title\":\"Two-way fixed effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twoway_2x2\",\"id\":\"did.twoway_2x2\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Two groups, two periods: compare how much the treated group changed with how much the comparison group changed over the same window.\",\"title\":\"Difference-in-differences (2x2)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.event_study\",\"id\":\"event_study\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Event study\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cell_means\",\"id\":\"cell_means\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Cell means\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Raw means by cohort and period\",\"worry_when\":\"Lines that already diverge before anyone adopts; a group whose line is a different shape entirely; a jump in a period when nothing happened.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:raw_means\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"raw_means\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The average outcome for each group in each period, before any model is fitted.\",\"title\":\"Raw means by cohort and period\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Ard_plot> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Binned scatter at the cutoff" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:rd_plot\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rd_plot\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"rd_plot\",\"in_more_depth\":\"This plot is the design. Before any technical choice has been made, it shows whether something happens at the threshold and whether the relationship away from the threshold is smooth enough for a local fit to be believable. The step you can see in the fitted curve at the cutoff is the estimate; nothing else in the analysis adds a jump that is not visible here. In a kink design, where the rule changes the rate rather than the level, you are looking for a bend rather than a step, and the estimate is the change in slope.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Average outcomes in narrow bands of the score, either side of the threshold, with the fitted curve drawn through them.\",\"plain_language\":\"Average outcomes in narrow bands of the score, either side of the threshold, with the fitted curve drawn through them.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.covariate_balance\",\"id\":\"rd.covariate_balance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same estimator on variables the programme cannot have changed; they should not step at the threshold.\",\"title\":\"Do the covariates jump at the cutoff too?\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.density_test\",\"id\":\"rd.density_test\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare how many units sit just below the threshold with how many sit just above.\",\"title\":\"Did people sort across the cutoff?\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.fuzzy\",\"id\":\"rd.fuzzy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Divide the jump in the outcome by the jump in take-up: the effect for the people the cutoff actually moved.\",\"title\":\"Fuzzy RD (crossing the cutoff only nudged people in)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.kink\",\"id\":\"rd.kink\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Where a formula changes slope rather than switching on, measure the change in slope of the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Regression kink (the rule bends, it does not switch)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_linear\",\"id\":\"rd.local_linear\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a line either side of the threshold and measure the step between them.\",\"title\":\"Sharp RD at the cutoff\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_randomization\",\"id\":\"rd.local_randomization\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat a narrow window around the cutoff as a small randomised experiment rather than fitting a curve through it.\",\"title\":\"Local randomisation RD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.multi_cutoff\",\"id\":\"rd.multi_cutoff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Several thresholds, or a boundary on a map, pooled into one design.\",\"title\":\"Multi-cutoff and geographic RD\"}],\"references\":[\"Calonico, Cattaneo & Titiunik (2015), Optimal data-driven regression discontinuity plots\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"id\":\"bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Bandwidth sensitivity path\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},\"tags\":[\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"],\"title\":\"Binned scatter at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"The binned averages wander in ways the fitted line does not follow; the step at the cutoff is smaller than the wobble in the bands away from it; or very few observations sit in the bands nearest the threshold.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:rd_plot\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"rd_plot\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Average outcomes in narrow bands of the score, either side of the threshold, with the fitted curve drawn through them.\",\"title\":\"Binned scatter at the cutoff\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Areduced_form> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Reduced form" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:reduced_form\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.reduced_form\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"reduced_form\",\"in_more_depth\":\"If the instrument was well assigned, its relationship with the outcome is itself a credible causal effect -- of the encouragement, not of the treatment. That makes it the sturdiest number in an instrumental-variables analysis: no division, no weak-instrument problem, no reliance on how many people the instrument moved. A flat reduced form is decisive in a way the ratio is not. If the encouragement did not move the outcome at all, then either the treatment does nothing or the encouragement did not move treatment, and the ratio of two small numbers will not settle which.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The direct relationship between the instrument and the outcome, before any division. It is the part of the design that rests on the least.\",\"plain_language\":\"The direct relationship between the instrument and the outcome, before any division. It is the part of the design that rests on the least.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.shift_share\",\"id\":\"iv.shift_share\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Combine national shocks with local exposure shares to build an instrument for local changes.\",\"title\":\"Shift-share (Bartik) instruments\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.weak_robust\",\"id\":\"iv.weak_robust\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the set of effects the data cannot reject, which stays honest however weak the instrument is.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust confidence set\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"ITT\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.itt\",\"id\":\"ITT\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For everyone who was offered it, what did the offer do?\",\"title\":\"Intention to treat\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.first_stage\",\"id\":\"first_stage\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"First stage\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exclusion\",\"explains\":\"The instrument affects the outcome only through the treatment. On the board, that is the arrow you did not draw.\",\"id\":\"exclusion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exclusion restriction\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is a policy that changed several things at once; it is correlated with geography, time or institutions that have their own effects; it was chosen because it predicted the outcome.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exclusion\",\"explains\":\"The instrument affects the outcome only through the treatment. On the board, that is the arrow you did not draw.\",\"id\":\"exclusion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exclusion restriction\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is a policy that changed several things at once; it is correlated with geography, time or institutions that have their own effects; it was chosen because it predicted the outcome.\"},\"tags\":[\"Something else pushed people in\"],\"title\":\"Reduced form\",\"worry_when\":\"A flat reduced form combined with a weak first stage, in which case the ratio can land anywhere; or a reduced form that appears only once a particular control is added.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:reduced_form\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"reduced_form\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The direct relationship between the instrument and the outcome, before any division. It is the part of the design that rests on the least.\",\"title\":\"Reduced form\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aregime_support> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Support for the intervention regime" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:regime_support\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.regime_support\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"regime_support\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A question such as \\\"what if everybody had stayed on treatment for five years\\\" is answerable to the extent that somebody did. If almost nobody in the data followed that path, the estimate is the model's opinion about a history it never saw, and the correction terms that are meant to protect against a wrong model are dividing by a very small probability. A low share is not a reason to abandon the question. It is a reason to say plainly that the answer rests on the model rather than on comparable people.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many people actually followed the treatment strategy you are asking about.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many people actually followed the treatment strategy you are asking about.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.lmtp\",\"id\":\"long.lmtp\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate the effect of shifting a continuous or multi-valued treatment, rather than of setting everyone to one value.\",\"title\":\"Longitudinal modified treatment policies (lmtp)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Regress backwards from the final outcome, then correct with the treatment probabilities: right if either set of models is right.\",\"title\":\"Sequential doubly robust (ICE g-formula with augmentation)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.positivity_by_period\",\"id\":\"positivity_by_period\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Positivity by period\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.lmtp\",\"id\":\"long.lmtp\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate the effect of shifting a continuous or multi-valued treatment, rather than of setting everyone to one value.\",\"title\":\"Longitudinal modified treatment policies (lmtp)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\"],\"title\":\"Support for the intervention regime\",\"worry_when\":\"Almost nobody following the strategy being asked about; the estimate is then largely the outcome model's extrapolation.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:regime_support\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"regime_support\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many people actually followed the treatment strategy you are asking about.\",\"title\":\"Support for the intervention regime\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Arobustness_value> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Robustness value" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:robustness_value\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.robustness_value\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"robustness_value\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The robustness value is expressed as a share of the variation left over after your controls: a value of 0.05 means a hidden variable explaining five per cent of the remaining variation in treatment and five per cent of the remaining variation in the outcome would be enough to erase the estimate. Its usefulness comes from the benchmark. The same quantity can be computed for the covariates you did measure, so you can say \\\"an unmeasured variable would have to be twice as informative as household income\\\" instead of quoting an abstract number. Note the calculation assumes the hidden variable enters linearly and on its own, so it is a guide, not a guarantee.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A single number for how strongly an unmeasured variable would have to be related to both the treatment and the outcome to explain the result away.\",\"plain_language\":\"A single number for how strongly an unmeasured variable would have to be related to both the treatment and the outcome to explain the result away.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"id\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"How strong would an unmeasured confounder have to be -- compared with the covariates you already have -- to explain the estimate away?\",\"title\":\"Cinelli-Hazlett sensitivity (partial R2)\"}],\"references\":[\"Cinelli & Hazlett (2020), Making sense of sensitivity\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.extreme_confounder\",\"id\":\"extreme_confounder\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Confounder strength needed to explain the estimate\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.unobserved_confounder\",\"id\":\"unobserved_confounder\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Simulated unobserved confounder\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"id\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"How strong would an unmeasured confounder have to be -- compared with the covariates you already have -- to explain the estimate away?\",\"title\":\"Cinelli-Hazlett sensitivity (partial R2)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"undecided\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"Any design\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Any design\"],\"title\":\"Robustness value\",\"worry_when\":\"The strength required is no more than a covariate you already measured. If a variable you happened to collect is that informative, one you did not collect plausibly is too.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:robustness_value\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"robustness_value\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A single number for how strongly an unmeasured variable would have to be related to both the treatment and the outcome to explain the result away.\",\"title\":\"Robustness value\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Arosenbaum_bounds> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Rosenbaum bounds" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:rosenbaum_bounds\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rosenbaum_bounds\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"rosenbaum_bounds\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Matching pairs units that look alike on the variables you measured. If something you did not measure still made one of them more likely to be treated, the pair is not the fair comparison it appears to be. The bound asks how large that hidden difference in the odds of treatment would have to be -- a factor of 1.2, of 2, of 5 -- before the result could be explained by it. The number is often called Gamma. It is a statement about a comparison, not a discovery about the world: it does not say a hidden variable exists, only what one would have to do. Note too that it assumes the hidden variable affects treatment but is otherwise summarised by that single factor.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"For matched pairs: how unequal two apparently identical units' chances of treatment would have to be before the conclusion stops holding.\",\"plain_language\":\"For matched pairs: how unequal two apparently identical units' chances of treatment would have to be before the conclusion stops holding.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.rosenbaum\",\"id\":\"probe.rosenbaum\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"How unequal could the odds of treatment be inside a matched pair before the conclusion stopped being clear?\",\"title\":\"Rosenbaum bounds\"}],\"references\":[\"Rosenbaum (2002), Observational Studies, ch. 4\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.pair_quality\",\"id\":\"pair_quality\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Matched-pair quality\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.rosenbaum\",\"id\":\"probe.rosenbaum\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"How unequal could the odds of treatment be inside a matched pair before the conclusion stopped being clear?\",\"title\":\"Rosenbaum bounds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.n_matched\",\"id\":\"n_matched\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Match quality\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Rosenbaum bounds\",\"worry_when\":\"A breakdown value close to 1. A value of 1.1 means a hidden variable that shifted the odds of treatment by a tenth would suffice, and unmeasured variables routinely do more than that.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:rosenbaum_bounds\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"rosenbaum_bounds\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"For matched pairs: how unequal two apparently identical units' chances of treatment would have to be before the conclusion stops holding.\",\"title\":\"Rosenbaum bounds\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Arunning_variable_discreteness> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Discreteness of the running variable" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:running_variable_discreteness\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.running_variable_discreteness\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"running_variable_discreteness\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The usual analysis treats the score as continuous, so that units get arbitrarily close to the threshold from both sides. Scores that take only whole numbers, or only a few values, break that picture: there is a nearest value below and a nearest value above, and nothing in between. When that happens the honest treatment is different. Standard errors should allow for units sharing a value, and an analysis that treats the units at the two adjacent values as if they had been randomised fits the situation better than a curve fitted through a handful of points.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many distinct values the score actually takes close to the threshold. A handful means this is a comparison of a few groups.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many distinct values the score actually takes close to the threshold. A handful means this is a comparison of a few groups.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.covariate_balance\",\"id\":\"rd.covariate_balance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same estimator on variables the programme cannot have changed; they should not step at the threshold.\",\"title\":\"Do the covariates jump at the cutoff too?\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.density_test\",\"id\":\"rd.density_test\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare how many units sit just below the threshold with how many sit just above.\",\"title\":\"Did people sort across the cutoff?\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.fuzzy\",\"id\":\"rd.fuzzy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Divide the jump in the outcome by the jump in take-up: the effect for the people the cutoff actually moved.\",\"title\":\"Fuzzy RD (crossing the cutoff only nudged people in)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.kink\",\"id\":\"rd.kink\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Where a formula changes slope rather than switching on, measure the change in slope of the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Regression kink (the rule bends, it does not switch)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_linear\",\"id\":\"rd.local_linear\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a line either side of the threshold and measure the step between them.\",\"title\":\"Sharp RD at the cutoff\"}],\"references\":[\"Kolesar & Rothe (2018), Inference in regression discontinuity designs with a discrete running variable\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rd.discreteness\",\"id\":\"diagnostic.rd.discreteness\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The distribution of the score across its whole range, with the threshold marked, so you can see how it is spaced and where units pile up.\",\"title\":\"Where the score actually sits\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_randomization\",\"id\":\"rd.local_randomization\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat a narrow window around the cutoff as a small randomised experiment rather than fitting a curve through it.\",\"title\":\"Local randomisation RD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.clustering\",\"id\":\"concept.clustering\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"When treatment was assigned to whole groups -- states, schools, clinics -- the number of independent observations is closer to the number of groups than the number of rows.\",\"title\":\"Clustering\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},\"tags\":[\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"],\"title\":\"Discreteness of the running variable\",\"worry_when\":\"Only a few distinct values either side of the threshold inside the window, or a large number of units sitting exactly on the cutoff.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:running_variable_discreteness\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"running_variable_discreteness\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many distinct values the score actually takes close to the threshold. A handful means this is a comparison of a few groups.\",\"title\":\"Discreteness of the running variable\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asc_augmentation> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Size of the augmentation correction" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:sc_augmentation\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_augmentation\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"sc_augmentation\",\"in_more_depth\":\"When no blend of comparison places can match the treated place, the augmented method estimates the leftover imbalance and corrects for it, using a fitted model to bridge the gap. This is a deliberate, well-motivated extrapolation, and its size tells you how much of the final answer comes from the model rather than from the comparison. A small correction means the donors were nearly good enough and the correction is a refinement. A large one means the pool was inadequate and the answer is substantially a model's prediction.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How far the correction for imperfect pre-treatment fit moved the answer.\",\"plain_language\":\"How far the correction for imperfect pre-treatment fit moved the answer.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.augmented\",\"id\":\"sc.augmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When no weighted average of the donors can match the treated place, correct the leftover bias with a ridge model and show the size of the correction.\",\"title\":\"Augmented synthetic control\"}],\"references\":[\"Ben-Michael, Feller & Rothstein (2021), The augmented synthetic control method\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.augmented\",\"id\":\"sc.augmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When no weighted average of the donors can match the treated place, correct the leftover bias with a ridge model and show the size of the correction.\",\"title\":\"Augmented synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_convex_hull\",\"id\":\"sc_convex_hull\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor hull\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_pre_fit\",\"id\":\"sc_pre_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.convex_hull\",\"explains\":\"The treated unit's characteristics can be written as a weighted average of the donors', with weights that do not go negative.\",\"id\":\"convex_hull\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor convex hull\",\"worry_when\":\"The treated unit is at an extreme of the donor distribution on the predictors, or the pre-period gap has a persistent level offset.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.convex_hull\",\"explains\":\"The treated unit's characteristics can be written as a weighted average of the donors', with weights that do not go negative.\",\"id\":\"convex_hull\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor convex hull\",\"worry_when\":\"The treated unit is at an extreme of the donor distribution on the predictors, or the pre-period gap has a persistent level offset.\"},\"tags\":[\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Size of the augmentation correction\",\"worry_when\":\"A correction larger than the original estimate; the donors were then far from the treated unit and the outcome model, not the donor pool, is doing the work.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:sc_augmentation\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"sc_augmentation\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How far the correction for imperfect pre-treatment fit moved the answer.\",\"title\":\"Size of the augmentation correction\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asc_convex_hull> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Treated unit inside the donor hull" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:sc_convex_hull\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_convex_hull\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"sc_convex_hull\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A blend with non-negative weights can only land somewhere between the units being blended. If the treated place is more extreme than every comparison place on some characteristic -- richer than all of them, larger than all of them -- then no blend can match it, and the fit will be systematically off in a direction the weights cannot fix. Classic synthetic control refuses to extrapolate and simply fits as best it can. Methods that add a correction term extrapolate deliberately, which is a reasonable response provided the correction is reported and read, not buried.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Whether any blend of the comparison places could actually reproduce the treated place, or whether it sits outside their range entirely.\",\"plain_language\":\"Whether any blend of the comparison places could actually reproduce the treated place, or whether it sits outside their range entirely.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.abadie\",\"id\":\"sc.abadie\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build a weighted copy of the treated place out of untreated places that looked like it beforehand, then watch the two lines separate.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.augmented\",\"id\":\"sc.augmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When no weighted average of the donors can match the treated place, correct the leftover bias with a ridge model and show the size of the correction.\",\"title\":\"Augmented synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.matrix_completion\",\"id\":\"sc.matrix_completion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat the missing treated-period outcomes as holes in a panel and fill them in with a low-rank model of what the panel would have done.\",\"title\":\"Matrix completion\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.synthdid\",\"id\":\"sc.synthdid\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the comparison units and the pre-treatment periods, then run a difference in differences on the result - a level gap is allowed, a trend gap is not.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences\"}],\"references\":[\"Abadie (2021)\",\"Ben-Michael, Feller & Rothstein (2021)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.convex_hull\",\"explains\":\"The treated unit's characteristics can be written as a weighted average of the donors', with weights that do not go negative.\",\"id\":\"convex_hull\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor convex hull\",\"worry_when\":\"The treated unit is at an extreme of the donor distribution on the predictors, or the pre-period gap has a persistent level offset.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_augmentation\",\"id\":\"sc_augmentation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Size of the augmentation correction\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.donor_pool\",\"id\":\"concept.donor_pool\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The set of untreated places a synthetic comparison is allowed to be built from. Choosing it is a research decision, not a technical one.\",\"title\":\"The donor pool\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.convex_hull\",\"explains\":\"The treated unit's characteristics can be written as a weighted average of the donors', with weights that do not go negative.\",\"id\":\"convex_hull\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor convex hull\",\"worry_when\":\"The treated unit is at an extreme of the donor distribution on the predictors, or the pre-period gap has a persistent level offset.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor hull\",\"worry_when\":\"The treated unit sitting outside the range of the donors on characteristics that matter; better optimisation cannot repair that.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:sc_convex_hull\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"sc_convex_hull\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Whether any blend of the comparison places could actually reproduce the treated place, or whether it sits outside their range entirely.\",\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor hull\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asc_gap> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Treated minus synthetic gap" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:sc_gap\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_gap\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"sc_gap\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Before the intervention the gap should hover near zero -- that is what the weights were chosen to achieve. Afterwards it is free to move, and the movement is the estimated effect. Reading it as a picture rather than as a single number tells you whether the effect arrived suddenly, built up, or faded. The most informative part is often the last stretch before the intervention. A gap that has already started drifting in the direction of the effect indicates anticipation, a mis-dated intervention, or a fit that was never good, and each of those undermines what follows.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The distance between the treated place and its synthetic twin, period by period. After the intervention, that distance is the estimate.\",\"plain_language\":\"The distance between the treated place and its synthetic twin, period by period. After the intervention, that distance is the estimate.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.abadie\",\"id\":\"sc.abadie\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build a weighted copy of the treated place out of untreated places that looked like it beforehand, then watch the two lines separate.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.augmented\",\"id\":\"sc.augmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When no weighted average of the donors can match the treated place, correct the leftover bias with a ridge model and show the size of the correction.\",\"title\":\"Augmented synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.matrix_completion\",\"id\":\"sc.matrix_completion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat the missing treated-period outcomes as holes in a panel and fill them in with a low-rank model of what the panel would have done.\",\"title\":\"Matrix completion\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.scpi\",\"id\":\"sc.scpi\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Confidence intervals for a synthetic control that come from a model of the prediction error rather than from permutation.\",\"title\":\"Prediction intervals for synthetic control (scpi)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.synthdid\",\"id\":\"sc.synthdid\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the comparison units and the pre-treatment periods, then run a difference in differences on the result - a level gap is allowed, a trend gap is not.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_pre_fit\",\"id\":\"sc_pre_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_anticipation\",\"explains\":\"Units did not change behaviour before treatment arrived because they knew it was coming.\",\"id\":\"no_anticipation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No anticipation\",\"worry_when\":\"There was a public announcement well before the policy started, or the pre-period coefficients move in the period immediately before adoption.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_placebo_time\",\"id\":\"sc_placebo_time\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Placebo in time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Treated minus synthetic gap\",\"worry_when\":\"A gap that is already moving in the direction of the effect before the intervention date.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:sc_gap\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"sc_gap\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The distance between the treated place and its synthetic twin, period by period. After the intervention, that distance is the estimate.\",\"title\":\"Treated minus synthetic gap\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asc_loo> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Leave one donor out" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:sc_loo\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_loo\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"sc_loo\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Synthetic controls often rest on a few places carrying most of the weight, and those places have their own histories -- a local recession, a different policy, a data revision. Dropping each in turn and refitting shows how much of the estimate is really about the treated unit and how much is about one of its stand-ins. Some movement is expected, since the blend has to be rebuilt each time. What matters is the size of the movement relative to the effect being claimed.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Drop each contributing comparison place in turn and refit. The estimate should not depend on any single one.\",\"plain_language\":\"Drop each contributing comparison place in turn and refit. The estimate should not depend on any single one.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.abadie\",\"id\":\"sc.abadie\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build a weighted copy of the treated place out of untreated places that looked like it beforehand, then watch the two lines separate.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.augmented\",\"id\":\"sc.augmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When no weighted average of the donors can match the treated place, correct the leftover bias with a ridge model and show the size of the correction.\",\"title\":\"Augmented synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.matrix_completion\",\"id\":\"sc.matrix_completion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat the missing treated-period outcomes as holes in a panel and fill them in with a low-rank model of what the panel would have done.\",\"title\":\"Matrix completion\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.synthdid\",\"id\":\"sc.synthdid\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the comparison units and the pre-treatment periods, then run a difference in differences on the result - a level gap is allowed, a trend gap is not.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_weights\",\"id\":\"sc_weights\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Donor weights\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.leave_one_out\",\"id\":\"leave_one_out\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Leave one unit out\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.donor_pool\",\"id\":\"concept.donor_pool\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The set of untreated places a synthetic comparison is allowed to be built from. Choosing it is a research decision, not a technical one.\",\"title\":\"The donor pool\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Leave one donor out\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropping one donor moving the estimate by a large share of the effect itself; the result then rests on one comparison unit rather than on a synthetic one.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:sc_loo\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"sc_loo\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Drop each contributing comparison place in turn and refit. The estimate should not depend on any single one.\",\"title\":\"Leave one donor out\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asc_mc_rank> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Matrix-completion rank" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:sc_mc_rank\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_mc_rank\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"sc_mc_rank\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Matrix completion fills in the outcomes that would have been observed if nobody had been treated, by assuming the panel is driven by a small number of underlying patterns shared across units and periods. The rank is how many such patterns were used, and the penalty is what stopped it using more. A higher rank fits the observed data more closely, including its noise. That is why the pre-treatment fit here is not evidence of anything on its own -- the model was fitted on those very periods. The placebo-in-time check is the honest fit test for this family of methods.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How flexible the fitted model of untreated behaviour was allowed to be.\",\"plain_language\":\"How flexible the fitted model of untreated behaviour was allowed to be.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.matrix_completion\",\"id\":\"sc.matrix_completion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat the missing treated-period outcomes as holes in a panel and fill them in with a low-rank model of what the panel would have done.\",\"title\":\"Matrix completion\"}],\"references\":[\"Athey et al. (2021), Matrix completion methods for causal panel data models\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.matrix_completion\",\"id\":\"sc.matrix_completion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat the missing treated-period outcomes as holes in a panel and fill them in with a low-rank model of what the panel would have done.\",\"title\":\"Matrix completion\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_placebo_time\",\"id\":\"sc_placebo_time\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Placebo in time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_weights\",\"id\":\"sc_weights\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Donor weights\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Matrix-completion rank\",\"worry_when\":\"A high rank, which means the model was flexible enough to fit noise; read the placebo-in-time panel rather than the pre-period fit.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:sc_mc_rank\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"sc_mc_rank\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How flexible the fitted model of untreated behaviour was allowed to be.\",\"title\":\"Matrix-completion rank\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asc_placebo_space> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Placebo in space" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:sc_placebo_space\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_placebo_space\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"sc_placebo_space\",\"in_more_depth\":\"With a single treated unit there is no sampling distribution to appeal to, so the yardstick has to be built by pretending. Each comparison place takes a turn as the treated unit and gets its own gap; the real one is then ranked among them. The statistic is usually the post-intervention gap relative to the pre-intervention fit error, because a place that was fitted badly to begin with will show a large gap afterwards for no interesting reason. Note the arithmetic limit: with seventeen comparison places the smallest attainable p-value is about one in eighteen, which is a property of the design and not a shortcoming of the software.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-run the whole method pretending each comparison place was the treated one. If many of them show a gap as large, the real one is unremarkable.\",\"plain_language\":\"Re-run the whole method pretending each comparison place was the treated one. If many of them show a gap as large, the real one is unremarkable.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.abadie\",\"id\":\"sc.abadie\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build a weighted copy of the treated place out of untreated places that looked like it beforehand, then watch the two lines separate.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.augmented\",\"id\":\"sc.augmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When no weighted average of the donors can match the treated place, correct the leftover bias with a ridge model and show the size of the correction.\",\"title\":\"Augmented synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.matrix_completion\",\"id\":\"sc.matrix_completion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat the missing treated-period outcomes as holes in a panel and fill them in with a low-rank model of what the panel would have done.\",\"title\":\"Matrix completion\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.microsynth\",\"id\":\"sc.microsynth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Synthetic control when the treated unit is a neighbourhood or a small area and there are many of them.\",\"title\":\"Microsynth (many treated units)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.synthdid\",\"id\":\"sc.synthdid\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the comparison units and the pre-treatment periods, then run a difference in differences on the result - a level gap is allowed, a trend gap is not.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences\"}],\"references\":[\"Abadie, Diamond & Hainmueller (2010)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_placebo\",\"id\":\"diagnostic.sc_placebo\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-run the whole method pretending each donor was the treated unit. If many donors show a gap as large, the real one is unremarkable.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic control placebos\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.placebo\",\"id\":\"concept.placebo\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same analysis somewhere the effect cannot exist. If you find one, the design is producing effects that are not there.\",\"title\":\"Placebo tests\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.donor_pool\",\"id\":\"concept.donor_pool\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The set of untreated places a synthetic comparison is allowed to be built from. Choosing it is a research decision, not a technical one.\",\"title\":\"The donor pool\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Placebo in space\",\"worry_when\":\"Several comparison places showing ratios as large as the treated unit's; or a pool so small that no small p-value is attainable however large the effect.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:sc_placebo_space\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"sc_placebo_space\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Re-run the whole method pretending each comparison place was the treated one. If many of them show a gap as large, the real one is unremarkable.\",\"title\":\"Placebo in space\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asc_placebo_time> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Placebo in time" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:sc_placebo_time\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_placebo_time\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"sc_placebo_time\",\"in_more_depth\":\"This is the cleanest test of whether the method manufactures breaks. Only data from before the real intervention is used, so any apparent effect at the fake date is produced by the fitting rather than by a policy. A large pseudo-effect means either that the pre-period fit is unstable or that the outcome contains breaks of this size routinely, in which case the real one is not distinguishable from them. It needs enough pre-treatment history to be possible at all, which is why it is sometimes unavailable.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Move the intervention to an earlier date when nothing happened, refit on the earlier data only, and see whether the method finds an effect anyway.\",\"plain_language\":\"Move the intervention to an earlier date when nothing happened, refit on the earlier data only, and see whether the method finds an effect anyway.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.abadie\",\"id\":\"sc.abadie\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build a weighted copy of the treated place out of untreated places that looked like it beforehand, then watch the two lines separate.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.augmented\",\"id\":\"sc.augmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When no weighted average of the donors can match the treated place, correct the leftover bias with a ridge model and show the size of the correction.\",\"title\":\"Augmented synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.matrix_completion\",\"id\":\"sc.matrix_completion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat the missing treated-period outcomes as holes in a panel and fill them in with a low-rank model of what the panel would have done.\",\"title\":\"Matrix completion\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.synthdid\",\"id\":\"sc.synthdid\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the comparison units and the pre-treatment periods, then run a difference in differences on the result - a level gap is allowed, a trend gap is not.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.placebo\",\"id\":\"concept.placebo\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same analysis somewhere the effect cannot exist. If you find one, the design is producing effects that are not there.\",\"title\":\"Placebo tests\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_gap\",\"id\":\"sc_gap\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Treated minus synthetic gap\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_pre_fit\",\"id\":\"sc_pre_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_anticipation\",\"explains\":\"Units did not change behaviour before treatment arrived because they knew it was coming.\",\"id\":\"no_anticipation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No anticipation\",\"worry_when\":\"There was a public announcement well before the policy started, or the pre-period coefficients move in the period immediately before adoption.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_anticipation\",\"explains\":\"Units did not change behaviour before treatment arrived because they knew it was coming.\",\"id\":\"no_anticipation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No anticipation\",\"worry_when\":\"There was a public announcement well before the policy started, or the pre-period coefficients move in the period immediately before adoption.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Placebo in time\",\"worry_when\":\"A sizeable effect at a date when nothing happened; or too little pre-treatment history for the check to run, leaving nothing that rules this out.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:sc_placebo_time\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"sc_placebo_time\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Move the intervention to an earlier date when nothing happened, refit on the earlier data only, and see whether the method finds an effect anyway.\",\"title\":\"Placebo in time\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asc_pre_fit> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Pre-treatment fit" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:sc_pre_fit\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_pre_fit\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"sc_pre_fit\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A synthetic control is a made-up comparison unit: a weighted blend of other places chosen so that the blend follows the treated place through the years before anything happened. If the blend does that well, it is reasonable to let it stand in for what would have happened afterwards. If it does not, there is no argument left. The fit is reported as the typical distance between the two lines before treatment, compared against how much the treated place's own outcome moves about. When that distance is a large share of the outcome's ordinary variation, much of the gap you see afterwards could simply be the same fit error continuing.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How closely the weighted combination of comparison places tracked the treated place before the intervention. This is the whole basis of the method.\",\"plain_language\":\"How closely the weighted combination of comparison places tracked the treated place before the intervention. This is the whole basis of the method.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.synthdid_r\",\"id\":\"did.synthdid_r\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight both units and periods, then take a difference-in-differences -- halfway between synthetic control and DiD.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences (synthdid)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.abadie\",\"id\":\"sc.abadie\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build a weighted copy of the treated place out of untreated places that looked like it beforehand, then watch the two lines separate.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.augmented\",\"id\":\"sc.augmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When no weighted average of the donors can match the treated place, correct the leftover bias with a ridge model and show the size of the correction.\",\"title\":\"Augmented synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.matrix_completion\",\"id\":\"sc.matrix_completion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat the missing treated-period outcomes as holes in a panel and fill them in with a low-rank model of what the panel would have done.\",\"title\":\"Matrix completion\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.microsynth\",\"id\":\"sc.microsynth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Synthetic control when the treated unit is a neighbourhood or a small area and there are many of them.\",\"title\":\"Microsynth (many treated units)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.scpi\",\"id\":\"sc.scpi\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Confidence intervals for a synthetic control that come from a model of the prediction error rather than from permutation.\",\"title\":\"Prediction intervals for synthetic control (scpi)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.synthdid\",\"id\":\"sc.synthdid\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the comparison units and the pre-treatment periods, then run a difference in differences on the result - a level gap is allowed, a trend gap is not.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences\"}],\"references\":[\"Abadie, Diamond & Hainmueller (2010)\",\"Abadie (2021), Using synthetic controls\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_gap\",\"id\":\"sc_gap\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Treated minus synthetic gap\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.donor_pool\",\"id\":\"concept.donor_pool\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The set of untreated places a synthetic comparison is allowed to be built from. Choosing it is a research decision, not a technical one.\",\"title\":\"The donor pool\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.abadie\",\"id\":\"sc.abadie\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build a weighted copy of the treated place out of untreated places that looked like it beforehand, then watch the two lines separate.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit\",\"worry_when\":\"A pre-treatment fit error that is a large share of the treated unit's own variation; a synthetic that cannot reproduce the pre-period is not a counterfactual.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:sc_pre_fit\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"sc_pre_fit\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How closely the weighted combination of comparison places tracked the treated place before the intervention. This is the whole basis of the method.\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asc_time_weights> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Time weights" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:sc_time_weights\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_time_weights\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"sc_time_weights\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Some variants of the method weight the earlier periods rather than treating them all alike, concentrating the match on the stretch of history most informative about the years that follow. That is often sensible, and it has a consequence worth seeing: if nearly all the weight sits on one or two periods, the comparison rests on that slice of history. The fitted level difference reported alongside is the other half of the story. Donors can reproduce the shape of the treated unit's path while sitting systematically above or below it, and some methods allow for that explicitly.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Which pre-treatment periods the fit was asked to match most closely.\",\"plain_language\":\"Which pre-treatment periods the fit was asked to match most closely.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.synthdid_r\",\"id\":\"did.synthdid_r\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight both units and periods, then take a difference-in-differences -- halfway between synthetic control and DiD.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences (synthdid)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.synthdid\",\"id\":\"sc.synthdid\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the comparison units and the pre-treatment periods, then run a difference in differences on the result - a level gap is allowed, a trend gap is not.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences\"}],\"references\":[\"Arkhangelsky et al. (2021), Synthetic difference-in-differences\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.synthdid\",\"id\":\"sc.synthdid\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the comparison units and the pre-treatment periods, then run a difference in differences on the result - a level gap is allowed, a trend gap is not.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_weights\",\"id\":\"sc_weights\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Donor weights\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_pre_fit\",\"id\":\"sc_pre_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.donor_fit\",\"explains\":\"The weighted combination of untreated units reproduces the treated unit's pre-period path closely enough to stand in for it afterwards.\",\"id\":\"donor_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Pre-treatment fit from the donor pool\",\"worry_when\":\"Visible pre-period gaps; a short pre-period relative to the number of donors; one donor carrying most of the weight.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Time weights\",\"worry_when\":\"Nearly all the weight on one or two periods; or a large level shift, meaning the donors match the shape but not the level of the treated unit.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:sc_time_weights\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"sc_time_weights\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Which pre-treatment periods the fit was asked to match most closely.\",\"title\":\"Time weights\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asc_weights> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Donor weights" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:sc_weights\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_weights\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"sc_weights\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The method chooses a weighted average of the available comparison units, usually with the weights adding to one and none of them negative, so that the result is a genuine blend rather than an extrapolation. Reading the weights is how you sanity-check the comparison: a synthetic California made mostly of two states is a two-state comparison, and those two states' histories are now your identifying assumption. Sparse weights are normal and not by themselves a problem. Concentration is the thing to notice, because it decides how much of the answer rests on one place's particular circumstances.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Which comparison places make up the synthetic twin, and how much of it each one is.\",\"plain_language\":\"Which comparison places make up the synthetic twin, and how much of it each one is.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.synthdid_r\",\"id\":\"did.synthdid_r\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight both units and periods, then take a difference-in-differences -- halfway between synthetic control and DiD.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences (synthdid)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.abadie\",\"id\":\"sc.abadie\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build a weighted copy of the treated place out of untreated places that looked like it beforehand, then watch the two lines separate.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.augmented\",\"id\":\"sc.augmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When no weighted average of the donors can match the treated place, correct the leftover bias with a ridge model and show the size of the correction.\",\"title\":\"Augmented synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.matrix_completion\",\"id\":\"sc.matrix_completion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat the missing treated-period outcomes as holes in a panel and fill them in with a low-rank model of what the panel would have done.\",\"title\":\"Matrix completion\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.microsynth\",\"id\":\"sc.microsynth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Synthetic control when the treated unit is a neighbourhood or a small area and there are many of them.\",\"title\":\"Microsynth (many treated units)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.scpi\",\"id\":\"sc.scpi\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Confidence intervals for a synthetic control that come from a model of the prediction error rather than from permutation.\",\"title\":\"Prediction intervals for synthetic control (scpi)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.synthdid\",\"id\":\"sc.synthdid\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the comparison units and the pre-treatment periods, then run a difference in differences on the result - a level gap is allowed, a trend gap is not.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.donor_pool\",\"id\":\"concept.donor_pool\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The set of untreated places a synthetic comparison is allowed to be built from. Choosing it is a research decision, not a technical one.\",\"title\":\"The donor pool\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_loo\",\"id\":\"sc_loo\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Leave one donor out\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.sc_convex_hull\",\"id\":\"sc_convex_hull\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor hull\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.convex_hull\",\"explains\":\"The treated unit's characteristics can be written as a weighted average of the donors', with weights that do not go negative.\",\"id\":\"convex_hull\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor convex hull\",\"worry_when\":\"The treated unit is at an extreme of the donor distribution on the predictors, or the pre-period gap has a persistent level offset.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.convex_hull\",\"explains\":\"The treated unit's characteristics can be written as a weighted average of the donors', with weights that do not go negative.\",\"id\":\"convex_hull\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Treated unit inside the donor convex hull\",\"worry_when\":\"The treated unit is at an extreme of the donor distribution on the predictors, or the pre-period gap has a persistent level offset.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Donor weights\",\"worry_when\":\"One donor carrying more than about half the weight; the analysis is then a two-unit comparison in synthetic-control costume, and that donor's own history around the intervention deserves reading.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:sc_weights\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"sc_weights\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Which comparison places make up the synthetic twin, and how much of it each one is.\",\"title\":\"Donor weights\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aseed_stability> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Stability across seeds" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:seed_stability\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.seed_stability\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"seed_stability\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Cross-fitting divides the data into folds at random, and the division is an arbitrary choice made by a random number generator. If the estimate changes materially when that choice changes, then the reported precision is describing sampling variation while ignoring a second source of variation that is just as real. The remedy when it fires is to average over several splits and to widen the interval accordingly, or to use a simpler learner that is less sensitive to which rows it trained on.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Repeat the whole procedure with different random splits and see whether the answer stays put.\",\"plain_language\":\"Repeat the whole procedure with different random splits and see whether the answer stays put.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_plr\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_plr\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Predict the outcome and the treatment from the confounders, then regress what is left of one on what is left of the other.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (partially linear)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.policy_tree\",\"id\":\"ml.policy_tree\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn a short, readable rule for who to treat, then value that rule on data it never saw.\",\"title\":\"Policy tree (who should be treated)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.cross_fitting\",\"id\":\"concept.cross_fitting\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the nuisance models on one part of the data and apply them to another, so a flexible learner cannot fit its own noise into the effect estimate.\",\"title\":\"Cross-fitting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.fold_stability\",\"id\":\"fold_stability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Stability across folds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.bootstrap_spread\",\"id\":\"bootstrap_spread\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Bootstrap spread\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Stability across seeds\",\"worry_when\":\"A spread across seeds comparable to the standard error, or a sign that changes; the split is arbitrary and the answer should not depend on it.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:seed_stability\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"seed_stability\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Repeat the whole procedure with different random splits and see whether the answer stays put.\",\"title\":\"Stability across seeds\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aspec_curve> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Specification curve" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:spec_curve\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.spec_curve\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"spec_curve\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Reporting three robustness checks is a sample of size three from a space the analyst chose. Running the grid -- covariate sets, calipers, bandwidths, trimming rules -- and drawing all of the answers puts the whole space on one picture, so a reader can see how much of it supports the conclusion. A finding that holds across most of the curve is stronger than one that holds for three hand-picked variants. A curve that crosses zero says the finding is a specification rather than a result. Where the marked specification sits in the ordering is itself worth reading.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Every defensible version of this analysis, sorted from smallest estimate to largest, with the one on your board marked.\",\"plain_language\":\"Every defensible version of this analysis, sorted from smallest estimate to largest, with the one on your board marked.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.alternate_spec\",\"id\":\"probe.alternate_spec\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the grid of defensible specifications and sort the answers. The curve is the result, not the one you happened to run.\",\"title\":\"Specification curve\"}],\"references\":[\"Simonsohn, Simmons & Nelson (2020), Specification curve analysis\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.spec_curve\",\"id\":\"concept.spec_curve\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Every defensible version of the analysis, sorted by estimate, so you can see the whole distribution instead of one draw from it.\",\"title\":\"Specification curve\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.alternate_spec\",\"id\":\"probe.alternate_spec\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the grid of defensible specifications and sort the answers. The curve is the result, not the one you happened to run.\",\"title\":\"Specification curve\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.forest_plot\",\"id\":\"concept.forest_plot\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"One row per method, each with its interval. When several methods ran, the forest is the result -- not a single number chosen from it.\",\"title\":\"The forest plot, and why there is no headline number\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"A shock in a single series\",\"Something else pushed people in\",\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Specification curve\",\"worry_when\":\"A curve that crosses zero; or the specification on the board sitting at one end of the curve rather than in the middle of it.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:spec_curve\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"spec_curve\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Every defensible version of this analysis, sorted from smallest estimate to largest, with the one on your board marked.\",\"title\":\"Specification curve\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Astrategy_paths> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Treatment strategies compared" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:strategy_paths\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.strategy_paths\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"strategy_paths\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The headline is a difference at the end of follow-up; the paths show how it got there. A gap that widens steadily is a plausible cumulative effect. A gap that is fully present in the first period, before the treatment could have acted, is a sign the models are picking up differences between the people following each strategy rather than the strategies themselves. Both paths come from the same fitted models, so the difference between them is exactly as good as those models are.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The simulated outcome path under each strategy being compared, so you can see when and how they diverge.\",\"plain_language\":\"The simulated outcome path under each strategy being compared, so you can see when and how they diverge.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.gformula\",\"id\":\"long.gformula\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model each period in turn, then simulate the world where everyone followed the strategy you name.\",\"title\":\"Parametric g-formula\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.natural_course\",\"id\":\"natural_course\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Natural-course calibration\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.gformula\",\"id\":\"long.gformula\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model each period in turn, then simulate the world where everyone followed the strategy you name.\",\"title\":\"Parametric g-formula\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.model_form\",\"explains\":\"The line you fit before the interruption is the line the outcome would have continued to follow.\",\"id\":\"model_form\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Functional form of the counterfactual trend\",\"worry_when\":\"Few pre-period points; residual autocorrelation that survives the model; the estimate flips when you add a seasonal term or a quadratic trend.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.consistency\",\"explains\":\"The outcome you observe under the treatment a unit received is the same as the potential outcome under that treatment. In practice: the treatment has to be a thing someone could actually do.\",\"id\":\"consistency\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Consistency / well-defined intervention\",\"worry_when\":\"The treatment is a state of the world rather than an action, or it is coded from several very different real interventions.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.consistency\",\"explains\":\"The outcome you observe under the treatment a unit received is the same as the potential outcome under that treatment. In practice: the treatment has to be a thing someone could actually do.\",\"id\":\"consistency\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Consistency / well-defined intervention\",\"worry_when\":\"The treatment is a state of the world rather than an action, or it is coded from several very different real interventions.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\"],\"title\":\"Treatment strategies compared\",\"worry_when\":\"A gap that appears in the first period, before the treatment has had time to act.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:strategy_paths\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"strategy_paths\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The simulated outcome path under each strategy being compared, so you can see when and how they diverge.\",\"title\":\"Treatment strategies compared\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asubgroups> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Pre-specified subgroups" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:subgroups\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"*\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"*\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.subgroups\",\"gaps\":[\"no method in the catalogue lists this check, so it may never appear\"],\"id\":\"subgroups\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Each subgroup contrast is estimated the same way as the headline, so individually they are sound. The difficulty is collective: with twenty contrasts and no real differences at all, you expect one to cross the conventional threshold by luck, and it is that one that tends to get written up. The defence is to say in advance which subgroups you would examine, and to label everything else as exploration. Where a stricter threshold is appropriate, this panel reports what it would be for the number of contrasts actually run.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Effects estimated inside named groups, with a reminder of how easily one of them looks impressive by chance.\",\"plain_language\":\"Effects estimated inside named groups, with a reminder of how easily one of them looks impressive by chance.\",\"produced_by\":[],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.multiplicity\",\"id\":\"concept.multiplicity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Look at enough subgroups and one will look impressive. Pre-specify them, or report that you did not.\",\"title\":\"Subgroups and multiplicity\"},{\"acronym\":\"GATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.gate\",\"id\":\"GATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"How does the effect vary across pre-specified groups?\",\"title\":\"Group average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cate_calibration\",\"id\":\"cate_calibration\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"CATE calibration\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"*\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":null,\"title\":\"*\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"*\"],\"title\":\"Pre-specified subgroups\",\"worry_when\":\"Any of these groups was chosen after seeing the data, or a single significant subgroup is reported without the others beside it.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[\"no method in the catalogue lists this check, so it may never appear\"],\"id\":\"diagnostic:subgroups\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"subgroups\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Effects estimated inside named groups, with a reminder of how easily one of them looks impressive by chance.\",\"title\":\"Pre-specified subgroups\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asubset_stability> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Stability on random subsets" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:subset_stability\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.subset_stability\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"subset_stability\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Half the data gives a noisier estimate, and that is expected: the interval should widen by roughly the square root of two. What is not expected is draws that contradict each other, or a spread far wider than the loss of precision explains. That pattern means the estimate depends on which rows happened to be included, which is a different and worse problem than ordinary sampling noise. This is a blunt check and a useful one, because it needs no assumptions beyond the ones the analysis already makes.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Refit the analysis on random parts of the data and see whether the answer stays put.\",\"plain_language\":\"Refit the analysis on random parts of the data and see whether the answer stays put.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.subset\",\"id\":\"probe.subset\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-estimate on random subsets. The answer should move about as much as losing that much data costs, and no more.\",\"title\":\"Subset stability\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.bootstrap_spread\",\"id\":\"bootstrap_spread\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Bootstrap spread\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.leave_one_out\",\"id\":\"leave_one_out\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Leave one unit out\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.provisional\",\"id\":\"concept.provisional\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A result the product does not think you should trust as it stands. It still computed it; it just will not let the flag disappear.\",\"title\":\"Provisional results\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"A shock in a single series\",\"Something else pushed people in\",\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Stability on random subsets\",\"worry_when\":\"A spread much wider than the loss of precision explains, or subsets that flip sign while the headline interval excludes zero.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:subset_stability\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"subset_stability\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Refit the analysis on random parts of the data and see whether the answer stays put.\",\"title\":\"Stability on random subsets\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asurvival_censoring> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Right-censoring assumptions" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:survival_censoring\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.survival\",\"id\":\"survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Follow people from a common time zero; some leave follow-up before the event is observed.\",\"title\":\"Time until an event\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.survival_censoring\",\"gaps\":[\"no hand-written explanation yet, so the app generates one from the record\"],\"id\":\"survival_censoring\",\"in_more_depth\":\"No check can prove an assumption true. The strongest honest thing any of them can do is fail to contradict one, so read this next to the claim it speaks to rather than as a verdict. This one is not tied to a single assumption: it describes the data or the fit rather than testing a claim about them.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A check Causal Capybara runs for you and draws on the results screen. It appears when the design is \\\"Time until an event\\\". The card it appears on carries a plain summary of what it found in your data, and a line saying what would worry me about it.\",\"plain_language\":\"A check Causal Capybara runs for you and draws on the results screen. It appears when the design is \\\"Time until an event\\\". The card it appears on carries a plain summary of what it found in your data, and a line saying what would worry me about it.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.aft\",\"id\":\"survival.aft\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a right-censored log-normal/Weibull AFT model; standardize survival/RMST over baseline covariates under explicit causal assumptions, or report a conditional time factor.\",\"title\":\"Censored AFT regression and causal standardization\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.kaplan_meier\",\"id\":\"survival.kaplan_meier\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare observed group event-free probabilities at an explicit common horizon; this is an unadjusted association.\",\"title\":\"Unadjusted Kaplan–Meier survival contrast\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.rmst\",\"id\":\"survival.rmst\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare areas under observed Kaplan–Meier curves through a common horizon in time units; this is an unadjusted association.\",\"title\":\"Unadjusted restricted mean survival contrast\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.survival\",\"id\":\"survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Follow people from a common time zero; some leave follow-up before the event is observed.\",\"title\":\"Time until an event\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Time until an event\"],\"title\":\"Right-censoring assumptions\",\"worry_when\":\"The card itself says what would worry me here, because that depends on the numbers this particular run produced. A longer written explanation of this check has not been added to the library yet.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[\"no hand-written explanation yet, so the app generates one from the record\"],\"id\":\"diagnostic:survival_censoring\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"survival_censoring\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A check Causal Capybara runs for you and draws on the results screen. It appears when the design is \\\"Time until an event\\\". The card it appears on carries a plain summary of what it found in your data, and a line saying what would worry me about it.\",\"title\":\"Right-censoring assumptions\"}" .
