n and causal standardization\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.kaplan_meier\",\"id\":\"survival.kaplan_meier\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare observed group event-free probabilities at an explicit common horizon; this is an unadjusted association.\",\"title\":\"Unadjusted Kaplan–Meier survival contrast\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.rmst\",\"id\":\"survival.rmst\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare areas under observed Kaplan–Meier curves through a common horizon in time units; this is an unadjusted association.\",\"title\":\"Unadjusted restricted mean survival contrast\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.survival\",\"id\":\"survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Follow people from a common time zero; some leave follow-up before the event is observed.\",\"title\":\"Time until an event\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Time until an event\"],\"title\":\"Follow-up and common horizon support\",\"worry_when\":\"The card itself says what would worry me here, because that depends on the numbers this particular run produced. A longer written explanation of this check has not been added to the library yet.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[\"no hand-written explanation yet, so the app generates one from the record\"],\"id\":\"diagnostic:survival_followup\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"survival_followup\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A check Causal Capybara runs for you and draws on the results screen. It appears when the design is \\\"Time until an event\\\". The card it appears on carries a plain summary of what it found in your data, and a line saying what would worry me about it.\",\"title\":\"Follow-up and common horizon support\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Asurvival_heterogeneity> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Baseline subgroup heterogeneity in survival contrasts" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:survival_heterogeneity\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.survival\",\"id\":\"survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Follow people from a common time zero; some leave follow-up before the event is observed.\",\"title\":\"Time until an event\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.survival_heterogeneity\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"survival_heterogeneity\",\"in_more_depth\":\"One categorical baseline modifier defines 2–8 supported subgroups. For Kaplan–Meier and RMST, the contrasts remain raw observed-group associations. For adjusted AFT survival/RMST, each subgroup uses its own empirical baseline profiles under both treatment predictions; time-factor equality is tested on the log scale. A Wald omnibus test compares an independent vector of between-subgroup contrasts. Singular contrast covariance yields no p-value; fewer than 200 usable bootstrap draws trigger a Monte Carlo precision warning. Reference-subgroup comparisons and confidence intervals are pointwise, without multiplicity adjustment; searches across modifiers, methods, scales and horizons require an explicit analysis plan. Sparse events, unsupported cell-specific follow-up and failed bootstrap refits are reported. A prespecification declaration cannot verify the protocol or baseline timing.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Test equality of subgroup treatment contrasts directly on one prespecified scale and horizon using their full joint bootstrap covariance.\",\"plain_language\":\"Test equality of subgroup treatment contrasts directly on one prespecified scale and horizon using their full joint bootstrap covariance.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.aft\",\"id\":\"survival.aft\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a right-censored log-normal/Weibull AFT model; standardize survival/RMST over baseline covariates under explicit causal assumptions, or report a conditional time factor.\",\"title\":\"Censored AFT regression and causal standardization\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.kaplan_meier\",\"id\":\"survival.kaplan_meier\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare observed group event-free probabilities at an explicit common horizon; this is an unadjusted association.\",\"title\":\"Unadjusted Kaplan–Meier survival contrast\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.rmst\",\"id\":\"survival.rmst\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare areas under observed Kaplan–Meier curves through a common horizon in time units; this is an unadjusted association.\",\"title\":\"Unadjusted restricted mean survival contrast\"}],\"references\":[\"Altman and Bland (2003), Interaction revisited: the difference between two estimates\",\"Wang et al. (2007), Statistics in Medicine — Reporting of Subgroup Analyses in Clinical Trials\",\"Knol and VanderWeele (2012), Recommendations for presenting analyses of effect modification and interaction\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.kaplan_meier\",\"id\":\"survival.kaplan_meier\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare observed group event-free probabilities at an explicit common horizon; this is an unadjusted association.\",\"title\":\"Unadjusted Kaplan–Meier survival contrast\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.rmst\",\"id\":\"survival.rmst\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare areas under observed Kaplan–Meier curves through a common horizon in time units; this is an unadjusted association.\",\"title\":\"Unadjusted restricted mean survival contrast\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.survival.aft\",\"id\":\"survival.aft\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a right-censored log-normal/Weibull AFT model; standardize survival/RMST over baseline covariates under explicit causal assumptions, or report a conditional time factor.\",\"title\":\"Censored AFT regression and causal standardization\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.heterogeneity_scale\",\"id\":\"concept.heterogeneity_scale\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Absolute and relative treatment contrasts can vary differently across the same baseline groups.\",\"title\":\"Heterogeneity depends on the effect scale\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.heterogeneity_prespecification\",\"id\":\"concept.heterogeneity_prespecification\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Fix the modifier, coding, outcome and tested scale before inspecting effects, and report the complete testing family.\",\"title\":\"Baseline hypotheses, support and multiplicity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.survival\",\"id\":\"survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Follow people from a common time zero; some leave follow-up before the event is observed.\",\"title\":\"Time until an event\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Time until an event\"],\"title\":\"Baseline subgroup heterogeneity in survival contrasts\",\"worry_when\":null},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:survival_heterogeneity\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"survival_heterogeneity\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Test equality of subgroup treatment contrasts directly on one prespecified scale and horizon using their full joint bootstrap covariance.\",\"title\":\"Baseline subgroup heterogeneity in survival contrasts\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Atemporal_ordering> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Temporal ordering of covariates" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:temporal_ordering\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.temporal_ordering\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"temporal_ordering\",\"in_more_depth\":\"A variable cannot confound a choice that had already been made. If a measurement was taken after treatment started, adjusting for it does not remove bias; it removes part of the effect, because some of what the treatment did shows up in that measurement. In longitudinal data this is easy to get wrong, because the same variable appears at many time points and only some of them are eligible. The rule is simple to state and worth restating: a covariate may adjust a treatment decision only if it was recorded before that decision.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A check that every variable used to adjust a decision was measured before that decision was taken.\",\"plain_language\":\"A check that every variable used to adjust a decision was measured before that decision was taken.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.g_estimation\",\"id\":\"long.g_estimation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate the effect of a treatment regime by finding the parameter that removes the association between treatment and the blipped-down outcome.\",\"title\":\"G-estimation of structural nested models\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.gformula\",\"id\":\"long.gformula\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model each period in turn, then simulate the world where everyone followed the strategy you name.\",\"title\":\"Parametric g-formula\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Regress backwards from the final outcome, then correct with the treatment probabilities: right if either set of models is right.\",\"title\":\"Sequential doubly robust (ICE g-formula with augmentation)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.msm_iptw\",\"id\":\"long.msm_iptw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight each person-period so the treated and untreated histories look alike, then fit one simple model to the reweighted data.\",\"title\":\"Marginal structural model (time-varying weights)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.controlled_direct\",\"id\":\"med.controlled_direct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"What the treatment would still do if the mediator were fixed at one value for everybody.\",\"title\":\"Controlled direct effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.interventional\",\"id\":\"med.interventional\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Shift the mediator to the distribution it would have in the population, rather than to each person's own unobservable counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Interventional direct and indirect effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.natural_effects\",\"id\":\"med.natural_effects\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Split the total effect into the part that travelled through the mediator and the part that did not.\",\"title\":\"Natural direct and indirect effects\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"guardrail.bad_control\",\"id\":\"guardrail.bad_control\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Anything measured after treatment, or caused by it, does not belong in the adjustment set. Adjusting for it blocks part of the effect you are trying to measure.\",\"title\":\"Bad controls\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.time_varying_confounding\",\"id\":\"concept.time_varying_confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A variable that is both a consequence of past treatment and a cause of future treatment. Ordinary regression cannot handle it; g-methods can.\",\"title\":\"Time-varying confounding\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_time_varying_confounding\",\"explains\":\"At every period, the things that drive the treatment decision and also affect the outcome are measured -- and measured before that decision.\",\"id\":\"no_time_varying_confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No unmeasured time-varying confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Clinical measurements taken after the dose was changed; employment status recorded at the end of a period used to explain treatment at its start.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_time_varying_confounding\",\"explains\":\"At every period, the things that drive the treatment decision and also affect the outcome are measured -- and measured before that decision.\",\"id\":\"no_time_varying_confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No unmeasured time-varying confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Clinical measurements taken after the dose was changed; employment status recorded at the end of a period used to explain treatment at its start.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\"],\"title\":\"Temporal ordering of covariates\",\"worry_when\":\"A covariate measured after the treatment it is adjusting for.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:temporal_ordering\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"temporal_ordering\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A check that every variable used to adjust a decision was measured before that decision was taken.\",\"title\":\"Temporal ordering of covariates\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Atmle_targeting> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Targeting step" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:tmle_targeting\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.tmle_targeting\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"tmle_targeting\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Targeted learning starts from an outcome model's prediction and then applies one small correction designed to remove the bias that a prediction-focused model leaves behind. That correction is driven by the treatment probabilities, so its size tells you two things at once: how far off the initial outcome model was, and how extreme the weights are. A small, cleanly converged correction is the healthy case. A large one is not automatically wrong, but it means the answer is coming from the correction rather than from the model it corrected, and the overlap diagnostics are then the ones to read.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How far the final correction moved the estimate away from the plain outcome model's answer, and whether the correction converged.\",\"plain_language\":\"How far the final correction moved the estimate away from the plain outcome model's answer, and whether the correction converged.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\"}],\"references\":[\"van der Laan & Rose (2011), Targeted Learning\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.overlap\",\"id\":\"overlap\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Overlap between the arms\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.propensity_clipping\",\"id\":\"propensity_clipping\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Propensity clipping\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":null,\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Targeting step\",\"worry_when\":\"A large correction, or an equation the step failed to solve; both point back to extreme treatment probabilities.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:tmle_targeting\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"tmle_targeting\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How far the final correction moved the estimate away from the plain outcome model's answer, and whether the correction converged.\",\"title\":\"Targeting step\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Atreatment_mediator_interaction> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Treatment-mediator interaction" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:treatment_mediator_interaction\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.treatment_mediator_interaction\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"treatment_mediator_interaction\",\"in_more_depth\":\"If it does, the decomposition into direct and indirect effects is unambiguous. If it does not -- if the treatment changes how much the mediator matters -- then there are two mirror-image ways of splitting the total, they disagree, and neither is more correct than the other. Which one gets reported becomes an unstated choice. A model fitted without an interaction term assumes the question away rather than answering it, which is why the term is tested rather than omitted.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Whether the mediator does the same work whether or not the treatment is on.\",\"plain_language\":\"Whether the mediator does the same work whether or not the treatment is on.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.controlled_direct\",\"id\":\"med.controlled_direct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"What the treatment would still do if the mediator were fixed at one value for everybody.\",\"title\":\"Controlled direct effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.interventional\",\"id\":\"med.interventional\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Shift the mediator to the distribution it would have in the population, rather than to each person's own unobservable counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Interventional direct and indirect effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.natural_effects\",\"id\":\"med.natural_effects\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Split the total effect into the part that travelled through the mediator and the part that did not.\",\"title\":\"Natural direct and indirect effects\"}],\"references\":[\"VanderWeele (2015), Explanation in Causal Inference\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cde_path\",\"id\":\"cde_path\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Controlled direct effect across mediator levels\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.mediation_decomposition\",\"id\":\"mediation_decomposition\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Total = direct + indirect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.natural_effects\",\"id\":\"med.natural_effects\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Split the total effect into the part that travelled through the mediator and the part that did not.\",\"title\":\"Natural direct and indirect effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.sequential_ignorability\",\"explains\":\"No unmeasured confounding of the treatment-outcome relationship, and none of the mediator-outcome relationship either, even after conditioning on treatment.\",\"id\":\"sequential_ignorability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Sequential ignorability\",\"worry_when\":\"There is a variable affected by treatment that also confounds the mediator-outcome relationship. Under that structure the natural effects are not identified at all.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.sequential_ignorability\",\"explains\":\"No unmeasured confounding of the treatment-outcome relationship, and none of the mediator-outcome relationship either, even after conditioning on treatment.\",\"id\":\"sequential_ignorability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Sequential ignorability\",\"worry_when\":\"There is a variable affected by treatment that also confounds the mediator-outcome relationship. Under that structure the natural effects are not identified at all.\"},\"tags\":[\"How it worked (pathways)\"],\"title\":\"Treatment-mediator interaction\",\"worry_when\":\"A strong interaction reported as a single direct effect; or a real interaction forced to zero, so the decomposition depends on which mirror you happened to report.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:treatment_mediator_interaction\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"treatment_mediator_interaction\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Whether the mediator does the same work whether or not the treatment is on.\",\"title\":\"Treatment-mediator interaction\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Atrim_cost> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "What trimming costs" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:trim_cost\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.trim_cost\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"trim_cost\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Trimming discards units whose estimated chance of treatment is too extreme for a stable comparison. It is a defensible response to poor overlap, and it is not free: the units dropped are not a random sample, they are precisely the ones least like anybody in the other arm. After trimming, the estimate refers to the units that remain. If a fifth of the treated group has gone, \\\"the effect of the programme on participants\\\" is no longer what the number means, and the sample flow records the change so the report can say so.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How many units a proposed trimming rule would drop, and who they are.\",\"plain_language\":\"How many units a proposed trimming rule would drop, and who they are.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.trim_curve\",\"id\":\"probe.trim_curve\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-estimate across a grid of propensity-score trimming thresholds instead of picking one and hoping.\",\"title\":\"Trimming curve\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.trim_stability\",\"id\":\"trim_stability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Stability across trimming thresholds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.consort\",\"id\":\"concept.consort\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A step-by-step record of how the imported rows became the analysis sample, with a reason for every row that left.\",\"title\":\"The sample flow\"},{\"acronym\":\"ATO\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ato\",\"id\":\"ATO\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For the people where treated and untreated genuinely overlap, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the overlap population\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"What trimming costs\",\"worry_when\":\"A threshold that drops a large share of the treated units, which has quietly redefined who the answer is about.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:trim_cost\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"trim_cost\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How many units a proposed trimming rule would drop, and who they are.\",\"title\":\"What trimming costs\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Atrim_stability> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Stability across trimming thresholds" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:trim_stability\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.trim_stability\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"trim_stability\",\"in_more_depth\":\"If the estimate barely moves as you trim more aggressively, the tails were not doing much and the finding is about the bulk of the data. If it walks steadily in one direction, the answer is being set by the units with the least comparable counterparts -- the ones the model had to extrapolate for. There is no threshold that is correct in general. The point of the curve is to make the choice visible rather than to make it for you, and to let a reader see whether the headline sits in the middle of the range or at one end of it.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"The estimate recomputed at a range of trimming thresholds, so you can see whether the units in the tails are deciding it.\",\"plain_language\":\"The estimate recomputed at a range of trimming thresholds, so you can see whether the units in the tails are deciding it.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.trim_curve\",\"id\":\"probe.trim_curve\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-estimate across a grid of propensity-score trimming thresholds instead of picking one and hoping.\",\"title\":\"Trimming curve\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.trim_cost\",\"id\":\"trim_cost\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"What trimming costs\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.spec_curve\",\"id\":\"concept.spec_curve\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Every defensible version of the analysis, sorted by estimate, so you can see the whole distribution instead of one draw from it.\",\"title\":\"Specification curve\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.overlap\",\"id\":\"overlap\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Overlap between the arms\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Stability across trimming thresholds\",\"worry_when\":\"The estimate walks steadily as you trim, or changes sign anywhere along the path.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:trim_stability\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"trim_stability\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The estimate recomputed at a range of trimming thresholds, so you can see whether the units in the tails are deciding it.\",\"title\":\"Stability across trimming thresholds\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aunobserved_confounder> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Simulated unobserved confounder" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:unobserved_confounder\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.unobserved_confounder\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"unobserved_confounder\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Every adjustment in an observational design rests on having measured the things that pushed people towards treatment and also move the outcome. That assumption cannot be checked against the data, because the variables in question are precisely the ones you do not have. What can be done is to imagine one and see what it would need to look like. If a variable would have to be more strongly related to both the treatment and the outcome than anything you actually measured, the finding is fairly robust. If an ordinary, unremarkable variable would be enough, it is not. That comparison -- against the covariates you did collect -- is what makes the exercise informative rather than decorative.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Nobody can prove there is no missing common cause. This asks the answerable question instead: how strong would one have to be to overturn the finding?\",\"plain_language\":\"Nobody can prove there is no missing common cause. This asks the answerable question instead: how strong would one have to be to overturn the finding?\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.add_unobserved_confounder\",\"id\":\"probe.add_unobserved_confounder\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Simulate a common cause of a stated strength and watch the estimate move; read off how strong one would have to be to explain the result away.\",\"title\":\"Unobserved confounder\"}],\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.robustness_value\",\"id\":\"robustness_value\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Robustness value\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.evalue\",\"id\":\"evalue\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"E-value\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"id\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"How strong would an unmeasured confounder have to be -- compared with the covariates you already have -- to explain the estimate away?\",\"title\":\"Cinelli-Hazlett sensitivity (partial R2)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"A shock in a single series\",\"Something else pushed people in\",\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\",\"One place, constructed twin\"],\"title\":\"Simulated unobserved confounder\",\"worry_when\":\"A confounder weaker than the ones you did measure would be enough to take the estimate to zero.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:unobserved_confounder\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"unobserved_confounder\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Nobody can prove there is no missing common cause. This asks the answerable question instead: how strong would one have to be to overturn the finding?\",\"title\":\"Simulated unobserved confounder\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aweak_iv_set> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Weak-instrument-robust set" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:weak_iv_set\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.weak_iv_set\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"weak_iv_set\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The usual two-stage least squares interval assumes the instrument is strong. When it is not, that interval is simply wrong -- too narrow, and centred in the wrong place. The Anderson-Rubin set makes no such assumption. An unbounded set is not a failure of the method: it is the correct statement that the data cannot pin the parameter down, and reporting a tidy interval instead would be the failure.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"A confidence set built by inverting a test that stays valid however weak the instrument is. It can be an interval, several intervals, or the whole line.\",\"plain_language\":\"A confidence set built by inverting a test that stays valid however weak the instrument is. It can be an interval, several intervals, or the whole line.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.weak_robust\",\"id\":\"iv.weak_robust\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the set of effects the data cannot reject, which stays honest however weak the instrument is.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust confidence set\"}],\"references\":[\"Anderson & Rubin (1949)\",\"Moreira (2003), A conditional likelihood ratio test\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.first_stage\",\"id\":\"first_stage\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"First stage\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.relevance\",\"explains\":\"The instrument actually moves treatment, and moves it enough that the resulting estimate is not mostly noise.\",\"id\":\"relevance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Instrument relevance\",\"worry_when\":\"An effective F below about 10; a first-stage coefficient that is substantively tiny even if statistically significant; many instruments.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.relevance\",\"explains\":\"The instrument actually moves treatment, and moves it enough that the resulting estimate is not mostly noise.\",\"id\":\"relevance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Instrument relevance\",\"worry_when\":\"An effective F below about 10; a first-stage coefficient that is substantively tiny even if statistically significant; many instruments.\"},\"tags\":[\"Something else pushed people in\"],\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust set\",\"worry_when\":\"The robust set is much wider than the Wald interval, or unbounded, while the Wald interval looks precise.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:weak_iv_set\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"weak_iv_set\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"A confidence set built by inverting a test that stays valid however weak the instrument is. It can be an interval, several intervals, or the whole line.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust set\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aweighted_balance> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Balance after weighting" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:weighted_balance\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.weighted_balance\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"weighted_balance\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Weighting for time-varying treatment is meant to create a pseudo-population in which the treatment decision at each period is unrelated to everything measured up to that point. This table is the check on whether it did: every covariate at every period, compared between the person-periods where treatment was given and those where it was not, after the weights are applied. The check deliberately uses the raw rather than the stabilised weights, because it is the raw ones that define the pseudo-population balance is supposed to hold in. Residual imbalance means the treatment model has not removed the confounding it was there to remove -- which is a modelling failure, and separate from the untestable assumption that everything relevant was measured at all.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"Whether, after weighting, the treated and untreated person-periods look alike on the variables the weights were built from.\",\"plain_language\":\"Whether, after weighting, the treated and untreated person-periods look alike on the variables the weights were built from.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.msm_iptw\",\"id\":\"long.msm_iptw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight each person-period so the treated and untreated histories look alike, then fit one simple model to the reweighted data.\",\"title\":\"Marginal structural model (time-varying weights)\"}],\"references\":[\"Jackson (2016), Diagnostics for confounding of time-varying and other joint exposures\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.standardised_difference\",\"id\":\"concept.standardised_difference\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"The gap between groups on a covariate, divided by a pooled standard deviation, so covariates on different scales can be compared on one axis.\",\"title\":\"Standardised mean difference\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.love\",\"id\":\"love\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Covariate balance (Love plot)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.weights_by_period\",\"id\":\"weights_by_period\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Weights by period\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_time_varying_confounding\",\"explains\":\"At every period, the things that drive the treatment decision and also affect the outcome are measured -- and measured before that decision.\",\"id\":\"no_time_varying_confounding\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No unmeasured time-varying confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Clinical measurements taken after the dose was changed; employment status recorded at the end of a period used to explain treatment at its start.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\"],\"title\":\"Balance after weighting\",\"worry_when\":\"Covariates still far apart after weighting, especially ones that strongly predict the outcome.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:weighted_balance\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"weighted_balance\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Whether, after weighting, the treated and untreated person-periods look alike on the variables the weights were built from.\",\"title\":\"Balance after weighting\"}" .
<urn:capy:diagnostic%3Aweights_by_period> a capy:diagnostic ; skos:prefLabel "Weights by period" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"diagnostic:weights_by_period\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"engine\":\"python\",\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.weights_by_period\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"weights_by_period\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Weighting for time-varying treatment multiplies one weight per period together, so the product grows more extreme the longer the follow-up. A history that was mildly unusual at each of eight time points can end up with an enormous cumulative weight, and the estimate then rests on a handful of people. Watching the effective sample fall period by period is the clearest early warning this design gives. The point estimate will not show it, and neither will the standard error, which assumes the weighting worked.\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"one_liner\":\"How much of the sample survives, in effective terms, as the weights accumulate across periods.\",\"plain_language\":\"How much of the sample survives, in effective terms, as the weights accumulate across periods.\",\"produced_by\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.lmtp\",\"id\":\"long.lmtp\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate the effect of shifting a continuous or multi-valued treatment, rather than of setting everyone to one value.\",\"title\":\"Longitudinal modified treatment policies (lmtp)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.msm_iptw\",\"id\":\"long.msm_iptw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight each person-period so the treated and untreated histories look alike, then fit one simple model to the reweighted data.\",\"title\":\"Marginal structural model (time-varying weights)\"}],\"references\":[\"Cole & Hernán (2008), Constructing inverse probability weights\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"concept.effective_sample_size\",\"id\":\"concept.effective_sample_size\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"How many observations your weighted comparison is really worth. Almost always smaller than the number of rows, sometimes dramatically.\",\"title\":\"Effective sample size\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.msm_iptw\",\"id\":\"long.msm_iptw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight each person-period so the treated and untreated histories look alike, then fit one simple model to the reweighted data.\",\"title\":\"Marginal structural model (time-varying weights)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.positivity_by_period\",\"id\":\"positivity_by_period\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Positivity by period\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"}],\"speaks_to\":{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},\"tags\":[\"Treatment that changes over time\"],\"title\":\"Weights by period\",\"worry_when\":\"The effective sample collapsing as more periods are multiplied in, or one history carrying a large share of the weight.\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"diagnostic:weights_by_period\",\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"weights_by_period\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"How much of the sample survives, in effective terms, as the weights accumulate across periods.\",\"title\":\"Weights by period\"}" .
<urn:capy:did.bjs_imputation> a capy:method ; skos:prefLabel "Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[\"method:did.bjs_imputation\"],\"catalogue_key\":\"method:did.bjs_imputation\",\"citation_ids\":[\"10.1093/restud/rdae007\",\"source:unresolved:8060396d0111d6160836\"],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"availability\":{\"coming\":null,\"engines\":{\"python\":true,\"r\":\"didimputation\"},\"needs_r_packages\":[\"didimputation\"],\"runs_here\":true,\"summary\":\"Runs on this computer with what the app already installs.\"},\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"diagnostics\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.raw_means\",\"id\":\"raw_means\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Raw means by cohort and period\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.adoption\",\"id\":\"adoption\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Adoption pattern\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.panel_balance\",\"id\":\"panel_balance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Panel balance\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.comparison_group\",\"id\":\"comparison_group\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Comparison group used\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cohort_att\",\"id\":\"cohort_att\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Cohort effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.event_study\",\"id\":\"event_study\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Event study\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.pre_trends\",\"id\":\"pre_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Pre-trend test\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.imputation_fit\",\"id\":\"imputation_fit\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Untreated-cell model fit\"}],\"estimands\":[{\"acronym\":\"ATT\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.att\",\"id\":\"ATT\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who actually got it, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the treated\"},{\"acronym\":\"ATT(g,t)\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cohort_att\",\"id\":\"cohort_ATT\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For each group that adopted it, what did their own rollout do?\",\"title\":\"Group-time average treatment effect\"}],\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"gaps\":[\"no plain-language description has been written yet, so the one-line summary is standing in for it\",\"some of the robustness probes it names have no card of their own yet\"],\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_more_depth\":null,\"kind\":\"method\",\"needs_overlap\":false,\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"options\":[{\"default\":3,\"help\":\"The comparison model is fitted on the pre-period, so pre-period 'effects' are near zero by construction unless some periods are held out. These held-out periods are what the parallel-trends test is actually run on. Units that would lose their last untreated period keep it.\",\"label\":\"Pre-adoption periods to hold out for testing\",\"max\":20,\"min\":0,\"name\":\"pre_test_periods\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"default\":12,\"help\":\"Anything later is pooled into the last point, so no observation is dropped.\",\"label\":\"Periods after adoption to show separately\",\"max\":60,\"min\":0,\"name\":\"max_lag\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"choices\":[\"additive\",\"by_period\"],\"default\":\"additive\",\"help\":\"Additive shifts the untreated path by a constant. By period lets each covariate matter differently in each period, which is the Wooldridge-Mundlak form and much more flexible -- and much hungrier for data.\",\"label\":\"How covariates enter the comparison model\",\"name\":\"covariate_mode\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"default\":0.95,\"help\":\"Width of the interval reported next to the estimate.\",\"label\":\"Confidence level\",\"max\":0.999,\"min\":0.5,\"name\":\"ci_level\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"number\"}],\"plain_language\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"probes\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"id\":\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same analysis on something the treatment could not have changed. It should find nothing.\",\"requested_as\":\"placebo_outcome\",\"title\":\"Placebo outcome\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"placebo_period\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":null,\"requested_as\":\"placebo_period\",\"title\":\"Placebo period\"},{\"explain_key\":null,\"id\":\"leave_one_cohort_out\",\"in_catalogue\":false,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":null,\"requested_as\":\"leave_one_cohort_out\",\"title\":\"Leave one adoption cohort out\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"id\":\"probe.honest_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"What survives when parallel trends is allowed to fail by a stated amount.\",\"requested_as\":\"honest_did\",\"title\":\"Honest DiD (Rambachan-Roth)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.alternate_spec\",\"id\":\"probe.alternate_spec\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the grid of defensible specifications and sort the answers. The curve is the result, not the one you happened to run.\",\"requested_as\":\"alternate_spec\",\"title\":\"Specification curve\"}],\"references\":[\"Borusyak, Jaravel and Spiess (2024), Revisiting event-study designs: robust and efficient estimation\",\"Gardner (2022), Two-stage differences in differences\"],\"roles_forbidden\":[\"a running variable\",\"an instrument\"],\"roles_optional\":[\"a clustering variable\",\"at least one measured confounder\",\"weight\"],\"roles_required\":[\"a treatment\",\"an outcome\",\"a unit id\",\"a time variable\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.chaisemartin\",\"id\":\"did.chaisemartin\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"An estimator built for panels where treatment switches on and off, which the imputation and cohort estimators assume never happens.\",\"title\":\"de Chaisemartin & D'Haultfoeuille\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.dr_did\",\"id\":\"did.dr_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One before-and-after with covariates, using an outcome model and a comparison-group model together so either one can carry it.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust DiD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.event_study\",\"id\":\"did.event_study\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Plot the effect period by period around adoption, so you can see whether the gap opened when the policy arrived or long before it.\",\"title\":\"Event study (leads and lags)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.gardner_2s\",\"id\":\"did.gardner_2s\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Strip out unit and period effects using untreated cells only, then regress what is left on the policy.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage DiD (Gardner / Wooldridge-Mundlak)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.goodman_bacon\",\"id\":\"did.goodman_bacon\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Open up a two-way fixed effects estimate and show every two-group comparison inside it, with the weight each one carries -- including the ones that use already-treated units as controls.\",\"title\":\"Goodman-Bacon decomposition\"}],\"status\":\"recommended\",\"status_label\":\"Recommended\",\"tags\":[\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"ATT\",\"ATT(g,t)\",\"Recommended\"],\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\",\"what_it_assumes\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.parallel_trends\",\"explains\":\"Without the policy, treated and comparison groups would have moved the same way. Not that they were at the same level -- that their paths would have been parallel.\",\"id\":\"parallel_trends\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Parallel trends\",\"worry_when\":\"Pre-period event-study coefficients drift away from zero; the policy was adopted because outcomes were already moving; the groups differ in composition in ways that interact with time.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_anticipation\",\"explains\":\"Units did not change behaviour before treatment arrived because they knew it was coming.\",\"id\":\"no_anticipation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No anticipation\",\"worry_when\":\"There was a public announcement well before the policy started, or the pre-period coefficients move in the period immediately before adoption.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.sutva\",\"explains\":\"One unit's treatment does not change another unit's outcome, and \\\"treated\\\" means the same thing for everyone who got it.\",\"id\":\"sutva\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"SUTVA / no interference\",\"worry_when\":\"Units interact: they compete for the same jobs, infect each other, share a classroom or a market. Or the \\\"programme\\\" is really a family of programmes whose intensity varied a lot across sites.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.consistency\",\"explains\":\"The outcome you observe under the treatment a unit received is the same as the potential outcome under that treatment. In practice: the treatment has to be a thing someone could actually do.\",\"id\":\"consistency\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Consistency / well-defined intervention\",\"worry_when\":\"The treatment is a state of the world rather than an action, or it is coded from several very different real interventions.\"}],\"when_not_to_use\":[\"It leans harder on the additive unit-plus-period model than the 2x2 estimators do -- if untreated paths are not parallel, that shows up as effect rather than as a failed diagnostic. Pre-period effects are zero by construction unless periods are held out, so a test on a model that saw the pre-period is not a test.\"],\"when_to_use\":[\"It uses every untreated cell in the panel to build the counterfactual, which makes it the most efficient of the staggered estimators when the unit-and-period model is right. The logic is also the easiest to say out loud: what happened, minus what the untreated cells say would have.\"]},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[\"no plain-language description has been written yet, so the one-line summary is standing in for it\",\"some of the robustness probes it names have no card of their own yet\"],\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"kind\":\"method\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"registry\":{\"designs\":[\"did\"],\"diagnostics\":[\"raw_means\",\"adoption\",\"panel_balance\",\"comparison_group\",\"cohort_att\",\"event_study\",\"pre_trends\",\"imputation_fit\"],\"disrecommend_when\":null,\"engine_support_basis\":\"Static adapter decorator and declared package metadata; installed availability not checked.\",\"engines\":{\"python\":true,\"r\":\"didimputation\"},\"estimands\":[\"ATT\",\"cohort_ATT\"],\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"needs\":[\"numpy\",\"scipy\",\"pandas\"],\"needs_overlap\":false,\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"options\":[{\"default\":3,\"help\":\"The comparison model is fitted on the pre-period, so pre-period 'effects' are near zero by construction unless some periods are held out. These held-out periods are what the parallel-trends test is actually run on. Units that would lose their last untreated period keep it.\",\"label\":\"Pre-adoption periods to hold out for testing\",\"max\":20,\"min\":0,\"name\":\"pre_test_periods\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"default\":12,\"help\":\"Anything later is pooled into the last point, so no observation is dropped.\",\"label\":\"Periods after adoption to show separately\",\"max\":60,\"min\":0,\"name\":\"max_lag\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"choices\":[\"additive\",\"by_period\"],\"default\":\"additive\",\"help\":\"Additive shifts the untreated path by a constant. By period lets each covariate matter differently in each period, which is the Wooldridge-Mundlak form and much more flexible -- and much hungrier for data.\",\"label\":\"How covariates enter the comparison model\",\"name\":\"covariate_mode\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"default\":0.95,\"help\":\"Width of the interval reported next to the estimate.\",\"label\":\"Confidence level\",\"max\":0.999,\"min\":0.5,\"name\":\"ci_level\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"number\"}],\"probes\":[\"placebo_outcome\",\"placebo_period\",\"leave_one_cohort_out\",\"honest_did\",\"alternate_spec\"],\"reference_dispositions\":[{\"disposition\":\"resolved_metadata\",\"id\":\"10.1093/restud/rdae007\",\"requested_as\":\"Borusyak, Jaravel and Spiess (2024), Revisiting event-study designs: robust and efficient estimation\"},{\"disposition\":\"unresolved_search_only\",\"id\":\"source:unresolved:8060396d0111d6160836\",\"requested_as\":\"Gardner (2022), Two-stage differences in differences\"}],\"reference_ids\":[\"10.1093/restud/rdae007\",\"source:unresolved:8060396d0111d6160836\"],\"references\":[\"Borusyak, Jaravel and Spiess (2024), Revisiting event-study designs: robust and efficient estimation\",\"Gardner (2022), Two-stage differences in differences\"],\"roles_forbidden\":[\"running\",\"instruments\"],\"roles_optional\":[\"cluster\",\"confounders\",\"weight\"],\"roles_required\":[\"treatment\",\"outcome\",\"unit\",\"time\"],\"source_path\":\"engines/python/capy_py/did_modern.py\",\"source_sha256\":\"f5546f580cd2c75174a6cb550cf3b66ca1a008151067f52513cafaa766ebfe16\",\"status\":\"recommended\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\",\"what_can_go_wrong\":\"It leans harder on the additive unit-plus-period model than the 2x2 estimators do -- if untreated paths are not parallel, that shows up as effect rather than as a failed diagnostic. Pre-period effects are zero by construction unless periods are held out, so a test on a model that saw the pre-period is not a test.\",\"why_recommended\":\"It uses every untreated cell in the panel to build the counterfactual, which makes it the most efficient of the staggered estimators when the unit-and-period model is right. The logic is also the easiest to say out loud: what happened, minus what the untreated cells say would have.\"},\"relations\":[{\"assertion_mode\":\"editorial\",\"conditions\":[],\"counterevidence\":[],\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"relation:001b5f720c1a1b46a51d641b\",\"object_id\":\"diagnostic:event_study\",\"predicate\":\"checked_by_diagnostic\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"scope\":\"Declared project registry/navigation relationship; not an identified causal effect.\",\"subject_id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\"},{\"assertion_mode\":\"editorial\",\"conditions\":[],\"counterevidence\":[],\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"relation:014b00a0b3d0d46a704d297d\",\"object_id\":\"softwarepackage:didimputation\",\"predicate\":\"implemented_by\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"scope\":\"Declared project registry/navigation relationship; not an identified causal effect.\",\"subject_id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\"},{\"assertion_mode\":\"editorial\",\"conditions\":[],\"counterevidence\":[],\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"relation:021d8bce538ce0caef83d06d\",\"object_id\":\"design:did\",\"predicate\":\"related_to\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICEN