not a reviewed implementation or equivalence assertion.\",\"subject_id\":\"discovery.structure_learning\"},{\"assertion_mode\":\"editorial\",\"conditions\":[\"Interpret the relation within the stated design, population and observation process.\"],\"counterevidence\":[],\"gaps\":[\"This editorial link does not certify semantic entailment or implementation correctness.\"],\"id\":\"relation:cefff06dabd8de14c67e28de\",\"object_id\":\"concept:positivity\",\"predicate\":\"related_to\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-ontology\",\"rosenbaum1983\"],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"basis\":[\"Original explanation and primary reference context; not a completed implementation audit.\"],\"date\":\"2026-10-13\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"agent_editorial_context\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"scope\":\"Editorial methodological context for this registry entry; not a reviewed implementation or equivalence assertion.\",\"subject_id\":\"discovery.structure_learning\"},{\"assertion_mode\":\"editorial\",\"conditions\":[\"Interpret the relation within the stated design, population and observation process.\"],\"counterevidence\":[],\"gaps\":[\"This editorial link does not certify semantic entailment or implementation correctness.\"],\"id\":\"relation:dc3205408f022dc593d1222b\",\"object_id\":\"concept:causal-discovery\",\"predicate\":\"related_to\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-ontology\",\"source:ontology:pearl2009\"],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"basis\":[\"Original explanation and primary reference context; not a completed implementation audit.\"],\"date\":\"2026-10-13\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"agent_editorial_context\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"scope\":\"Editorial methodological context for this registry entry; not a reviewed implementation or equivalence assertion.\",\"subject_id\":\"discovery.structure_learning\"},{\"assertion_mode\":\"editorial\",\"conditions\":[],\"counterevidence\":[],\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"relation:fe6b8f76fc709cd4546e38f6\",\"object_id\":\"assumption:consistency\",\"predicate\":\"requires_assumption\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"scope\":\"Declared project registry/navigation relationship; not an identified causal effect.\",\"subject_id\":\"discovery.structure_learning\"}],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Search for a graph consistent with the data. It does not recover the true causal structure from observational data without extra assumptions.\",\"title\":\"Structure learning (causal discovery)\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3AATC> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "Average treatment effect on the controls" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"estimand:ATC\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"acronym\":\"ATC\",\"common_mistake\":\"Extrapolating to untreated units that look nothing like any treated unit.\",\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"explain_key\":\"estimand.atc\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"ATC\",\"in_more_depth\":\"This is the expansion question: if you extended an existing programme to the people currently outside it, what could you expect? It requires the comparison to be credible in the opposite direction -- treated units that resemble the untreated -- which is often the thinner side of the overlap.\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"methods\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.nn\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.nn\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Pair each treated unit with the most similar untreated unit, then compare within pairs.\",\"title\":\"Nearest-neighbour matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.outcome_regression\",\"id\":\"obs.outcome_regression\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the outcome, then ask what would have happened if everyone had been treated, and if nobody had.\",\"title\":\"Outcome regression (g-computation)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.ipw\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.ipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight the sample so the treated and untreated groups look alike on the measured confounders.\",\"title\":\"Propensity weighting\"}],\"notation\":\"E[Y(1) - Y(0) | D = 0]\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who didn't get it, what would it have done?\",\"plain_language\":\"For the units that did not get the programme, what would it have done to them?\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"ATT\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.att\",\"id\":\"ATT\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who actually got it, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the treated\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.nn\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.nn\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Pair each treated unit with the most similar untreated unit, then compare within pairs.\",\"title\":\"Nearest-neighbour matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.outcome_regression\",\"id\":\"obs.outcome_regression\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the outcome, then ask what would have happened if everyone had been treated, and if nobody had.\",\"title\":\"Outcome regression (g-computation)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.ipw\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.ipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight the sample so the treated and untreated groups look alike on the measured confounders.\",\"title\":\"Propensity weighting\"}],\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the controls\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"estimand:ATC\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"ATC\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"For the units that did not get the programme, what would it have done to them?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the controls\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3AATE> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "Average treatment effect" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"estimand:ATE\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"acronym\":\"ATE\",\"common_mistake\":\"Reporting an ATE from a design that only identifies the effect for the treated, or for compliers.\",\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"}],\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ate\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"ATE\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The most natural quantity and often the hardest to identify. It requires the comparison to be credible for every kind of unit, including those who would never realistically be treated, so it leans hardest on overlap. Where the treated and untreated groups barely resemble each other, the ATE is estimated partly by extrapolation, and an effect on the overlap population may be the more honest target.\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"methods\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.bounds.manski\",\"id\":\"bounds.manski\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the range the effect must lie in given only what the data can support, without assuming away the missing counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Manski bounds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.interference.exposure\",\"id\":\"interference.exposure\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When one unit's treatment affects another's outcome, estimate the direct and spillover effects separately instead of pretending neither exists.\",\"title\":\"Interference and spillovers\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.mte\",\"id\":\"iv.mte\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Trace the effect as a function of how reluctant a unit is to be treated, instead of reporting one local average.\",\"title\":\"Marginal treatment effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.shift_share\",\"id\":\"iv.shift_share\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Combine national shocks with local exposure shares to build an instrument for local changes.\",\"title\":\"Shift-share (Bartik) instruments\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.g_estimation\",\"id\":\"long.g_estimation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate the effect of a treatment regime by finding the parameter that removes the association between treatment and the blipped-down outcome.\",\"title\":\"G-estimation of structural nested models\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.gformula\",\"id\":\"long.gformula\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model each period in turn, then simulate the world where everyone followed the strategy you name.\",\"title\":\"Parametric g-formula\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.lmtp\",\"id\":\"long.lmtp\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate the effect of shifting a continuous or multi-valued treatment, rather than of setting everyone to one value.\",\"title\":\"Longitudinal modified treatment policies (lmtp)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Regress backwards from the final outcome, then correct with the treatment probabilities: right if either set of models is right.\",\"title\":\"Sequential doubly robust (ICE g-formula with augmentation)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle_survival\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle_survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Time-to-event outcomes with time-varying treatment and censoring, targeted at each time.\",\"title\":\"Longitudinal TMLE for survival outcomes\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.msm_iptw\",\"id\":\"long.msm_iptw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight each person-period so the treated and untreated histories look alike, then fit one simple model to the reweighted data.\",\"title\":\"Marginal structural model (time-varying weights)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.interventional\",\"id\":\"med.interventional\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Shift the mediator to the distribution it would have in the population, rather than to each person's own unobservable counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Interventional direct and indirect effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.med.natural_effects\",\"id\":\"med.natural_effects\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Split the total effect into the part that travelled through the mediator and the part that did not.\",\"title\":\"Natural direct and indirect effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_plr\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_plr\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Predict the outcome and the treatment from the confounders, then regress what is left of one on what is left of the other.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (partially linear)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.aipw\",\"id\":\"obs.aipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use a treatment model and an outcome model together; it is right if either one is.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust (AIPW)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.cem\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.cem\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Bin the covariates, then compare only inside bins that contain both arms.\",\"title\":\"Coarsened exact matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.genetic\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.genetic\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Search over distance-metric weights until balance is as good as it can get, instead of trusting one metric.\",\"title\":\"Genetic matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.optimal\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.optimal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Choose the whole set of matches at once to minimise total distance, rather than greedily one treated unit at a time.\",\"title\":\"Optimal and full matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.missing.mice\",\"id\":\"obs.missing.mice\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Impute the missing values several times, run the analysis in each, and combine -- instead of dropping every row with a gap.\",\"title\":\"Multiple imputation compatible with the analysis\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.outcome_regression\",\"id\":\"obs.outcome_regression\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the outcome, then ask what would have happened if everyone had been treated, and if nobody had.\",\"title\":\"Outcome regression (g-computation)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.superlearner\",\"id\":\"obs.superlearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Combine several candidate learners by cross-validated weights instead of betting on one.\",\"title\":\"Super Learner nuisance models\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.cbps\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.cbps\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the treatment model so that it balances covariates, rather than fitting it to predict treatment and hoping balance follows.\",\"title\":\"Covariate balancing propensity score\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.ipw\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.ipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight the sample so the treated and untreated groups look alike on the measured confounders.\",\"title\":\"Propensity weighting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.twang\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.twang\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow a boosted model for the propensity score, stopping at the iteration that balances covariates best.\",\"title\":\"Boosted propensity scores (twang)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cluster\",\"id\":\"rct.cluster\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When whole schools, clinics or villages were assigned, the number of clusters is the sample size that matters.\",\"title\":\"Cluster-randomised trial\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.diff_means\",\"id\":\"rct.diff_means\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the two arms as they were randomised, and let covariates sharpen the comparison without letting them change the question.\",\"title\":\"Difference in means\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.randomization_inference\",\"id\":\"rct.randomization_inference\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-run the randomisation thousands of times with the outcomes held fixed, and see how unusual what actually happened really is.\",\"title\":\"Randomisation (Fisher) inference\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.stratified\",\"id\":\"rct.stratified\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate inside the blocks the randomisation was run in, then average them the way your estimand says to.\",\"title\":\"Blocked or stratified experiment\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_randomization\",\"id\":\"rd.local_randomization\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat a narrow window around the cutoff as a small randomised experiment rather than fitting a curve through it.\",\"title\":\"Local randomisation RD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.transport.generalize\",\"id\":\"transport.generalize\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Take an effect estimated in one population and ask what it would be in another with a different mix of characteristics.\",\"title\":\"Transportability and generalisability\"}],\"notation\":\"E[Y(1) - Y(0)]\",\"one_liner\":\"If everyone got the programme, how would the average outcome change?\",\"plain_language\":\"If everyone in the population had been treated, compared with nobody being treated, how would the average outcome differ?\",\"profile\":\"beginner\",\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"ATT\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.att\",\"id\":\"ATT\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who actually got it, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the treated\"},{\"acronym\":\"ATO\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ato\",\"id\":\"ATO\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For the people where treated and untreated genuinely overlap, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the overlap population\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.mediation\",\"id\":\"mediation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"We care about a mediator, not only the total effect.\",\"title\":\"How it worked (pathways)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.bounds.manski\",\"id\":\"bounds.manski\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the range the effect must lie in given only what the data can support, without assuming away the missing counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Manski bounds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.interference.exposure\",\"id\":\"interference.exposure\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When one unit's treatment affects another's outcome, estimate the direct and spillover effects separately instead of pretending neither exists.\",\"title\":\"Interference and spillovers\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"}],\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Something else pushed people in\",\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"How it worked (pathways)\"],\"title\":\"Average treatment effect\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"estimand:ATE\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"ATE\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"If everyone in the population had been treated, compared with nobody being treated, how would the average outcome differ?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3AATO> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "Average treatment effect on the overlap population" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"estimand:ATO\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"acronym\":\"ATO\",\"common_mistake\":\"Reporting it as if it were the ATE for a policy-relevant population.\",\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ato\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"ATO\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Overlap weights put most weight where the probability of treatment is near a half -- the units for whom the comparison is best supported -- and almost none where treatment was nearly certain either way. The result has the smallest variance among weighted estimators and never has an exploding weight. The price is that the population is defined by the data rather than by policy. It is an excellent stability check and an awkward headline: \\\"the effect among the people where comparison was possible\\\" is honest, but it is not a group a minister can point at.\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"methods\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.cbps\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.cbps\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the treatment model so that it balances covariates, rather than fitting it to predict treatment and hoping balance follows.\",\"title\":\"Covariate balancing propensity score\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.ipw\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.ipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight the sample so the treated and untreated groups look alike on the measured confounders.\",\"title\":\"Propensity weighting\"}],\"notation\":\"weights: (1 - e) for treated, e for untreated\",\"one_liner\":\"For the people where treated and untreated genuinely overlap, what did it do?\",\"plain_language\":\"The effect for the units where treated and untreated genuinely overlap, weighted so that the comparison is as stable as it can be.\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"references\":[\"Li, Morgan & Zaslavsky (2018), Balancing covariates via propensity score weighting\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.ess\",\"id\":\"ess\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Effective sample size\"},{\"acronym\":\"ATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ate\",\"id\":\"ATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"If everyone got the programme, how would the average outcome change?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.cbps\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.cbps\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the treatment model so that it balances covariates, rather than fitting it to predict treatment and hoping balance follows.\",\"title\":\"Covariate balancing propensity score\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.ipw\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.ipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight the sample so the treated and untreated groups look alike on the measured confounders.\",\"title\":\"Propensity weighting\"}],\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\"],\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the overlap population\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"estimand:ATO\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"ATO\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The effect for the units where treated and untreated genuinely overlap, weighted so that the comparison is as stable as it can be.\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the overlap population\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3AATT> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "Average treatment effect on the treated" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"estimand:ATT\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"acronym\":\"ATT\",\"common_mistake\":\"Assuming the ATT equals the ATE when treated units differ from untreated ones -- which is exactly why they needed adjusting.\",\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"explain_key\":\"estimand.att\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"ATT\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Usually the policy-relevant number for an existing programme: it describes the people the programme actually reached. It also asks less of the data than the ATE, because you only need comparison units that resemble the treated, not the other way round. It is the default for matching and for difference-in-differences, and it is what the sample flow is quietly protecting: if matching drops a fifth of the treated units, the ATT is now for the four fifths that remained, and that is a different population.\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"methods\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.bounds.manski\",\"id\":\"bounds.manski\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the range the effect must lie in given only what the data can support, without assuming away the missing counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Manski bounds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.chaisemartin\",\"id\":\"did.chaisemartin\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"An estimator built for panels where treatment switches on and off, which the imputation and cohort estimators assume never happens.\",\"title\":\"de Chaisemartin & D'Haultfoeuille\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.dr_did\",\"id\":\"did.dr_did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One before-and-after with covariates, using an outcome model and a comparison-group model together so either one can carry it.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust DiD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.event_study\",\"id\":\"did.event_study\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Plot the effect period by period around adoption, so you can see whether the gap opened when the policy arrived or long before it.\",\"title\":\"Event study (leads and lags)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.gardner_2s\",\"id\":\"did.gardner_2s\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Strip out unit and period effects using untreated cells only, then regress what is left on the policy.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage DiD (Gardner / Wooldridge-Mundlak)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.goodman_bacon\",\"id\":\"did.goodman_bacon\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Open up a two-way fixed effects estimate and show every two-group comparison inside it, with the weight each one carries -- including the ones that use already-treated units as controls.\",\"title\":\"Goodman-Bacon decomposition\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.matched\",\"id\":\"did.matched\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Match treated to comparison units on pre-period characteristics first, then difference.\",\"title\":\"Matched difference-in-differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.sun_abraham\",\"id\":\"did.sun_abraham\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Give every cohort its own event-study path, then average the paths by cohort size instead of letting the regression choose the weights.\",\"title\":\"Sun & Abraham event study\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.synthdid_r\",\"id\":\"did.synthdid_r\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight both units and periods, then take a difference-in-differences -- halfway between synthetic control and DiD.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences (synthdid)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.triple_diff\",\"id\":\"did.triple_diff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the treated-versus-comparison difference for the group the policy could reach with the same difference for a group inside the same units that it could not.\",\"title\":\"Triple differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twfe\",\"id\":\"did.twfe\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"One regression with unit and period fixed effects. Familiar, fast, and not an average treatment effect when units adopt at different dates.\",\"title\":\"Two-way fixed effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.twoway_2x2\",\"id\":\"did.twoway_2x2\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Two groups, two periods: compare how much the treated group changed with how much the comparison group changed over the same window.\",\"title\":\"Difference-in-differences (2x2)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.interference.exposure\",\"id\":\"interference.exposure\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When one unit's treatment affects another's outcome, estimate the direct and spillover effects separately instead of pretending neither exists.\",\"title\":\"Interference and spillovers\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.arima\",\"id\":\"its.arima\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the memory in the series itself, then ask how big a step, pulse or ramp the data need at the intervention date.\",\"title\":\"ARIMA with an intervention\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.causal_impact\",\"id\":\"its.causal_impact\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the counterfactual series with a structural time-series model and control series, and report a posterior rather than an interval.\",\"title\":\"Bayesian structural time series (CausalImpact)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.controlled\",\"id\":\"its.controlled\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The same interruption, measured against a series the intervention did not touch, so a shock that hit everybody is not booked as your effect.\",\"title\":\"Controlled ITS\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.its.segmented\",\"id\":\"its.segmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the trend before the interruption, project it forward, and measure the jump and the change of direction after it.\",\"title\":\"Segmented regression\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.mte\",\"id\":\"iv.mte\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Trace the effect as a function of how reluctant a unit is to be treated, instead of reporting one local average.\",\"title\":\"Marginal treatment effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.g_estimation\",\"id\":\"long.g_estimation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate the effect of a treatment regime by finding the parameter that removes the association between treatment and the blipped-down outcome.\",\"title\":\"G-estimation of structural nested models\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.long.ltmle_survival\",\"id\":\"long.ltmle_survival\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Time-to-event outcomes with time-varying treatment and censoring, targeted at each time.\",\"title\":\"Longitudinal TMLE for survival outcomes\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.aipw\",\"id\":\"obs.aipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use a treatment model and an outcome model together; it is right if either one is.\",\"title\":\"Doubly robust (AIPW)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.cem\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.cem\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Bin the covariates, then compare only inside bins that contain both arms.\",\"title\":\"Coarsened exact matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.genetic\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.genetic\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Search over distance-metric weights until balance is as good as it can get, instead of trusting one metric.\",\"title\":\"Genetic matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.nn\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.nn\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Pair each treated unit with the most similar untreated unit, then compare within pairs.\",\"title\":\"Nearest-neighbour matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.matching.optimal\",\"id\":\"obs.matching.optimal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Choose the whole set of matches at once to minimise total distance, rather than greedily one treated unit at a time.\",\"title\":\"Optimal and full matching\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.missing.mice\",\"id\":\"obs.missing.mice\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Impute the missing values several times, run the analysis in each, and combine -- instead of dropping every row with a gap.\",\"title\":\"Multiple imputation compatible with the analysis\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.outcome_regression\",\"id\":\"obs.outcome_regression\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Model the outcome, then ask what would have happened if everyone had been treated, and if nobody had.\",\"title\":\"Outcome regression (g-computation)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.superlearner\",\"id\":\"obs.superlearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Combine several candidate learners by cross-validated weights instead of betting on one.\",\"title\":\"Super Learner nuisance models\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.cbps\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.cbps\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit the treatment model so that it balances covariates, rather than fitting it to predict treatment and hoping balance follows.\",\"title\":\"Covariate balancing propensity score\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.entropy\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.entropy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Find the weights closest to equal that make the covariate means match exactly.\",\"title\":\"Entropy balancing\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.ipw\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.ipw\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Reweight the sample so the treated and untreated groups look alike on the measured confounders.\",\"title\":\"Propensity weighting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.weighting.twang\",\"id\":\"obs.weighting.twang\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow a boosted model for the propensity score, stopping at the iteration that balances covariates best.\",\"title\":\"Boosted propensity scores (twang)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.abadie\",\"id\":\"sc.abadie\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build a weighted copy of the treated place out of untreated places that looked like it beforehand, then watch the two lines separate.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.augmented\",\"id\":\"sc.augmented\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When no weighted average of the donors can match the treated place, correct the leftover bias with a ridge model and show the size of the correction.\",\"title\":\"Augmented synthetic control\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.matrix_completion\",\"id\":\"sc.matrix_completion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat the missing treated-period outcomes as holes in a panel and fill them in with a low-rank model of what the panel would have done.\",\"title\":\"Matrix completion\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.microsynth\",\"id\":\"sc.microsynth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Synthetic control when the treated unit is a neighbourhood or a small area and there are many of them.\",\"title\":\"Microsynth (many treated units)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.scpi\",\"id\":\"sc.scpi\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Confidence intervals for a synthetic control that come from a model of the prediction error rather than from permutation.\",\"title\":\"Prediction intervals for synthetic control (scpi)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.sc.synthdid\",\"id\":\"sc.synthdid\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the comparison units and the pre-treatment periods, then run a difference in differences on the result - a level gap is allowed, a trend gap is not.\",\"title\":\"Synthetic difference-in-differences\"}],\"notation\":\"E[Y(1) - Y(0) | D = 1]\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who actually got it, what did it do?\",\"plain_language\":\"Among the units that actually got the programme, how much did it change their outcome compared with what would have happened without it?\",\"profile\":\"beginner\",\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"ATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ate\",\"id\":\"ATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"If everyone got the programme, how would the average outcome change?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect\"},{\"acronym\":\"ATC\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.atc\",\"id\":\"ATC\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who didn't get it, what would it have done?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the controls\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.consort\",\"id\":\"concept.consort\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"A step-by-step record of how the imported rows became the analysis sample, with a reason for every row that left.\",\"title\":\"The sample flow\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.synth\",\"id\":\"synth\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"One treated unit; build a comparison from a donor pool.\",\"title\":\"One place, constructed twin\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.its\",\"id\":\"its\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Before versus after an interruption; maybe a control series.\",\"title\":\"A shock in a single series\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.longitudinal\",\"id\":\"longitudinal\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"The same person is treated, untreated, treated again; confounders also move.\",\"title\":\"Treatment that changes over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.bounds.manski\",\"id\":\"bounds.manski\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the range the effect must lie in given only what the data can support, without assuming away the missing counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Manski bounds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.bjs_imputation\",\"id\":\"did.bjs_imputation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn what untreated life looks like from untreated cells only, then ask how far each treated cell landed from it.\",\"title\":\"Imputation (Borusyak-Jaravel-Spiess)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.callaway_santanna\",\"id\":\"did.callaway_santanna\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate one clean before-and-after for every adoption cohort, then say out loud how you averaged them.\",\"title\":\"Callaway & Sant'Anna group-time effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.did.chaisemartin\",\"id\":\"did.chaisemartin\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"An estimator built for panels where treatment switches on and off, which the imputation and cohort estimators assume never happens.\",\"title\":\"de Chaisemartin & D'Haultfoeuille\"}],\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Policy rolled out over time\",\"One place, constructed twin\",\"A shock in a single series\",\"Treatment that changes over time\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"],\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the treated\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"estimand:ATT\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"ATT\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Among the units that actually got the programme, how much did it change their outcome compared with what would have happened without it?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the treated\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3ACACE> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "Complier average causal effect" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"estimand:CACE\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"acronym\":\"CACE\",\"common_mistake\":\"Reporting the CACE when the policy question is about everyone offered the programme -- that is the ITT.\",\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cace\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"CACE\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The LATE by another name, in the setting where the instrument is random assignment. It scales the intention-to-treat effect by the share of people whose behaviour the offer changed. Both numbers are correct and they answer different questions. If the policy you are evaluating can only make an offer, the ITT is the effect of the policy. The CACE is the effect of the treatment on the people who accept it, which is what a clinician wants to know.\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"methods\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.weak_robust\",\"id\":\"iv.weak_robust\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the set of effects the data cannot reject, which stays honest however weak the instrument is.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust confidence set\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cace\",\"id\":\"rct.cace\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Not everyone offered a programme takes it. Report what the offer did, and separately what taking it up did for the people it moved.\",\"title\":\"ITT and CACE (noncompliance)\"}],\"notation\":\"ITT_Y / ITT_D\",\"one_liner\":\"For the people who comply with what they were offered, what did it do?\",\"plain_language\":\"In a randomised encouragement design, the effect among the people who took the treatment because they were offered it.\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"ITT\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.itt\",\"id\":\"ITT\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For everyone who was offered it, what did the offer do?\",\"title\":\"Intention to treat\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.monotonicity\",\"explains\":\"The instrument pushes everyone the same way, or not at all. Nobody takes the treatment only because they were discouraged from it.\",\"id\":\"monotonicity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Monotonicity / no defiers\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is an incentive that could plausibly backfire for some people, or it is a judge or examiner whose leniency ordering differs across case types.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cace\",\"id\":\"rct.cace\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Not everyone offered a programme takes it. Report what the offer did, and separately what taking it up did for the people it moved.\",\"title\":\"ITT and CACE (noncompliance)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.weak_robust\",\"id\":\"iv.weak_robust\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the set of effects the data cannot reject, which stays honest however weak the instrument is.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust confidence set\"}],\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"Something else pushed people in\"],\"title\":\"Complier average causal effect\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"estimand:CACE\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"CACE\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"In a randomised encouragement design, the effect among the people who took the treatment because they were offered it.\",\"title\":\"Complier average causal effect\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3ACATE> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "Conditional average treatment effect" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"estimand:CATE\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"acronym\":\"CATE\",\"common_mistake\":\"Reading noise as heterogeneity. Honest splitting and calibration checks exist for this reason.\",\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cate\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"CATE\",\"in_more_depth\":\"The most useful and most abused quantity in the catalogue. A flexible model will always produce varying predictions, including from noise, so the question is never \\\"is there heterogeneity\\\" but \\\"is this ranking real\\\". Two checks make it credible. Calibration asks whether the units predicted to benefit most actually did. The rank-weighted effect asks whether treating in the model's recommended order beats treating at random, and gives a standard error for the answer. Honest splitting -- choosing where the subgroups are on different rows from the ones that estimate their effects -- is what stops the estimate being optimistic by construction.\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"methods\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.binary\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.binary\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Logistic interactions plus separate standardized risk-difference, risk-ratio or marginal odds-ratio heterogeneity tests.\",\"title\":\"Binary outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.categorical\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.categorical\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Multinomial treatment interactions and a joint test of category-probability contrasts; preserves categories without assigning numeric scores.\",\"title\":\"Nominal and ordinal outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.continuous\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.continuous\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"OLS treatment-by-baseline-modifier interactions with HC3 or cluster sandwich inference, direct mean-difference tests and Holm pairwise comparisons.\",\"title\":\"Continuous outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.count\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.count\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Poisson conditional-mean interactions with robust sandwich inference; compare standardized count or fixed-exposure rate contrasts.\",\"title\":\"Count and rate outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.policy_tree\",\"id\":\"ml.policy_tree\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Learn a short, readable rule for who to treat, then value that rule on data it never saw.\",\"title\":\"Policy tree (who should be treated)\"}],\"notation\":null,\"one_liner\":\"How does the effect vary from person to person, or place to place?\",\"plain_language\":\"The effect as a function of characteristics: who benefits, and by how much.\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"references\":[\"Athey & Imbens (2016)\",\"Chernozhukov et al. (2018), Generic machine learning inference\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cate_calibration\",\"id\":\"cate_calibration\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"CATE calibration\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rate\",\"id\":\"rate\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Rank-weighted average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.honest_splitting\",\"id\":\"concept.honest_splitting\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Use one half of the data to decide where the subgroups are and the other half to estimate their effects.\",\"title\":\"Honest splitting\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.binary\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.binary\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Logistic interactions plus separate standardized risk-difference, risk-ratio or marginal odds-ratio heterogeneity tests.\",\"title\":\"Binary outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.categorical\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.categorical\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Multinomial treatment interactions and a joint test of category-probability contrasts; preserves categories without assigning numeric scores.\",\"title\":\"Nominal and ordinal outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.continuous\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.continuous\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"OLS treatment-by-baseline-modifier interactions with HC3 or cluster sandwich inference, direct mean-difference tests and Holm pairwise comparisons.\",\"title\":\"Continuous outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.count\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.count\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Poisson conditional-mean interactions with robust sandwich inference; compare standardized count or fixed-exposure rate contrasts.\",\"title\":\"Count and rate outcome heterogeneity\"}],\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Conditional average treatment effect\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"estimand:CATE\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"CATE\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The effect as a function of characteristics: who benefits, and by how much.\",\"title\":\"Conditional average treatment effect\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3ACDE> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "CDE" ; capy:record "{\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"disposition\":\"unresolved\",\"gaps\":[\"No complete catalogue record exists in this snapshot.\"],\"id\":\"estimand:CDE\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"raw_id\":\"CDE\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Registry marker or referenced item without a maintained catalogue description.\",\"title\":\"CDE\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3AGATE> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "Group average treatment effect" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"estimand:GATE\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"acronym\":\"GATE\",\"common_mistake\":\"Naming the groups after seeing the results.\",\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"}],\"explain_key\":\"estimand.gate\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"GATE\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Pre-specification is what separates a group effect from a fishing expedition. With a handful of groups defined in advance -- by age band, by region, by baseline severity -- the estimates are interpretable and the multiplicity correction is manageable. Groups discovered in the data are a different object, and belong to the CATE machinery with its calibration checks, not to a table of subgroup p-values.\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"methods\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.binary\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.binary\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Logistic interactions plus separate standardized risk-difference, risk-ratio or marginal odds-ratio heterogeneity tests.\",\"title\":\"Binary outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.categorical\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.categorical\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Multinomial treatment interactions and a joint test of category-probability contrasts; preserves categories without assigning numeric scores.\",\"title\":\"Nominal and ordinal outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.continuous\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.continuous\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"OLS treatment-by-baseline-modifier interactions with HC3 or cluster sandwich inference, direct mean-difference tests and Holm pairwise comparisons.\",\"title\":\"Continuous outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.count\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.count\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Poisson conditional-mean interactions with robust sandwich inference; compare standardized count or fixed-exposure rate contrasts.\",\"title\":\"Count and rate outcome heterogeneity\"}],\"notation\":null,\"one_liner\":\"How does the effect vary across pre-specified groups?\",\"plain_language\":\"The effect within groups you named before you looked.\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"CATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cate\",\"id\":\"CATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"How does the effect vary from person to person, or place to place?\",\"title\":\"Conditional average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"concept.multiplicity\",\"id\":\"concept.multiplicity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"concept\",\"one_liner\":\"Look at enough subgroups and one will look impressive. Pre-specify them, or report that you did not.\",\"title\":\"Subgroups and multiplicity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.did\",\"id\":\"did\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Some places or groups got it earlier than others.\",\"title\":\"Policy rolled out over time\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.binary\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.binary\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Logistic interactions plus separate standardized risk-difference, risk-ratio or marginal odds-ratio heterogeneity tests.\",\"title\":\"Binary outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.categorical\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.categorical\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Multinomial treatment interactions and a joint test of category-probability contrasts; preserves categories without assigning numeric scores.\",\"title\":\"Nominal and ordinal outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.continuous\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.continuous\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"OLS treatment-by-baseline-modifier interactions with HC3 or cluster sandwich inference, direct mean-difference tests and Holm pairwise comparisons.\",\"title\":\"Continuous outcome heterogeneity\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.heterogeneity.count\",\"id\":\"heterogeneity.count\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Poisson conditional-mean interactions with robust sandwich inference; compare standardized count or fixed-exposure rate contrasts.\",\"title\":\"Count and rate outcome heterogeneity\"}],\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\",\"Adjust for background differences\",\"Policy rolled out over time\"],\"title\":\"Group average treatment effect\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"estimand:GATE\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"GATE\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The effect within groups you named before you looked.\",\"title\":\"Group average treatment effect\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3AIDE> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "IDE" ; capy:record "{\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"disposition\":\"unresolved\",\"gaps\":[\"No complete catalogue record exists in this snapshot.\"],\"id\":\"estimand:IDE\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"raw_id\":\"IDE\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Registry marker or referenced item without a maintained catalogue description.\",\"title\":\"IDE\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3AIIE> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "IIE" ; capy:record "{\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"disposition\":\"unresolved\",\"gaps\":[\"No complete catalogue record exists in this snapshot.\"],\"id\":\"estimand:IIE\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"raw_id\":\"IIE\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Registry marker or referenced item without a maintained catalogue description.\",\"title\":\"IIE\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3AITT> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "Intention to treat" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"estimand:ITT\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"acronym\":\"ITT\",\"common_mistake\":\"Treating a diluted ITT as evidence the programme does not work, when take-up was the problem.\",\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"}],\"explain_key\":\"estimand.itt\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"ITT\",\"in_more_depth\":\"It preserves the randomisation exactly, so it is the estimate that needs no extra assumptions. It is also diluted by noncompliance: an effective treatment with 30% take-up shows a small ITT, and that small number is the correct description of the policy as implemented. Reporting both the ITT and the complier effect, and saying which question each answers, is almost always better than choosing one.\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"methods\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cace\",\"id\":\"rct.cace\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Not everyone offered a programme takes it. Report what the offer did, and separately what taking it up did for the people it moved.\",\"title\":\"ITT and CACE (noncompliance)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cluster\",\"id\":\"rct.cluster\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When whole schools, clinics or villages were assigned, the number of clusters is the sample size that matters.\",\"title\":\"Cluster-randomised trial\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.diff_means\",\"id\":\"rct.diff_means\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the two arms as they were randomised, and let covariates sharpen the comparison without letting them change the question.\",\"title\":\"Difference in means\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.randomization_inference\",\"id\":\"rct.randomization_inference\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-run the randomisation thousands of times with the outcomes held fixed, and see how unusual what actually happened really is.\",\"title\":\"Randomisation (Fisher) inference\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.stratified\",\"id\":\"rct.stratified\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Estimate inside the blocks the randomisation was run in, then average them the way your estimand says to.\",\"title\":\"Blocked or stratified experiment\"}],\"notation\":\"E[Y | Z = 1] - E[Y | Z = 0]\",\"one_liner\":\"For everyone who was offered it, what did the offer do?\",\"plain_language\":\"The effect of being offered the programme, whether or not people took it up. Usually the number a policy can deliver.\",\"profile\":\"beginner\",\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"CACE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cace\",\"id\":\"CACE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For the people who comply with what they were offered, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Complier average causal effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rct\",\"id\":\"rct\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"People were assigned by chance, or as good as chance.\",\"title\":\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.randomisation\",\"explains\":\"Assignment really was by chance, and nothing after assignment broke the comparison -- no differential dropout, no crossover you have not modelled.\",\"id\":\"randomisation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Randomisation held\",\"worry_when\":\"Dropout differs across arms; take-up is far from assignment; the randomiser was a person with discretion.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cace\",\"id\":\"rct.cace\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Not everyone offered a programme takes it. Report what the offer did, and separately what taking it up did for the people it moved.\",\"title\":\"ITT and CACE (noncompliance)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cluster\",\"id\":\"rct.cluster\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When whole schools, clinics or villages were assigned, the number of clusters is the sample size that matters.\",\"title\":\"Cluster-randomised trial\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.diff_means\",\"id\":\"rct.diff_means\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Compare the two arms as they were randomised, and let covariates sharpen the comparison without letting them change the question.\",\"title\":\"Difference in means\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.randomization_inference\",\"id\":\"rct.randomization_inference\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-run the randomisation thousands of times with the outcomes held fixed, and see how unusual what actually happened really is.\",\"title\":\"Randomisation (Fisher) inference\"}],\"tags\":[\"Randomised or as-if randomised\"],\"title\":\"Intention to treat\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"estimand:ITT\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"ITT\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The effect of being offered the programme, whether or not people took it up. Usually the number a policy can deliver.\",\"title\":\"Intention to treat\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3ALATE> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "Local average treatment effect" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"estimand:LATE\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"acronym\":\"LATE\",\"common_mistake\":\"Calling it \\\"the\\\" effect. Different instruments identify different compliers and so different numbers.\",\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"explain_key\":\"estimand.late\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"LATE\",\"in_more_depth\":\"In an instrumental-variables design the compliers are the people the instrument moved -- and you cannot identify who they are, only their average effect. In a regression discontinuity the analogous group is units at the threshold. Different instruments move different people and therefore identify different numbers. Two studies with different instruments can both be right and still disagree, which is a feature of the estimand rather than a contradiction.\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"methods\":[{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.mte\",\"id\":\"iv.mte\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Trace the effect as a function of how reluctant a unit is to be treated, instead of reporting one local average.\",\"title\":\"Marginal treatment effects\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.shift_share\",\"id\":\"iv.shift_share\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Combine national shocks with local exposure shares to build an instrument for local changes.\",\"title\":\"Shift-share (Bartik) instruments\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.weak_robust\",\"id\":\"iv.weak_robust\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the set of effects the data cannot reject, which stays honest however weak the instrument is.\",\"title\":\"Weak-instrument-robust confidence set\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rct.cace\",\"id\":\"rct.cace\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Not everyone offered a programme takes it. Report what the offer did, and separately what taking it up did for the people it moved.\",\"title\":\"ITT and CACE (noncompliance)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.fuzzy\",\"id\":\"rd.fuzzy\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Divide the jump in the outcome by the jump in take-up: the effect for the people the cutoff actually moved.\",\"title\":\"Fuzzy RD (crossing the cutoff only nudged people in)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_linear\",\"id\":\"rd.local_linear\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Fit a line either side of the threshold and measure the step between them.\",\"title\":\"Sharp RD at the cutoff\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.local_randomization\",\"id\":\"rd.local_randomization\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Treat a narrow window around the cutoff as a small randomised experiment rather than fitting a curve through it.\",\"title\":\"Local randomisation RD\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.multi_cutoff\",\"id\":\"rd.multi_cutoff\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Several thresholds, or a boundary on a map, pooled into one design.\",\"title\":\"Multi-cutoff and geographic RD\"}],\"notation\":\"E[Y(1) - Y(0) | complier]\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who only got it because of the instrument or the cutoff, what did it do?\",\"plain_language\":\"The effect for the units whose treatment status was actually changed by the instrument, or who sit at the cutoff. Not for everyone.\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"references\":[\"Imbens & Angrist (1994)\",\"Mogstad & Torgovitsky (2018)\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.monotonicity\",\"explains\":\"The instrument pushes everyone the same way, or not at all. Nobody takes the treatment only because they were discouraged from it.\",\"id\":\"monotonicity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Monotonicity / no defiers\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is an incentive that could plausibly backfire for some people, or it is a judge or examiner whose leniency ordering differs across case types.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exclusion\",\"explains\":\"The instrument affects the outcome only through the treatment. On the board, that is the arrow you did not draw.\",\"id\":\"exclusion\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exclusion restriction\",\"worry_when\":\"The instrument is a policy that changed several things at once; it is correlated with geography, time or institutions that have their own effects; it was chosen because it predicted the outcome.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.2sls\",\"id\":\"iv.2sls\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Use only the part of the treatment that the instrument moved, and see what happened to the outcome.\",\"title\":\"Two-stage least squares\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.gmm\",\"id\":\"iv.gmm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Weight the instruments by how informative each one actually is, using a weight matrix estimated from the data.\",\"title\":\"Two-step GMM\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.liml\",\"id\":\"iv.liml\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"The maximum-likelihood cousin of two-stage least squares, which stays much better behaved when the instruments are weak.\",\"title\":\"LIML (and Fuller)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.iv.mte\",\"id\":\"iv.mte\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Trace the effect as a function of how reluctant a unit is to be treated, instead of reporting one local average.\",\"title\":\"Marginal treatment effects\"}],\"tags\":[\"Something else pushed people in\",\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"],\"title\":\"Local average treatment effect\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"estimand:LATE\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"LATE\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The effect for the units whose treatment status was actually changed by the instrument, or who sit at the cutoff. Not for everyone.\",\"title\":\"Local average treatment effect\"}" .
<urn:capy:estimand%3ALATET> a capy:estimand ; skos:prefLabel "Local average treatment effect on the treated" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[],\"catalogue_key\":\"estimand:LATET\",\"citation_ids\":[],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"acronym\":\"LATET\",\"common_mistake\":null,\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"explain_key\":\"estimand.latet\",\"gaps\":[\"no note on the mistake people make with it\"],\"id\":\"LATET\",\"in_more_depth\":\"Relevant when the instrument encourages rather than assigns, and you want the effect among treated compliers rather than all compliers. It coincides with the LATE under one-sided noncompliance, where nobody takes the treatment without the encouragement.\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"methods\":[],\"notation\":null,\"one_liner\":\"For treated compliers, what did it do?\",\"plain_language\":\"The complier effect restricted to those who actually took the treatment.\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"references\":[],\"see_also\":[{\"acronym\":\"LATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.late\",\"id\":\"LATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who only got it because of the instrument or the cutoff, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Local average treatment effect\"},{\"acronym\":\"CACE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.cace\",\"id\":\"CACE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For the people who comply with what they were offered, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Complier average causal effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"design.iv\",\"id\":\"iv\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"An instrument or encouragement changed treatment, not the outcome directly.\",\"title\":\"Something else pushed people in\"}],\"tags\":[\"Something else pushed people in\"],\"title\":\"Local average treatment effect on the treated\"},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[\"no note on the mistake people make with it\"],\"id\":\"estimand:LATET\",\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"LATET\",\"relations\":[],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"The complier effect restricted to those who actually took the treatment.\",\"title\":\"Local average treatment effect on the treated\"}" .
