ported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"scope\":\"Editorial methodological context for this registry entry; not a reviewed implementation or equivalence assertion.\",\"subject_id\":\"obs.superlearner\"},{\"assertion_mode\":\"editorial\",\"conditions\":[],\"counterevidence\":[],\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"relation:fc4fbed65711f15feb43c464\",\"object_id\":\"assumption:sutva\",\"predicate\":\"requires_assumption\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"scope\":\"Declared project registry/navigation relationship; not an identified causal effect.\",\"subject_id\":\"obs.superlearner\"}],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Combine several candidate learners by cross-validated weights instead of betting on one.\",\"title\":\"Super Learner nuisance models\"}" .
<urn:capy:obs.tmle> a capy:method ; skos:prefLabel "Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[\"method:obs.tmle\"],\"catalogue_key\":\"method:obs.tmle\",\"citation_ids\":[\"10.1093/aje/kww165\",\"10.2202/1557-4679.1043\",\"10.2202/1557-4679.1260\"],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"availability\":{\"coming\":null,\"engines\":{\"python\":true,\"r\":\"tmle\"},\"needs_r_packages\":[\"tmle\"],\"runs_here\":true,\"summary\":\"Runs on this computer with what the app already installs.\"},\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"}],\"diagnostics\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.nuisance_rmse\",\"id\":\"nuisance_rmse\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Nuisance models by fold\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.propensity_clipping\",\"id\":\"propensity_clipping\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Propensity clipping\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cate_distribution\",\"id\":\"cate_distribution\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"CATE distribution\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.cate_calibration\",\"id\":\"cate_calibration\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"CATE calibration\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rate\",\"id\":\"rate\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Rank-weighted average treatment effect\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.fold_stability\",\"id\":\"fold_stability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Stability across folds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.seed_stability\",\"id\":\"seed_stability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Stability across seeds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.tmle_targeting\",\"id\":\"tmle_targeting\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Targeting step\"}],\"estimands\":[{\"acronym\":\"ATE\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.ate\",\"id\":\"ATE\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"If everyone got the programme, how would the average outcome change?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect\"},{\"acronym\":\"ATT\",\"explain_key\":\"estimand.att\",\"id\":\"ATT\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"estimand\",\"one_liner\":\"For people who actually got it, what did it do?\",\"title\":\"Average treatment effect on the treated\"}],\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"gaps\":[\"no plain-language description has been written yet, so the one-line summary is standing in for it\"],\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"in_more_depth\":null,\"kind\":\"method\",\"needs_overlap\":true,\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"options\":[{\"choices\":[\"linear\",\"lasso\",\"forest\",\"gradient_boosting\"],\"default\":\"linear\",\"help\":\"The model used for E[Y|X] and E[D|X]. Boring is usually right: a regularised linear model is hard to beat when the sample is small, and a flexible learner cannot fix a confounder you did not measure.\",\"label\":\"Nuisance learner\",\"name\":\"learner\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"default\":5,\"help\":\"Each fold is scored by models that never saw it. More folds use the data better and cost more time; below 5 the estimate starts to depend on the split.\",\"label\":\"Cross-fitting folds\",\"max\":20,\"min\":2,\"name\":\"folds\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"default\":0.01,\"help\":\"Keeps the weights finite when a unit is nearly certain to be treated. Every clipped unit is reported, because clipping quietly changes the population.\",\"label\":\"Clip propensity scores at\",\"max\":0.45,\"min\":0.0,\"name\":\"clip\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"number\"},{\"default\":0.95,\"help\":\"The interval width. Changing it after seeing the result is not a neutral act.\",\"label\":\"Confidence level\",\"max\":0.999,\"min\":0.5,\"name\":\"ci_level\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"number\"},{\"default\":1,\"help\":\"Reruns the whole cross-fitting under fresh random splits. The split is an arbitrary choice; the answer should not depend on it.\",\"label\":\"Repeat under new fold splits\",\"max\":10,\"min\":1,\"name\":\"stability_reps\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"default\":200,\"help\":\"Draws used for the standard error of the targeting curve. 0 skips it.\",\"label\":\"Bootstrap draws for the RATE curve\",\"max\":2000,\"min\":0,\"name\":\"rate_reps\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"default\":[],\"help\":\"Groups you named BEFORE looking. Each one gets the same doubly robust score as the headline, and the multiplicity caution comes with them.\",\"label\":\"Pre-registered subgroups\",\"name\":\"subgroups\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"columns\"},{\"choices\":[\"ATE\",\"ATT\"],\"default\":\"ATE\",\"help\":\"ATE is everyone; ATT is the units that actually got treated.\",\"label\":\"Population\",\"name\":\"estimand\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"default\":0.0,\"help\":\"Continuous outcomes are squeezed into [0, 1] using the observed range before the targeting step. Padding widens that range if you expect values outside the sample's own minimum and maximum.\",\"label\":\"Padding on the outcome bounds\",\"max\":0.25,\"min\":0.0,\"name\":\"bound_padding\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"number\"}],\"plain_language\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"probes\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"id\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"How strong would an unmeasured confounder have to be -- compared with the covariates you already have -- to explain the estimate away?\",\"requested_as\":\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"title\":\"Cinelli-Hazlett sensitivity (partial R2)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.rosenbaum\",\"id\":\"probe.rosenbaum\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"How unequal could the odds of treatment be inside a matched pair before the conclusion stopped being clear?\",\"requested_as\":\"probe.rosenbaum\",\"title\":\"Rosenbaum bounds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"id\":\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the same analysis on something the treatment could not have changed. It should find nothing.\",\"requested_as\":\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"title\":\"Placebo outcome\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.subset\",\"id\":\"probe.subset\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-estimate on random subsets. The answer should move about as much as losing that much data costs, and no more.\",\"requested_as\":\"probe.subset\",\"title\":\"Subset stability\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.random_common_cause\",\"id\":\"probe.random_common_cause\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Add a variable that causes nothing to the adjustment set. The estimate should barely move.\",\"requested_as\":\"probe.random_common_cause\",\"title\":\"Random common cause\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.trim_curve\",\"id\":\"probe.trim_curve\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-estimate across a grid of propensity-score trimming thresholds instead of picking one and hoping.\",\"requested_as\":\"probe.trim_curve\",\"title\":\"Trimming curve\"}],\"references\":[\"van der Laan & Rubin (2006), Targeted maximum likelihood learning\",\"Gruber & van der Laan (2010), A targeted maximum likelihood estimator of a causal effect on a bounded continuous outcome\",\"Schuler & Rose (2017), Targeted maximum likelihood estimation for causal inference in observational studies\"],\"roles_forbidden\":[\"forbidden\"],\"roles_optional\":[\"effect_modifiers\",\"a clustering variable\"],\"roles_required\":[\"a treatment\",\"an outcome\",\"at least one measured confounder\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.observational\",\"id\":\"observational\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Treatment was not random; we can measure the things that made people get it.\",\"title\":\"Adjust for background differences\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.bounds.manski\",\"id\":\"bounds.manski\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Report the range the effect must lie in given only what the data can support, without assuming away the missing counterfactual.\",\"title\":\"Manski bounds\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.interference.exposure\",\"id\":\"interference.exposure\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"When one unit's treatment affects another's outcome, estimate the direct and spillover effects separately instead of pretending neither exists.\",\"title\":\"Interference and spillovers\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.causal_forest\",\"id\":\"ml.causal_forest\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Grow trees that look for units whose effect differs, keeping the rows that chose each split away from the rows that estimate it.\",\"title\":\"Honest causal forest\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_irm\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_irm\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"A separate outcome model for each arm plus a treatment model, combined in a doubly robust score: right if either side is right.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (interactive)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.dml_plr\",\"id\":\"ml.dml_plr\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Predict the outcome and the treatment from the confounders, then regress what is left of one on what is left of the other.\",\"title\":\"Double ML (partially linear)\"},{\"explain_key\":\"method.ml.metalearner\",\"id\":\"ml.metalearner\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"method\",\"one_liner\":\"Build the per-unit effect out of ordinary prediction models, four different ways, on the same cross-fitting.\",\"title\":\"Meta-learner (T, S, X, DR)\"}],\"status\":\"recommended\",\"status_label\":\"Recommended\",\"tags\":[\"Adjust for background differences\",\"ATE\",\"ATT\",\"Recommended\"],\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\",\"what_it_assumes\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.exchangeability\",\"explains\":\"Treated and untreated units are comparable once you condition on the confounders you measured. Everything that pushed people towards treatment and also moves the outcome is in your adjustment set.\",\"id\":\"exchangeability\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Exchangeability / no unmeasured confounding\",\"worry_when\":\"Treatment was chosen by people who knew something about the likely outcome that you do not observe. Clinical judgement, self-selection into a programme, and administrative triage are the classic cases.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.positivity\",\"explains\":\"Every kind of unit had some chance of being treated and some chance of not being treated. Where that fails, there is no comparison to make and the model is inventing one.\",\"id\":\"positivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Positivity / overlap\",\"worry_when\":\"Any single weight carries a large share of the total; the effective sample is far below the nominal one; the propensity model separates the arms almost perfectly; treated units sit outside the control group's range.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.sutva\",\"explains\":\"One unit's treatment does not change another unit's outcome, and \\\"treated\\\" means the same thing for everyone who got it.\",\"id\":\"sutva\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"SUTVA / no interference\",\"worry_when\":\"Units interact: they compete for the same jobs, infect each other, share a classroom or a market. Or the \\\"programme\\\" is really a family of programmes whose intensity varied a lot across sites.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.consistency\",\"explains\":\"The outcome you observe under the treatment a unit received is the same as the potential outcome under that treatment. In practice: the treatment has to be a thing someone could actually do.\",\"id\":\"consistency\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Consistency / well-defined intervention\",\"worry_when\":\"The treatment is a state of the world rather than an action, or it is coded from several very different real interventions.\"}],\"when_not_to_use\":[\"The targeting step is driven by inverse propensity weights, so extreme propensities move the estimate a long way and the interval with it. The run reports how far the targeting moved the number and whether the score equation was actually solved. Machine learning does not identify anything. Cross-fitting removes the bias that comes from fitting flexible models on the same rows you estimate with; it does nothing about a confounder you never measured.\",\"Overlap is so poor that most units sit at the propensity clip; then the fluctuation is fitting the boundary, not the data.\"],\"when_to_use\":[\"Doubly robust like AIPW, but the estimate is a prediction from a model, so it stays inside the bounds of the outcome -- a weighted estimator can return a risk difference no data could produce.\"]},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[\"no plain-language description has been written yet, so the one-line summary is standing in for it\"],\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"kind\":\"method\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"registry\":{\"designs\":[\"observational\"],\"diagnostics\":[\"nuisance_rmse\",\"propensity_clipping\",\"cate_distribution\",\"cate_calibration\",\"rate\",\"fold_stability\",\"seed_stability\",\"tmle_targeting\"],\"disrecommend_when\":\"Overlap is so poor that most units sit at the propensity clip; then the fluctuation is fitting the boundary, not the data.\",\"engine_support_basis\":\"Static adapter decorator and declared package metadata; installed availability not checked.\",\"engines\":{\"python\":true,\"r\":\"tmle\"},\"estimands\":[\"ATE\",\"ATT\"],\"explain_key\":\"method.obs.tmle\",\"id\":\"obs.tmle\",\"needs\":[\"numpy\",\"scipy\",\"pandas\",\"scikit-learn\"],\"needs_overlap\":true,\"one_liner\":\"Fit the outcome model, then nudge it once in the direction the treatment model says it is wrong.\",\"options\":[{\"choices\":[\"linear\",\"lasso\",\"forest\",\"gradient_boosting\"],\"default\":\"linear\",\"help\":\"The model used for E[Y|X] and E[D|X]. Boring is usually right: a regularised linear model is hard to beat when the sample is small, and a flexible learner cannot fix a confounder you did not measure.\",\"label\":\"Nuisance learner\",\"name\":\"learner\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"default\":5,\"help\":\"Each fold is scored by models that never saw it. More folds use the data better and cost more time; below 5 the estimate starts to depend on the split.\",\"label\":\"Cross-fitting folds\",\"max\":20,\"min\":2,\"name\":\"folds\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"default\":0.01,\"help\":\"Keeps the weights finite when a unit is nearly certain to be treated. Every clipped unit is reported, because clipping quietly changes the population.\",\"label\":\"Clip propensity scores at\",\"max\":0.45,\"min\":0.0,\"name\":\"clip\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"number\"},{\"default\":0.95,\"help\":\"The interval width. Changing it after seeing the result is not a neutral act.\",\"label\":\"Confidence level\",\"max\":0.999,\"min\":0.5,\"name\":\"ci_level\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"number\"},{\"default\":1,\"help\":\"Reruns the whole cross-fitting under fresh random splits. The split is an arbitrary choice; the answer should not depend on it.\",\"label\":\"Repeat under new fold splits\",\"max\":10,\"min\":1,\"name\":\"stability_reps\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"default\":200,\"help\":\"Draws used for the standard error of the targeting curve. 0 skips it.\",\"label\":\"Bootstrap draws for the RATE curve\",\"max\":2000,\"min\":0,\"name\":\"rate_reps\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"default\":[],\"help\":\"Groups you named BEFORE looking. Each one gets the same doubly robust score as the headline, and the multiplicity caution comes with them.\",\"label\":\"Pre-registered subgroups\",\"name\":\"subgroups\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"columns\"},{\"choices\":[\"ATE\",\"ATT\"],\"default\":\"ATE\",\"help\":\"ATE is everyone; ATT is the units that actually got treated.\",\"label\":\"Population\",\"name\":\"estimand\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"default\":0.0,\"help\":\"Continuous outcomes are squeezed into [0, 1] using the observed range before the targeting step. Padding widens that range if you expect values outside the sample's own minimum and maximum.\",\"label\":\"Padding on the outcome bounds\",\"max\":0.25,\"min\":0.0,\"name\":\"bound_padding\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"number\"}],\"probes\":[\"probe.cinelli_hazlett\",\"probe.rosenbaum\",\"probe.placebo_outcome\",\"probe.subset\",\"probe.random_common_cause\",\"probe.trim_curve\"],\"reference_dispositions\":[{\"disposition\":\"resolved_metadata\",\"id\":\"10.2202/1557-4679.1043\",\"requested_as\":\"van der Laan & Rubin (2006), Targeted maximum likelihood learning\"},{\"disposition\":\"resolved_metadata\",\"id\":\"10.2202/1557-4679.1260\",\"requested_as\":\"Gruber & van der Laan (2010), A targeted maximum likelihood estimator of a causal effect on a bounded continuous outcome\"},{\"disposition\":\"resolved_metadata\",\"id\":\"10.1093/aje/kww165\",\"requested_as\":\"Schuler & Rose (2017), Targeted maximum likelihood estimation for causal inference in observational studies\"}],\"reference_ids\":[\"10.2202/1557-4679.1043\",\"10.2202/1557-4679.1260\",\"10.1093/aje/kww165\"],\"references\":[\"van der Laan & Rubin (2006), Targeted maximum likelihood learning\",\"Gruber & van der Laan (2010), A targeted maximum likelihood estimator of a causal effect on a bounded continuous outcome\",\"Schuler & Rose (2017), Targeted maximum likelihood estimation for causal inference in observational studies\"],\"roles_forbidden\":[\"forbidden\"],\"roles_optional\":[\"effect_modifiers\",\"cluster\"],\"roles_required\":[\"treatment\",\"outcome\",\"confounders\"],\"source_path\":\"engines/python/capy_py/causalml.py\",\"source_sha256\":\"1c60218ff563078a18a811cc23ca51cddaad89bb27de76b552224a35be8473ca\",\"status\":\"recommended\",\"title\":\"Targeted maximum likelihood (TMLE)\",\"what_can_go_wrong\":\"The targeting step is driven by inverse propensity weights, so extreme propensities move the estimate a long way and the interval with it. The run reports how far the targeting moved the number and whether the score equation was actually solved. Machine learning does not identify anything. Cross-fitting removes the bias that comes from fitting flexible models on the same rows you estimate with; it does nothing about a confounder you never measured.\",\"why_recommended\":\"Doubly robust like AIPW, but the estimate is a prediction from a model, so it stays inside the bounds of the outcome -- a weighted estimator can return a risk difference no data could produce.\"},\"relations\":[{\"assertion_mode\":\"editorial\",\"conditions\":[\"Consult the method card and exact claim qualifications before treating conceptual coverage as implementation support.\"],\"counterevidence\":[],\"gaps\":[\"No independent human scientific review or study-specific causal identification is established.\"],\"id\":\"relation:090a2f7367329a0ad473f714\",\"object_id\":\"concept:direct-heterogeneity-test\",\"predicate\":\"related_to\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-ontology\",\"source:ontology:altman2003\",\"source:ontology:statsmodels-wald\"],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"basis\":[\"Original methodological explanation checked against linked primary sources; no independent human review or study audit.\"],\"date\":\"2026-10-14\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"agent_editorial_context\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"scope\":\"Qualified methodological context for this declared method; this link does not assert an implemented test or identifying assumption.\",\"subject_id\":\"obs.tmle\"},{\"assertion_mode\":\"editorial\",\"conditions\":[\"Interpret the relation within the stated design, population and observation process.\"],\"counterevidence\":[],\"gaps\":[\"This editorial link does not certify semantic entailment or implementation correctness.\"],\"id\":\"relation:0f3eca7484d2f653a0e17e1e\",\"object_id\":\"concept:tmle\",\"predicate\":\"related_to\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-ontology\",\"source:ontology:vanderlaan2006\"],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"basis\":[\"Original explanation and primary reference context; not a completed implementation audit.\"],\"date\":\"2026-10-13\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"agent_editorial_context\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apa