ntext for this registry entry; not a reviewed implementation or equivalence assertion.\",\"subject_id\":\"rct.stratified\"},{\"assertion_mode\":\"editorial\",\"conditions\":[],\"counterevidence\":[],\"gaps\":[],\"id\":\"relation:fc875a978c5882f622155a09\",\"object_id\":\"assumption:consistency\",\"predicate\":\"requires_assumption\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"scope\":\"Declared project registry/navigation relationship; not an identified causal effect.\",\"subject_id\":\"rct.stratified\"}],\"review\":{\"actor\":\"Codex agent\",\"actor_type\":\"agent\",\"date\":\"2026-10-12\",\"human_review\":false,\"semantic\":\"not_assessed\",\"structural\":\"validated\"},\"rights\":{\"attribution\":\"Causal Capybara contributors; Yusufhan Balci; VCESR\",\"content_type\":\"original_project_content\",\"license_id\":\"Apache-2.0\",\"license_url\":\"https://www.apache.org/licenses/LICENSE-2.0\",\"modified\":true,\"permissions\":{\"adapt\":true,\"cache\":true,\"display\":true,\"model_context\":true,\"public_export\":true},\"review_basis\":\"Original or imported Apache-2.0 project material; no third-party paper body included.\"},\"summary\":\"Estimate inside the blocks the randomisation was run in, then average them the way your estimand says to.\",\"title\":\"Blocked or stratified experiment\"}" .
<urn:capy:rd.covariate_balance> a capy:method ; skos:prefLabel "Do the covariates jump at the cutoff too?" ; prov:wasDerivedFrom <urn:capy:source%3Aproject-inventory> ; capy:record "{\"aliases\":[\"method:rd.covariate_balance\"],\"catalogue_key\":\"method:rd.covariate_balance\",\"citation_ids\":[\"10.1016/j.jeconom.2007.05.001\",\"10.1162/rest_a_00760\",\"calonico2014\",\"source:unresolved:179f2828781ef26e5dd8\",\"source:unresolved:afa378e198697172e867\"],\"citations\":[],\"content\":{\"availability\":{\"coming\":null,\"engines\":{\"python\":true,\"r\":\"rdrobust\"},\"needs_r_packages\":[\"rdrobust\"],\"runs_here\":true,\"summary\":\"Runs on this computer with what the app already installs.\"},\"designs\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"diagnostics\":[{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.covariate_balance\",\"id\":\"covariate_balance\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Covariate balance\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.rd_plot\",\"id\":\"rd_plot\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Binned scatter at the cutoff\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"id\":\"bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Bandwidth sensitivity path\"},{\"explain_key\":\"diagnostic.running_variable_discreteness\",\"id\":\"running_variable_discreteness\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"diagnostic\",\"title\":\"Discreteness of the running variable\"}],\"estimands\":[],\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.covariate_balance\",\"gaps\":[\"no plain-language description has been written yet, so the one-line summary is standing in for it\",\"does not say which effect it estimates\"],\"id\":\"rd.covariate_balance\",\"in_more_depth\":null,\"kind\":\"method\",\"needs_overlap\":false,\"one_liner\":\"Run the same estimator on variables the programme cannot have changed; they should not step at the threshold.\",\"options\":[{\"default\":1,\"help\":\"The same order you used for the outcome, so the check matches the estimate.\",\"label\":\"Polynomial order either side\",\"max\":4,\"min\":1,\"name\":\"polynomial_order\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"choices\":[\"mse\",\"ik\"],\"default\":\"mse\",\"help\":\"'mse' is capy's own finite-sample plug-in rule: it measures the bias and variance of this estimator on this sample. 'ik' is the Imbens-Kalyanaraman (2012) formula, for a sharp local linear fit only. Neither is rdrobust's CCT selector.\",\"label\":\"How to choose the window\",\"name\":\"bandwidth_selector\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"default\":null,\"help\":\"The half-width of the window either side of the cutoff, in the units of the running variable. Setting it by hand overrides the selector; the sensitivity path shows you what every other choice would have given.\",\"label\":\"Bandwidth (leave empty to choose one)\",\"min\":0.0,\"name\":\"bandwidth\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"number\"},{\"default\":0.0,\"help\":\"Drop units within this distance of the cutoff, where heaping and manipulation live. Every dropped row appears in the sample flow.\",\"label\":\"Donut hole radius\",\"min\":0.0,\"name\":\"donut\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"number\"},{\"choices\":[\"triangular\",\"epanechnikov\",\"uniform\"],\"default\":\"triangular\",\"help\":\"Triangular gives the most weight to units closest to the cutoff, which is what the MSE-optimal theory assumes. Uniform is the plain 'everyone inside the window counts equally' choice.\",\"label\":\"Weighting near the cutoff\",\"name\":\"kernel\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"choices\":[\"HC0\",\"HC1\",\"HC2\",\"HC3\"],\"default\":\"HC1\",\"help\":\"Heteroskedasticity-robust. HC3 is more conservative in small windows. Set the cluster role as well and the variance becomes cluster-robust.\",\"label\":\"Standard errors\",\"name\":\"vce\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"default\":0.95,\"help\":\"0.95 unless you have a reason.\",\"label\":\"Confidence level\",\"max\":0.999,\"min\":0.5,\"name\":\"ci_level\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"number\"},{\"default\":0,\"help\":\"0 picks about 2 n^(1/3) bins a side.\",\"label\":\"Bins per side in the plot\",\"max\":100,\"min\":0,\"name\":\"plot_bins\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"choices\":[\"quantile\",\"evenly\"],\"default\":\"quantile\",\"help\":\"Quantile bins hold the same number of units each; even bins hold the same width each.\",\"label\":\"Bin spacing\",\"name\":\"plot_bin_spacing\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"select\"}],\"plain_language\":\"Run the same estimator on variables the programme cannot have changed; they should not step at the threshold.\",\"probes\":[{\"explain_key\":\"probe.subset\",\"id\":\"probe.subset\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Re-estimate on random subsets. The answer should move about as much as losing that much data costs, and no more.\",\"requested_as\":\"probe.subset\",\"title\":\"Subset stability\"},{\"explain_key\":\"probe.alternate_spec\",\"id\":\"probe.alternate_spec\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"probe\",\"one_liner\":\"Run the grid of defensible specifications and sort the answers. The curve is the result, not the one you happened to run.\",\"requested_as\":\"probe.alternate_spec\",\"title\":\"Specification curve\"}],\"references\":[\"Imbens & Lemieux (2008), Regression discontinuity designs: a guide to practice\",\"Calonico, Cattaneo & Titiunik (2014), Robust nonparametric confidence intervals for regression-discontinuity designs\",\"Imbens & Kalyanaraman (2012), Optimal bandwidth choice for the regression discontinuity estimator\",\"Cattaneo, Idrobo & Titiunik (2020), A practical introduction to regression discontinuity designs\",\"Calonico, Cattaneo, Farrell & Titiunik (2019), Regression discontinuity designs using covariates\"],\"roles_forbidden\":[\"an instrument\",\"forbidden\"],\"roles_optional\":[\"a clustering variable\",\"a treatment\"],\"roles_required\":[\"a running variable\",\"a cutoff\",\"an outcome\",\"at least one measured confounder\"],\"see_also\":[{\"explain_key\":\"design.rd\",\"id\":\"rd\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"design\",\"one_liner\":\"Crossing a score, age, or border switched the programme on.\",\"title\":\"A cutoff decided eligibility\"}],\"status\":\"recommended\",\"status_label\":\"Recommended\",\"tags\":[\"A cutoff decided eligibility\",\"Recommended\"],\"title\":\"Do the covariates jump at the cutoff too?\",\"what_it_assumes\":[{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.no_manipulation\",\"explains\":\"People could not precisely control which side of the cutoff they landed on.\",\"id\":\"no_manipulation\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"No manipulation of the running variable\",\"worry_when\":\"A visible spike or gap in the density at the cutoff; covariates that jump; a cutoff that people knew about and had time to respond to.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.continuity\",\"explains\":\"Everything else that affects the outcome changes smoothly through the cutoff, so the only thing that jumps is the treatment.\",\"id\":\"continuity\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Continuity of potential outcomes at the cutoff\",\"worry_when\":\"Several rules share the threshold; the running variable is coarse, so \\\"at the cutoff\\\" is really a wide band; the estimate depends heavily on the bandwidth.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.sutva\",\"explains\":\"One unit's treatment does not change another unit's outcome, and \\\"treated\\\" means the same thing for everyone who got it.\",\"id\":\"sutva\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"SUTVA / no interference\",\"worry_when\":\"Units interact: they compete for the same jobs, infect each other, share a classroom or a market. Or the \\\"programme\\\" is really a family of programmes whose intensity varied a lot across sites.\"},{\"explain_key\":\"assumption.consistency\",\"explains\":\"The outcome you observe under the treatment a unit received is the same as the potential outcome under that treatment. In practice: the treatment has to be a thing someone could actually do.\",\"id\":\"consistency\",\"in_catalogue\":true,\"kind\":\"assumption\",\"title\":\"Consistency / well-defined intervention\",\"worry_when\":\"The treatment is a state of the world rather than an action, or it is coded from several very different real interventions.\"}],\"when_not_to_use\":[\"Passing this check does not make the design valid: it only speaks to the variables you happened to measure. With many covariates one will cross the 5% line by luck, which is why the joint test is reported alongside the individual ones.\"],\"when_to_use\":[\"It is the RD version of a balance table, and it uses exactly the estimator that produced the headline number, at exactly the same bandwidth. A covariate that jumps is the clearest possible evidence that something other than eligibility changes at the threshold.\"]},\"disposition\":\"imported\",\"gaps\":[\"no plain-language description has been written yet, so the one-line summary is standing in for it\",\"does not say which effect it estimates\"],\"id\":\"rd.covariate_balance\",\"kind\":\"method\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-inventory\"],\"raw_id\":\"rd.covariate_balance\",\"registry\":{\"designs\":[\"rd\"],\"diagnostics\":[\"covariate_balance\",\"rd_plot\",\"bandwidth_sensitivity\",\"running_variable_discreteness\"],\"disrecommend_when\":null,\"engine_support_basis\":\"Static adapter decorator and declared package metadata; installed availability not checked.\",\"engines\":{\"python\":true,\"r\":\"rdrobust\"},\"estimands\":[],\"explain_key\":\"method.rd.covariate_balance\",\"id\":\"rd.covariate_balance\",\"needs\":[\"numpy\",\"scipy\",\"pandas\"],\"needs_overlap\":false,\"one_liner\":\"Run the same estimator on variables the programme cannot have changed; they should not step at the threshold.\",\"options\":[{\"default\":1,\"help\":\"The same order you used for the outcome, so the check matches the estimate.\",\"label\":\"Polynomial order either side\",\"max\":4,\"min\":1,\"name\":\"polynomial_order\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"choices\":[\"mse\",\"ik\"],\"default\":\"mse\",\"help\":\"'mse' is capy's own finite-sample plug-in rule: it measures the bias and variance of this estimator on this sample. 'ik' is the Imbens-Kalyanaraman (2012) formula, for a sharp local linear fit only. Neither is rdrobust's CCT selector.\",\"label\":\"How to choose the window\",\"name\":\"bandwidth_selector\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"default\":null,\"help\":\"The half-width of the window either side of the cutoff, in the units of the running variable. Setting it by hand overrides the selector; the sensitivity path shows you what every other choice would have given.\",\"label\":\"Bandwidth (leave empty to choose one)\",\"min\":0.0,\"name\":\"bandwidth\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"number\"},{\"default\":0.0,\"help\":\"Drop units within this distance of the cutoff, where heaping and manipulation live. Every dropped row appears in the sample flow.\",\"label\":\"Donut hole radius\",\"min\":0.0,\"name\":\"donut\",\"profile\":\"standard\",\"type\":\"number\"},{\"choices\":[\"triangular\",\"epanechnikov\",\"uniform\"],\"default\":\"triangular\",\"help\":\"Triangular gives the most weight to units closest to the cutoff, which is what the MSE-optimal theory assumes. Uniform is the plain 'everyone inside the window counts equally' choice.\",\"label\":\"Weighting near the cutoff\",\"name\":\"kernel\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"choices\":[\"HC0\",\"HC1\",\"HC2\",\"HC3\"],\"default\":\"HC1\",\"help\":\"Heteroskedasticity-robust. HC3 is more conservative in small windows. Set the cluster role as well and the variance becomes cluster-robust.\",\"label\":\"Standard errors\",\"name\":\"vce\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"select\"},{\"default\":0.95,\"help\":\"0.95 unless you have a reason.\",\"label\":\"Confidence level\",\"max\":0.999,\"min\":0.5,\"name\":\"ci_level\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"number\"},{\"default\":0,\"help\":\"0 picks about 2 n^(1/3) bins a side.\",\"label\":\"Bins per side in the plot\",\"max\":100,\"min\":0,\"name\":\"plot_bins\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"int\"},{\"choices\":[\"quantile\",\"evenly\"],\"default\":\"quantile\",\"help\":\"Quantile bins hold the same number of units each; even bins hold the same width each.\",\"label\":\"Bin spacing\",\"name\":\"plot_bin_spacing\",\"profile\":\"advanced\",\"type\":\"select\"}],\"probes\":[\"probe.subset\",\"probe.alternate_spec\"],\"reference_dispositions\":[{\"disposition\":\"resolved_metadata\",\"id\":\"10.1016/j.jeconom.2007.05.001\",\"requested_as\":\"Imbens & Lemieux (2008), Regression discontinuity designs: a guide to practice\"},{\"disposition\":\"resolved_metadata\",\"id\":\"calonico2014\",\"requested_as\":\"Calonico, Cattaneo & Titiunik (2014), Robust nonparametric confidence intervals for regression-discontinuity designs\"},{\"disposition\":\"unresolved_search_only\",\"id\":\"source:unresolved:179f2828781ef26e5dd8\",\"requested_as\":\"Imbens & Kalyanaraman (2012), Optimal bandwidth choice for the regression discontinuity estimator\"},{\"disposition\":\"unresolved_search_only\",\"id\":\"source:unresolved:afa378e198697172e867\",\"requested_as\":\"Cattaneo, Idrobo & Titiunik (2020), A practical introduction to regression discontinuity designs\"},{\"disposition\":\"resolved_metadata\",\"id\":\"10.1162/rest_a_00760\",\"requested_as\":\"Calonico, Cattaneo, Farrell & Titiunik (2019), Regression discontinuity designs using covariates\"}],\"reference_ids\":[\"10.1016/j.jeconom.2007.05.001\",\"calonico2014\",\"source:unresolved:179f2828781ef26e5dd8\",\"source:unresolved:afa378e198697172e867\",\"10.1162/rest_a_00760\"],\"references\":[\"Imbens & Lemieux (2008), Regression discontinuity designs: a guide to practice\",\"Calonico, Cattaneo & Titiunik (2014), Robust nonparametric confidence intervals for regression-discontinuity designs\",\"Imbens & Kalyanaraman (2012), Optimal bandwidth choice for the regression discontinuity estimator\",\"Cattaneo, Idrobo & Titiunik (2020), A practical introduction to regression discontinuity designs\",\"Calonico, Cattaneo, Farrell & Titiunik (2019), Regression discontinuity designs using covariates\"],\"roles_forbidden\":[\"instruments\",\"forbidden\"],\"roles_optional\":[\"cluster\",\"treatment\"],\"roles_required\":[\"running\",\"cutoff\",\"outcome\",\"confounders\"],\"source_path\":\"engines/python/capy_py/rd.py\",\"source_sha256\":\"a86f14c44b23637b2b7378f638cdbfba5ad3bdcb0ed99ee9af783e9f071548bf\",\"status\":\"recommended\",\"title\":\"Do the covariates jump at the cutoff too?\",\"what_can_go_wrong\":\"Passing this check does not make the design valid: it only speaks to the variables you happened to measure. With many covariates one will cross the 5% line by luck, which is why the joint test is reported alongside the individual ones.\",\"why_recommended\":\"It is the RD version of a balance table, and it uses exactly the estimator that produced the headline number, at exactly the same bandwidth. A covariate that jumps is the clearest possible evidence that something other than eligibility changes at the threshold.\"},\"relations\":[{\"assertion_mode\":\"editorial\",\"conditions\":[\"Interpret the relation within the stated design, population and observation process.\"],\"counterevidence\":[],\"gaps\":[\"This editorial link does not certify semantic entailment or implementation correctness.\"],\"id\":\"relation:100f12ed266f6cdb03bcd1fd\",\"object_id\":\"concept:causal-framing\",\"predicate\":\"related_to\",\"provenance\":[\"source:project-ontology\",\"source:ontology:pearl2009\"],\"review\":{\"act